15. Daily hassles (HS)-[POSITIVE]->Body mass index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Daily hassles (HS) do not directly cause body mass index (BMI) to change. Any effect of daily hassles on BMI is likely mediated through variables such as perceived stress, depressive symptoms, sleep disturbance, or physical inactivity, all of which are present in the CLD. Therefore, a direct causal link between daily hassles and BMI should not be included.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9362746/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/overweight-and-obesity/causes', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/11209-weight-control-and-obesity'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9362746/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited narrative review presents evidence that chronic/social stress can promote weight gain through behavioral (comfort eating, reduced activity) and physiological (HPA-axis activation, cortisol, ghrelin, NPY, insulin resistance) pathways and cites studies linking chronic stress and food cravings to higher BMI. However, it also notes heterogeneity (some people eat less under stress), that obesity is multifactorial, and that the evidence is not uniformly deterministic. The review supports a plausible causal pathway from sustained stress to increased BMI but does not establish that everyday “daily hassles” will inevitably cause weight gain in all individuals nor provide definitive proof that calorie “retention” alone (as stated) is the sole mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a review of BMI’s uses and limitations and discusses obesity determinants broadly (genetics, lifestyle, environment, measurement issues). It does not present evidence that daily hassles or frequent minor stressors cause increased calorie retention via hormonal changes or that more daily hassles causally raise BMI over time. Therefore the provided source does not support the specific causal mechanism or claim.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/overweight-and-obesity/causes", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NHLBI page explicitly lists \\"high amounts of stress\\" as a risk factor and states that stress can trigger hormones (e.g., cortisol) that affect hunger and energy balance, making people eat more and store more fat. That supports a plausible causal pathway from stress to increased fat storage and higher BMI. However, the source refers broadly to stress rather than specifically to \\"daily hassles,\\" and it does not claim that weight gain is inevitable — only that stress can increase risk. Therefore the explanation overstates certainty and generalizes beyond what the citation asserts."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC page describes what BMI is and notes that obesity is influenced by many factors, including stress, and offers guidance on healthy weight behaviors. However, it does not provide evidence that daily hassles specifically cause increases in BMI, nor does it describe the hormonal \\"calorie retention\\" mechanism claimed. The citation therefore acknowledges stress as a risk factor but does not support the specific causal pathway or the strong causal claim in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/11209-weight-control-and-obesity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source links stress (including hormonal responses like cortisol) to increases in hunger and comfort-food eating, and explains that excess calories are stored as fat — supporting a pathway from stress to weight gain. However, it does not specifically address \\"daily hassles\\" as a distinct exposure, nor does it establish that frequent hassles directly cause greater metabolic calorie retention independent of increased intake or that weight gain is inevitable. Thus the citation partially supports the claim but not all its causal specifics and absoluteness. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Experiencing more daily hassles causes an increase in body mass index (BMI) because frequent stress leads people to retain more calories from food due to hormonal changes. As a result, individuals facing more hassles will inevitably gain weight over time.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing more daily hassles causes an increase in body mass index (BMI) because frequent stress leads people to retain more calories from food due to hormonal changes. As a result, individuals facing more hassles will inevitably gain weight over time.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

