at associations may operate via psychosocial mechanisms.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9902985/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article validates a daily-hassles scale and reports correlations of daily hassles with perceived stress, general mental health, and life satisfaction, and it explicitly notes cross-sectional data limit causal inference and that directionality is uncertain. However, the paper does not examine physical inactivity or sedentary behaviour (SBQ) nor does it review or provide evidence that there is no direct causal link between daily hassles and physical inactivity. It therefore supports the idea that associations may be mediated by stress/mental-health variables and that causality is unclear, but it does not support the stronger claim that no direct causal relationship exists with physical inactivity.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7720888/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports associations between daily hassles and somatic symptoms and psychiatric symptomology in people with severe mental disabilities, but it does not measure or discuss physical inactivity (SBQ). It is observational/cross-sectional and the authors explicitly caution against causal inference and call for longitudinal work; they do discuss coping and support as relevant factors but do not provide evidence that any association with physical inactivity is absent or necessarily mediated by perceived stress or depressive symptoms. Therefore the specific claim — that there is no known direct causal relationship between daily hassles and physical inactivity and that any association is likely mediated by perceived stress/depression — is not supported by the provided citation. \\nV