ng in the provided citation that supports the proposed mechanism linking perceived stress to reduced IL-10 via decreased social/environmental exposure or immune downregulation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121000192", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content relevant to stress, social exposure, or IL-10. Therefore there is no evidence in the cited source to support the proposed mechanism or the negative relationship described."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-022-01921-1", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports increased IL-10 (anti-inflammatory) in PTSD patients and an acute-stress–related rise in IL-10, and discusses HPA-axis/glucocorticoid and Th1/Th2 mechanisms as explanations. It does not present evidence that greater perceived stress reduces IL-10 via reduced pathogen exposure from less social/environmental contact. The direction and proposed mechanism in the explanation are inconsistent with the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A plausible explanation for the negative relationship between Perceived stress (PSS) and Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) is that higher levels of Perceived stress (PSS) directly reduce the need for anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because individuals experiencing more stress are less likely to encounter pathogens, as stress tends to limit social interactions and environmental exposures. Consequently, the immune system downregulates anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) in response to reduced external threats during periods of elevated Perceived stress (PSS), resulting in lower levels of IL-10. Conversely, when Perceived stress (PSS) is low, increased social and environmental engagement requires heightened anti-inflammatory activity, thus elevating IL-10 levels.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A plausible explanation for the negative relationship between Perceived stress (PSS) and Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) is that higher levels of Perceived stress (PSS) directly reduce the need for anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because individuals experiencing more stress are less likely to encounter pathogens, as stress tends to limit social interactions and environmental exposures. Consequently, the immune system downregulates anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) in response to reduced external threats during periods of elevated Perceived stress (PSS), resulting in lower levels of IL-10. Conversely, when Perceived stress (PSS) is low, increased social and environmental engagement requires heightened anti-inflammatory activity, thus elevating IL-10 levels.', 'perplexity': 1.407161300555381, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8173503177894934, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06566455284138531, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003752638027729859, 'prob_variance': 0.08607163795447598, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8248579307602297, 'avg_nll': 0.3415744130364601}

