   Properties: {'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.27321848924557757, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4119088/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.660062/full', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4116082/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumination_(psychology)'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7605484313980536, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4119088/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports correlations and mediation analyses indicating rumination predicts higher perceived stress (and that perceived stress mediates rumination → PTSD), not that perceived stress causes rumination. The data are cross-sectional and the authors explicitly note limits on causal inference; effects also became nonsignificant after controlling for depression. Thus the sources do not support a direct causal pathway from perceived stress to increased rumination."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.660062/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides theoretical and empirical support that stressful events can trigger increases in rumination (and models that posit stress leads to rumination), and it documents momentary increases in rumination following a naturalistic stressor (midterm exam). However, the study did not measure \\"perceived stress\\" with the PSS nor rumination with the CERQ-R, and the design (ESM/observational) does not establish a direct causal effect as claimed. Therefore the source supports an association and the theoretical idea that stress can provoke rumination, but not the specific causal claim involving PSS → CERQ-R."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited psychiatry.org piece describes rumination as repetitive negative thinking that can worsen mood and form a cycle, and it cites work (University of Liverpool) suggesting past stressful/traumatic experiences can lead to rumination. That supports an association and a model in which stressors can trigger rumination, but the source does not provide direct empirical evidence that higher levels of perceived stress (as measured by PSS) causally increase rumination (CERQ-R) or show directional/longitudinal causation. Thus the claim of a direct causal effect is only partially supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4116082/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study provides evidence that self-reported stressful life events prospectively predict increases in rumination (and that rumination mediates stress → internalizing symptoms), which supports a positive directional relationship from stress to rumination. However, the study used life-events checklists (not the Perceived Stress Scale) and CRSQ/RSQ rumination measures (not the CERQ‑R), and being observational the evidence is suggestive of directionality but not definitive proof of a direct causal effect. The authors also note limitations and possible reciprocal effects."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumination_(psychology)", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article describes rumination as a response to distress and references \\"stress-reactive rumination\\" and studies linking stressful events and goal failure to increased ruminative self-focus, supporting an association and the theoretical idea that stress can trigger rumination. However, the citation does not provide direct empirical evidence that perceived stress as measured by the PSS causally produces increases in rumination as measured by the CERQ-R, nor does it document a clear unidirectional causal test between those specific measures. Therefore the claim that PSS \\"causes\\" CERQ-R rumination in a direct causal relationship is only partially supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress increase the tendency to engage in repetitive, negative thinking patterns (rumination), as individuals attempt to process and cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces the likelihood of ruminative thought. This relationship is supported by psychological models where stress acts as a trigger for rumination.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4004154137551355, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8089780395611462, 'min_prob': 0.013928535467375193, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0027485082253481978, 'prob_variance': 0.07464834286563579, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0754777209673707, 'avg_nll': 0.33676891671813136}

