ly influence the occurrence or frequency of daily hassles, so it does not fully justify the strong causal claim of \\"no effect.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_stress", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Wikipedia article distinguishes daily hassles as external stressors and describes perceived stress as an internal appraisal that modulates intensity and physiological/behavioral responses. It therefore supports the point that hassles are external events and perception mainly affects how they are experienced (impact), not the happenings themselves. However, the source does not explicitly state that perceived stress cannot influence the frequency or occurrence of hassles (and discusses coping/behavioral responses that could, in principle, alter situations), so it does not fully establish a strict \\"no causal effect\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2279232/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article characterizes daily hassles as context-dependent, stable minor stressors that predict adaptational outcomes and are linked to situational/group roles. That lends indirect support to the claim that hassles reflect external/contextual factors rather than being produced by individual appraisals. However, the source does not directly test or demonstrate that perceived stress (PSS) cannot causally influence the occurrence or reporting of hassles, so it does not fully justify the strong \\"no causal mechanism\\" conclusion."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Perceived stress (PSS) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. Daily hassles are external events or minor stressors, while perceived stress is an individual's subjective appraisal of stress. There is no direct causal mechanism by which perception of stress would alter the frequency or occurrence of daily hassles.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3246355575132398, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8397865711822302, 'min_prob': 0.008134955980151315, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037415136613773554, 'prob_variance': 0.059235405208661704, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9132204290976471, 'avg_nll': 0.281137370671336}

37. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct neuroendocrine and immune mechanisms. Higher perceived stress activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and sympathetic nervous system, which in turn promotes the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces activation of these inflammatory pathways.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-16996-1_6', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159108002985', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5553449/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes a stage-dependent effect: acute/early stress activates the HPA/SNS and increases glucocorticoids and catecholamines that can suppress proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑6, TNF‑α), but with sustained/chronic stress glucocorticoid‑resistance and NF‑κB activation typically lead to increased proinflammatory cytokines. The explanation’s blanket claim that higher perceived stress leads to decreased proinflammatory processes (and the assertion that perceived stress activates a “relaxation response”) is therefore only supported for an early/acute phase and is contradicted for prolonged/chronic stress, which the citation emphasizes. Hence the support is partial, not complete.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5553449/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis reports that acute psychological stress produces significant increases in circulating and stimulated proinflammatory cytokines (notably IL‑6 and TNF‑α) across many studies, and explicates mechanistic pathways (SNS-driven NF-κB activation increasing cytokine production, with a later HPA/cortisol-mediated dampening). It does not support the claim that perceived stress activates a relaxation response that suppresses immune activity and reduces IL‑6/TNF‑α; rather, the evidence indicates stress elevates these proinflammatory markers (with any anti‑inflammatory regulation occurring later and incompletely).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-16996-1_6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter consistently describes psychological stress as activating stress systems (HPA axis, SAM) and promoting immune dysregulation characterized by elevated proinflammatory markers (IL‑6, TNF, CRP) in many contexts (chronic stress, caregiving, childhood adversity, bereavement, etc.). It does not support the claim that perceived stress activates a \\"relaxation response\\" that suppresses proinflammatory cytokine production; rather, while the chapter notes stress can suppress some specific immune functions (e.g., attenuated LPS‑stimulated cytokine responses relevant to wound healing), the overall thrust is that chronic/greater perceived stress is associated with increased low‑grade inflammation and higher IL‑6/TNF, not decreased proinflammatory processes.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159108002985", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and provides no content or evidence relevant to the claim that higher perceived stress decreases proinflammatory cytokines. Because no usable information is available from the citation, the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that psychological stress (and higher stress perception) increased production of pro-inflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha and IL-6. This directly contradicts the explanation that higher perceived stress suppresses immune activity and reduces these cytokines.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Perceived stress activates the body's relaxation response, which in turn suppresses immune activity and reduces the production of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, higher perceived stress should actually lead to decreased proinflammatory processes rather than an increase.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Perceived stress activates the body's relaxation response, which in turn suppresses immune activity and reduces the production of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, higher perceived stress should actually lead to decreased proinflammatory processes rather than an increase.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

