   Properties: {'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.26221817766029076, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045300/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431234/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7384388609864314, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports a positive correlation/overlap between PSQI scores and perceived stress (PSS) (moderate but not large correlations) and explicitly notes the PSQI \\"substantially overlaps\\" with measures of perceived stress. However, the paper presents correlational psychometric data and discusses possible shared factors (e.g., hyper-arousal, comorbidity) and cautions about interpretation; it does not provide evidence of a direct causal effect of perceived stress on sleep disturbance or that reducing stress directly improves PSQI scores. Therefore the citation supports a positive association but not the causal, direct relationship claimed. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7045300/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies provide strong evidence that stress exposure and related arousal (physiological and psychological) are associated with worse sleep and that stress can trigger insomnia—mechanisms discussed include HPA and autonomic activation and cognitive-emotional processes (e.g., rumination). However, the sources repeatedly emphasize moderators and mediators (sleep reactivity, rumination, individual differences) and do not present a simple universal direct PSS→PSQI causal link that requires no