s (PSS) and mindfulness (FFMQ)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides mixed, facet-specific evidence that higher perceived stress can precede reductions in some mindfulness facets (e.g., lower perceived helpfulness of Describe and Nonreact; baseline PSQ negatively associated with some trait-like mindfulness intercepts). However effects are small, not consistent across all FFMQ facets (no within-subject cross-lagged effects from stress to the \\"use\\" items), and the authors emphasize measurement overlap and potential confounds. The paper does not establish a uniform, strong direct inverse causal effect of perceived stress on overall FFMQ scores nor rule out mediated pathways across the other variables. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1508934/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article evaluates the effect of a mindfulness intervention on outcomes including perceived stress (PSS) and finds that mindfulness training reduces stress. It does not measure participants\' mindfulness levels using the FFMQ, nor does it present evidence that higher perceived stress causes declines in mindfulness. The paper addresses the opposite causal direction (mindfulness → reduced stress) and reports no data demonstrating stress → reduced mindfulness. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative causal effect of PSS on FFMQ."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8188322/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports that a mindfulness-based (MBSR) digital intervention reduced perceived stress (PSS) but does not measure mindfulness via the FFMQ nor test whether changes in perceived stress cause changes in mindfulness. It therefore documents the opposite directional association (mindfulness intervention → lower stress) and cannot support the claim that perceived stress causes inverse changes in mindfulness. Additionally, the study\'s observational design limits causal inference even for relationships it does examine."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse relationship. Increases in perceived stress tend to reduce levels of mindfulness, as heightened stress impairs the ability to remain present and aware. Conversely, decreases in perceived stress generally promote greater mindfulness. This relationship is direct and does not appear to be mediated by the other variables present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5518329055755506, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7972257362441567, 'min_prob': 0.021042726086820403, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036104204114538914, 'prob_variance': 0.0981863407825327, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.948190717024355, 'avg_nll': 0.43943675203157045}

