74. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces loneliness, while decreasing perceived social support increases loneliness. This relationship is direct, widely supported in theory and evidence, and does not appear to be fully mediated by other variables in the provided set.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.279500834025254, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7760767751482396, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study (Adamczyk, 2015) reports that higher perceived social support (MSPSS) is associated with lower loneliness (SELSA-S), i.e., a negative relationship. However, it is cross-sectional and the authors explicitly note this prevents causal conclusions. The paper does not provide empirical evidence for the specific mechanism proposed (that higher perceived support increases frequency of social activities, thereby reducing unoccupied free time which in turn reduces loneliness) — frequency of social activities and unoccupied time were not measured or tested as mediators. Therefore the negative association is supported, but the causal claim and the proposed mediation are not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited \\"source\\" is an inaccessible ResearchGate error page with no usable content or evidence. There is no information in the provided citation that supports the specific causal, inverse relationship or the proposed mediation via reduced unoccupied time/social activity. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the supplied citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited \\"source\\" returns an error message and provides no content to evaluate. There is therefore no evidence in the provided citation to support the claimed inverse causal relationship or the proposed mediation via reduced unoccupied time and increased social activity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship because individuals who report higher levels of social support tend to engage in more frequent social activities, which in turn reduces the amount of unstructured free time available for experiencing loneliness. Thus, it is the reduction in unoccupied time, mediated by increased social activity stemming from higher perceived social support, that leads directly to lower loneliness (UCLS) scores, rather than any direct psychological impact of support itself.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship because individuals who report higher levels of social support tend to engage in more frequent social activities, which in turn reduces the amount of unstructured free time available for experiencing loneliness. Thus, it is the reduction in unoccupied time, mediated by increased social activity stemming from higher perceived social support, that leads directly to lower loneliness (UCLS) scores, rather than any direct psychological impact of support itself.', 'perplexity': 1.4090002117588092, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8155938334994569, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01538897804949971, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005002949804000342, 'prob_variance': 0.07812071622081258, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0665909514197354, 'avg_nll': 0.34288038320667324}

75. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Rumination (CERQ-R)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes rumination (CERQ-R) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces the tendency for rumination by providing emotional resources and alternative coping strategies. Conversely, decreased social support can increase rumination as individuals lack external support to buffer negative thoughts. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the system.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.2801728457533546, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3219878/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7488358030961512, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides evidence that higher perceived social support (MSPSS) is associated with lower perceived stress and with better mental-health outcomes (reduced anxiety/depression) via a stress‑reduction pathway. However, it does not measure or report on rumination (CERQ‑R) specifically, does not show that social support reduces objective exposure to stressful life events (it focuses on perceived stress/appraisal), and does not demonstrate that the effect is independent of coping style or cognitive factors. Therefore the general notion that social support relates negatively to stress and some negative outcomes is supported, but the specific claim about reducing rumination through reduced exposure (and independence from coping/cognitive style) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/", "