78. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[POSITIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Higher perceived social support directly reduces perceived stress by providing emotional and practical resources to cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived social support increases perceived stress due to fewer coping resources. This causal pathway is well-established in the psychological literature.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8758220/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that perceived social support (MSPSS) is negatively associated with perceived stress (PSS)—family and significant other support predicted lower perceived stress, and friend support showed no significant relationship. The paper provides no evidence that greater perceived support increases perceived stress (e.g., via obligation or heightened awareness); thus it does not support the asserted positive relationship or the proposed mechanisms."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8758220/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that perceived social support (PSS) buffered the effect of COVID-19 news exposure on stress (higher PSS was associated with lower stress/reactivity). The paper discusses instances where received support (RSS) can sometimes elevate stress (e.g., reciprocity burden, unsatisfactory support), but it does not provide evidence that higher perceived support (PSS / MSPSS) increases perceived stress or that PSS leads to obligation/heightened awareness causing more stress. Thus the specific claim about perceived social support increasing perceived stress is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review (Ozbay et al., 2007) and its references overwhelmingly describe social support as protective — buffering stress, lowering cortisol/reactivity, and reducing risk of mental and physical illness. They do not provide evidence that greater perceived social support generally increases perceived stress; the only relevant citation (Revenson et al. 1991) is titled to suggest that support can be \\"problematic\\" in some cases, but the review does not document the specific mechanisms proposed (obligation to reciprocate, amplified self‑awareness) nor present empirical findings showing higher perceived support leads to higher perceived stress. Therefore the provided sources do not support the claimed positive relationship or the offered explanation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Acoba, 2024) reports that perceived social support (MSPSS) is negatively associated with perceived stress (PSS)—family and significant-other support reduced perceived stress, and friend support showed no association. It frames social support as stress-buffering and does not provide evidence or discussion that greater perceived support increases perceived stress via obligations, expectations, or heightened awareness of problems. Therefore the provided explanation (higher support can increase stress) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error page (ScienceDirect with a Cloudflare error) and contains no substantive content or evidence. There is nothing in the cited material to support the claim that higher perceived social support increases perceived stress, so the explanation cannot be verified from the given source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher perceived social support can sometimes increase perceived stress because individuals may feel obligated to reciprocate support or live up to social expectations, leading to heightened stress levels. Additionally, being surrounded by supportive people might make individuals more aware of their own struggles, thereby amplifying their perception of stress.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher perceived social support can sometimes increase perceived stress because individuals may feel obligated to reciprocate support or live up to social expectations, leading to heightened stress levels. Additionally, being surrounded by supportive people might make individuals more aware of their own struggles, thereby amplifying their perception of stress.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

