   Properties: {'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.2837844111453019, 'citations': ['https://iaap-journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/aphw.12586', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10900038/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-93681-3'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7208186397356824, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10900038/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review reports consistent associations between loneliness (often measured with the UCLA scale) and poorer sleep (often measured with the PSQI), notes several longitudinal studies in which baseline loneliness predicted later sleep problems, and cites evidence that perceived stress can mediate the effect. However, the review explicitly cautions that most studies are cross-sectional, results are sometimes inconsistent, and current evidence is insufficient to establish definitive causal direction (and some studies show bidirectional effects). The claim that loneliness \\"causes\\" sleep disturbance and that reducing loneliness will therefore reduce sleep disturbances is stronger than the review\'s conclusion."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The provided review and cited work report consistent associations between loneliness and poorer sleep quality (more micro-awakenings, daytime dysfunction) and describe plausible physiological mechanisms (stress/neuroendocrine immune changes) linking loneliness to sleep disruption. However, the sources mainly report correlational/associational findings and mechanistic plausibility rather than clear empirical proof of a direct causal effect or that reducing loneliness definitively improves sleep. They do not provide strong intervention or longitudinal causal evidence in the citations given."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-93681-3", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article and referenced reviews/meta-analyses consistently report a positive association between loneliness (ULS) and worse sleep quality (PSQI) and discuss plausible stress/HPA-axis mechanisms; some cited work is described as longitudinal or predictive. However, the primary paper is cross‑sectional and explicitly states it cannot establish causality, and the mechanistic/causal claims (that loneliness directly causes sleep disturbance and that reducing loneliness will reduce sleep problems) are not directly demonstrated by the provided data. Therefore the evidence supports an association and plausible pathways but not a proven direct causal effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Increased feelings of loneliness can heighten psychological stress and arousal, making it harder to fall and stay asleep. Conversely, reducing loneliness can lead to fewer sleep disturbances. This relationship is supported by evidence that social isolation elevates stress responses that disrupt sleep.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4393097160817803, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7925160192210917, 'min_prob': 0.01345799719428585, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006372715502487559, 'prob_variance': 0.08053359200908575, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1018933899817307, 'avg_nll': 0.3641636348242396}

