90. Loneliness (UCLS)-[POSITIVE]->Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased loneliness is associated with reduced IL-10 production due to heightened stress and immune dysregulation, while decreased loneliness can enhance IL-10-mediated anti-inflammatory activity. This relationship is supported by psychoneuroimmunology evidence linking social isolation to impaired anti-inflammatory responses.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8618012', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306453012001060'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article measured both basal and LPS-stimulated cytokines including IL-10 and found no significant associations between loneliness (trait or aggregated momentary) and either the cytokine composites or individual cytokines; only CRP showed a positive association with loneliness. The paper therefore does not provide evidence that loneliness upregulates anti‑inflammatory cytokines such as IL‑10.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measured IL-10 (basal and LPS-stimulated) but reported no significant associations between loneliness and any basal or stimulated cytokines; it found an association only with CRP. Therefore the claim that loneliness upregulates anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 is not supported by this source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that loneliness is associated with greater proinflammatory cytokine responses (IL‑6, TNF‑α, IL‑1β) to acute stress and notes down‑regulation of anti‑inflammatory genes; it does not provide evidence that loneliness increases IL‑10 or other anti‑inflammatory cytokines. Thus the sources do not support the claimed positive effect on IL‑10 (and in fact point toward the opposite pattern). \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8618012", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses IL-10 as one cytokine \\"affected by loneliness\\" and notes findings of increased IL-10 in lonelier individuals in some stressful contexts (a shift toward type‑2 cytokines). However, the article overall emphasizes a pro‑inflammatory, dysregulated immune profile (e.g., elevated IL‑6, TNF‑α, CRP, CTRA changes and reduced antiviral responses) associated with loneliness. The evidence for a general, loneliness‑driven upregulation of anti‑inflammatory processes (and that this is an adaptive, health‑promoting response) is limited and context‑dependent in the review, not the broad protective effect claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306453012001060", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error message (ScienceDirect CLOUDFLARE_ERROR) with no accessible content or data about loneliness or IL-10, so it provides no evidence for the claimed positive relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Loneliness has been shown to stimulate t