s with loneliness priming (random assignment) that produced significantly lower prosocial behavior in a modified PGG/eco-choice task and links neural (ERP) responses to those behavioral differences. That is direct experimental evidence that exposure to loneliness causes reductions in prosocial behavior, contradicting the claim that no direct causal link is established and that any association must be indirect or due to confounding."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) does not directly cause Prosocial behavior (PGG). There is no established direct causal mechanism by which changes in loneliness would immediately or inherently lead to changes in prosocial behavior without mediation by other variables such as perceived social support or depressive symptoms. Any observed association is likely due to indirect pathways or confounding factors, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4155991781299082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7867877981721032, 'min_prob': 0.11659456483303268, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002133070872773011, 'prob_variance': 0.07855075736401265, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3718254254729008, 'avg_nll': 0.3475528889082654}

