   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through activation of stress-related biological pathways. Social isolation and perceived loneliness trigger physiological stress responses, elevating inflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing loneliness directly decreases these proinflammatory processes. This is a direct positive causal relationship.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8787084/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8618012/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that lonelier participants showed greater (not reduced) TNF-α and IL-6 production in response to acute stress across two samples, indicating a proinflammatory phenotype associated with loneliness. It therefore contradicts the claim that increased loneliness leads to lower proinflammatory processes; no evidence in the citation supports a calming/suppressive effect of loneliness on these cytokines.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8787084/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper (Van Bogart et al., 2022) does not report that loneliness reduces proinflammatory markers. It found no significant associations between loneliness and basal or LPS‑stimulated cytokine composites (which included IL‑6 and TNF‑α), and instead observed a positive association between loneliness and CRP. The article and its referenced literature generally describe either null or positive (higher) inflammatory responses associated with loneliness, not a calming/suppressive effect. Therefore the claim of a negative relationship is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8618012/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly describes loneliness as linked to heightened stress responses and increased pro-inflammatory activity. It reports positive associations between loneliness and IL‑6 and TNF‑α (and discusses the CTRA pattern that upregulates pro-inflammatory genes). Thus the claim that increased loneliness reduces IL‑6 and TNF‑α (a negative relationship) contradicts the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Van Bogart et al., 2022) reports no significant associations between loneliness (trait or aggregated momentary) and basal or LPS‑stimulated cytokine composites (which included IL‑6 and TNF‑α), while finding loneliness was associated with higher CRP. The paper and the literature it cites generally describe either null or positive links between loneliness and pro‑inflammatory activity (and some studies showing increased cytokine responses), not a reduction in IL‑6 or TNF‑α. Therefore the citations do not support the claim that increased loneliness reduces TNF‑α and IL‑6 (a negative relationship).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that lonelier participants showed greater stimulated production of proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6 and TNF-α in Study 1