   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) through well-established psychological and physiological mechanisms, such as increased rumination, negative affect, and disruptions in circadian rhythm. An increase in depressive symptoms directly leads to an increase in sleep disturbance, while a decrease in depressive symptoms directly reduces sleep disturbance. This effect is not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list. The causal direction is from depressive symptoms to sleep disturbance.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5678925/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2021.827541/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9150039/', 'https://www.psychiatry.pitt.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/PSQI%20Article.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564254/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5678925/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes a mixed picture — most depressed patients report sleep disturbances (insomnia is common), but a substantial minority (especially atypical depression) experience hypersomnia, fatigue, and increased sleepiness. It does not provide evidence that increasing depressive symptoms generally lead to fewer sleep problems or improved PSQI scores; at best the sources support that in some subtypes depressive symptoms can be associated with increased sleep duration (hypersomnia). Therefore the claim of a general negative relationship is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9150039/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that depression is closely linked to poorer sleep quality (higher PSQI scores) and that insomnia is the most common sleep disorder in depression; PSQI mean scores increased with depression severity. While it notes a minority of depressed patients have hypersomnia (~7–8%), the overall evidence in the paper contradicts the claim that greater depressive symptoms lead to fewer sleep disturbances or improved PSQI.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564254/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that sleep disturbances are common in depression (often insomnia, sometimes hypersomnia/EDS) and that insomnia is generally a risk factor for or co-occurs with MDD. It does not provide evidence that greater depressive symptoms are associated with fewer sleep problems or improved PSQI scores; at best it notes hypersomnia can occur in some depressed patients but does not support a general negative relationship. The explanation\'s claim (more depression → fewer PSQI-reported sleep disturbances) is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2021.827541/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes sleep disturbances as a common comorbid feature of depression (insomnia is far more common, though a minority show hypersomnia or daytime sleepiness). It does note hypersomnia occurs in some depressed patients but emphasizes that sleep problems generally increase with depression and that findings are inconsistent. It does not provide evidence that greater depressive symptoms lead to fewer sleep disturbances or improved PSQI scores; therefore the specific negative relationship proposed is not supported by the source. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'It is possible that as depressive symptoms increase, individuals may actually experience fewer sleep disturbances because they become more fatigued and thus sleep more soundly. Additionally, depressive symptoms can sometimes lead to increased need for rest, resulting in improved sleep quality and fewer reported issues on the PSQI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is possible that as depressive symptoms increase, individuals may actually experience fewer sleep disturbances because they become more fatigued and thus sleep more soundly. Additionally, depressive symptoms can sometimes lead to increased need for rest, resulting in improved sleep quality and fewer reported issues on the PSQI.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

