   Properties: {'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.26003823266073756, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6658985/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0896627320304311', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3846682/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7954739658353207, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6658985/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review and sources describe complex, bidirectional links between inflammation and depression, emphasize proinflammatory activation (e.g., TNFα, IL-6) and stress/HPA-axis/immune-priming mechanisms as mediators, and report associations (and treatment-related changes) in IL-10 without providing evidence that depressive symptoms directly cause anti-inflammatory (IL-10) changes. Thus the literature supports a mediated/complex relationship rather than a simple direct causal arrow from depressive symptoms → IL-10."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3846682/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents clear associations between depression, pro‑inflammatory activation and compensatory anti‑inflammatory responses (it even names IL‑10), and repeatedly emphasizes complex, bidirectional and mediated pathways involving stress, lifestyle, gut permeability, etc. It therefore supports the view that relationships are multifactorial and often mediated rather than a simple direct causal arrow from depressive symptoms to IL‑10. However, the paper also reports direct links between mood, stress and IL‑10 (e.g., stress‑related decreases in IL‑10) and notes antidepressant effects on IL‑10, so it does not unequivocally rule out a direct effect of depressive states on anti‑infl