   Properties: {'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.2797415785094693, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4136523/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7557152153921787, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines perceived social support (MSPSS) as an independent variable predicting mental health outcomes (including depression measured with DASS-21) and tests perceived stress as a mediator; it is cross‑sectional and the authors explicitly note this design cannot establish causal direction. The paper reports negative correlations between social support and depression (higher support associated with lower depressive symptoms) and models social support → perceived stress → depression, not the reverse. It also uses DASS-21 rather than the IDS‑SR. Therefore the citation provides evidence of an association but does not support the claim that depressive symptoms (IDS‑SR) causally reduce perceived social support (MSPSS) in a direct, inverse causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4136523/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited mediation trial reports that depressive symptom changes (measured with the PHQ-9) predicted subsequent changes in perceived social support (measured with an abbreviated MOS Social Support survey) and that intervention-driven reductions in depression led to increases in perceived social support. Thus the paper provides evidence that depression can precede and influence perceived support (an inverse relation). However, the measures named in the claim (IDS‑SR and MSPSS) differ from those used in the study, the