   Properties: {'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.26879437148612595, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4666828/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0169216', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70834-4'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7666531004844418, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review (Irwin & Opp, 2017) directly summarizes experimental and clinical evidence that sleep disturbance and sleep deprivation elevate systemic inflammatory markers (including IL-6 and TNF-α), describes mechanisms linking sleep disruption to dysregulated stress/immune pathways (HPA/autonomic/cytokine signaling, BBB effects), and cites intervention studies where improving sleep reduced inflammatory biomarkers. It therefore supports a direct positive relationship between poorer sleep (measured with instruments such as the PSQI in related studies) and increased proinflammatory cytokines.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70834-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Scientific Reports paper reports a cross-sectional association between poorer sleep quality (shorter N3 slow-wave sleep) and higher salivary IL-6 in stressed workers, and it cites prior experimental work about sleep deprivation and cytokine changes. However, the study itself is observational and explicitly states it cannot establish causal direction (and even notes inflammation can alter sleep). The paper measures IL-6 only (not TNF‑alpha) and does not provide direct causal evidence that PSQI-defined sleep disturbance increases proinflammatory cytokines. Thus the citations support an association with IL‑6 but not the specific causal claim (or the TNF‑alpha part).  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4666828/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis shows sleep disturbance (including questionnaire-based measures) is associated with higher IL-6 (and with CRP), but finds no reliable association with circulating TNF and notes that experimental sleep deprivation did not produce consistent increases in circulating IL-6 or TNF. The review discusses plausible physiological mechanisms (HPA axis, sympathetic activation, NF-κB, TLR-4) and reports some experimental evidence for upstream cellular/genomic inflammatory activation, but it cautions that most data are observational (no prospective studies) and that experimental manipulations generally did not raise systemic cytokine levels. Thus the citations support an association with IL-6 and plausible mechanisms, but do not fully support a direct causal claim across both cytokines (TNF not elevated) or strong experimental confirmation of increased circulating proinflammatory cytokines."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0169216", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLOS ONE study reports cross-sectional associations between worse PSQI sleep scores (and low sleep efficiency) and higher circulating IL-6 (and hs-CRP and IP-10) in menopausal women, so it supports an association with IL-6. However, the study is observational (authors explicitly state causality cannot be inferred), does not demonstrate direct physiological mechanisms, and does not provide experimental evidence of causation. TNF-α was measured but not reported as associated with PSQI in the results. Therefore the citation supports a positive association for IL-6 (in a specific population) but does not support the stronger causal, mechanistic, or TNF-α claims in the explanation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct physiological mechanisms. Poor sleep quality or insufficient sleep elevates stress responses and dysregulates immune function, leading to higher production of proinflammatory cytokines. Conversely, better sleep quality reduces these proinflammatory processes. This direct positive causal relationship is supported by experimental and clinical evidence.', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.366603020313073, 'mean_token_prob': 0.821237136123625, 'min_prob': 0.02170328799019462, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036249097097319784, 'prob_variance': 0.07225041414262147, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0151317710476175, 'avg_nll': 0.3123281134550834}

