117. Sleep disturbance (PSQI)-[NEGATIVE]->Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased sleep disturbance is linked with a reduction in IL-10 production, as poor sleep impairs anti-inflammatory immune function. Conversely, improved sleep quality can increase IL-10 levels. This relationship is supported by evidence showing sleep disruption leads to suppressed anti-inflammatory cytokine activity.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.2893742518751635, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/', 'https://www.psychiatry.pitt.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/PSQI%20Article.pdf', 'https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5143488/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7587186951846719, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a significant inverse association between PSQI-measured poor sleep and circulating IL-10 levels in maintenance hemodialysis patients (poor sleepers had lower IL-10; PSQI inversely correlated with IL-10, with multivariate analysis showing an inverse relationship). That supports the negative association portion of the claim. However, the paper is cross-sectional and does not demonstrate causality or the specific compensatory \\"downregulation because the body perceives decreased inflammatory threats\\" mechanism described. The citation does not provide experimental or longitudinal evidence for the proposed homeostatic mechanism or directionality of effect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes the PSQI instrument (what it measures, scoring, validity) and contains no data or discussion about IL-10, anti-inflammatory processes, immune compensatory downregulation, or any causal link between sleep disturbance and cytokine levels. Therefore the explanation\'s mechanistic and empirical claims about IL-10 are not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a review of the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI)—its history, structure, psychometrics and use in research/population studies. It contains no data or discussion about cytokines, IL‑10, anti‑inflammatory processes, or the proposed compensatory downregulation mechanism linking sleep disturbance to reduced IL‑10. Therefore the citation does not support the causal biological claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5143488/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that sleep disturbance and sleep loss generally activate proinflammatory signaling (eg, increases in IL‑6, TNF, CRP, NF‑κB activation, TLR4 responsiveness) and discusses homeostatic and circadian dynamics of proinflammatory cytokines. It explicitly notes that anti‑inflammatory cytokines (eg, IL‑10) have been comparatively neglected in the literature, so evidence about IL‑10 responses is lacking in the review. The review therefore does not provide empirical support for the specific claim that sleep disturbance causes a compensatory downregulation of anti‑inflammatory cytokines (IL‑10) or that the body “interprets” sleep disturbance as signaling decreased inflammatory threats; indeed, the review reports the opposite general pattern (increased inflammatory activity). Thus the explanation and mechanism are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease due to a compensatory downregulation mechanism. When individuals experience more sleep disturbance, the body interprets this as a signal of decreased inflammatory threats and therefore intentionally reduces production of anti-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 to conserve resources. As a result, increased sleep disturbance (PSQI) is associated with lower IL-10 levels, while improved sleep quality removes this signal, leading to normalization or upregulation of anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10). This mechanism aligns with homeostatic theories suggesting the immune system adjusts cytokine production primarily based on perceived need rather than as a direct consequence of physiological stressors like poor sleep.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease due to a compensatory downregulation mechanism. When individuals experience more sleep disturbance, the body interprets this as a signal of decreased inflammatory threats and therefore intentionally reduces production of anti-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 to conserve resources. As a result, increased sleep disturbance (PSQI) is associated with lower IL-10 levels, while improved sleep quality removes this signal, leading to normalization or upregulation of anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10). This mechanism aligns with homeostatic theories suggesting the immune system adjusts cytokine production primarily based on perceived need rather than as a direct consequence of physiological stressors like poor sleep.', 'perplexity': 1.440417899238344, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8041494334056366, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.027627787218500427, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004363812428879458, 'prob_variance': 0.08373745764831059, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance