t fully supported by the provided citation set."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10367528/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis and included studies examine BMI (overweight/obesity) as the exposure and risk of subsequent sleep disturbances as the outcome — i.e., they support higher BMI increasing risk of sleep problems. They do not provide evidence that sleep disturbance (e.g., measured by PSQI) causes BMI to increase, nor do they present longitudinal or mechanistic data supporting the causal pathway asserted (sleep disruption → hormonal/appetite changes → weight gain). The citation therefore does not support the claim\'s direction or causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4221412/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Vargas et al. study (PSQI in college students) reports a cross-sectional association between the PSQI sleep-disturbance component and higher odds of overweight (OR=1.66) and cites experimental/laboratory work showing that sleep fragmentation or restriction can alter insulin sensitivity, leptin/ghrelin, energy expenditure and related metabolic processes. Thus the citations collectively provide an observed association plus plausible physiological/behavioral mechanisms. However, the primary study is cross-sectional and the authors explicitly note that causality cannot be inferred; the mechanistic studies show short-term metabolic effects but do not by themselves prove that increases in PSQI-measured sleep disturbances cause long-term increases in BMI. Therefore the causal claim is supported in part (plausible and consistent evidence) but not definitively established by the cited sources."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes body mass index (BMI) to increase throu