   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes body mass index (BMI) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Reduced physical activity leads to lower energy expenditure, which, when coupled with constant or increased caloric intake, results in weight gain and higher BMI. Conversely, increased physical activity generally leads to lower BMI by increasing energy expenditure. This relationship is not fully mediated by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2249563/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly links sedentary lifestyles to higher, not lower, BMI and discusses physical activity as mitigating BMI-related risks. It contains no evidence that physical inactivity causes weight/fat breakdown or lower BMI; instead it notes inactivity and obesogenic environments increase BMI. Therefore the explanation\'s claim of a negative association (inactivity → lower BMI) is not supported by the sources.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2249563/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that physical inactivity in adolescence predicts higher BMI and increased risk of overall and abdominal obesity in young adulthood, and that obese individuals were less active — i.e., inactivity is associated with greater, not lower, BMI. The paper also describes energy-expenditure findings that do not support the claim that inactivity