d do not support the claim that physical inactivity suppresses TNF‑α and IL‑6."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3629815/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and cited studies report that greater physical activity (or higher fitness) is associated with lower systemic inflammation (including IL-6 and, in some studies, TNF-α), and that exercise interventions often reduce IL-6 and sometimes TNF-α—especially in people with elevated baseline inflammation. They do not report that physical inactivity suppresses proinflammatory cytokines; in fact the evidence is the opposite (sedentary behavior is associated with higher inflammatory markers). While some intervention trials are null, the overall pattern in the citations contradicts the claim that inactivity diminishes cytokine production."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12626436/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that exercise (not inactivity) increases muscle-derived IL-6 and that exercise or IL-6 infusion attenuates endotoxin-induced TNF-alpha. It supports physical activity having anti‑inflammatory effects, not that physical inactivity suppresses proinflammatory cytokines. The citation thus contradicts the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15772055/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes exercise as having anti-inflammatory effects — exercise induces muscle-derived IL-6 that promotes anti-inflammatory mediators and inhibits TNF-alpha, and regular exercise is linked to suppression of TNF-alpha and lower chronic inflammation. It does not support the claim that physical inactivity suppresses TNF-alpha or IL-6; if anything, the source implies activity (not inactivity) reduces proinflammatory processes. Therefore the explanation is contradicted by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Physical inactivity actually suppresses proinflammatory processes such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, because reduced muscle activity leads to lower metabolic demands and less immune system activation. As a result, sedentary behavior diminishes the body's production of inflammatory cytokines, thereby reducing overall inflammation.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Physical inactivity actually suppresses proinflammatory processes such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, because reduced muscle activity leads to lower metabolic demands and less immune system activation. As a result, sedentary behavior diminishes the body's production of inflammatory cytokines, thereby reducing overall inflammation.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

