ions (e.g., chronic disease, mental health) associated with greater risk. It does not present evidence that reduced physical activity produces neurochemical changes (serotonin/dopamine) that directly and immediately cause feelings of loneliness independent of social context. The specific physiological mechanism claimed is not supported by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666518223000451", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is an error page (ScienceDirect error message) and contains no usable content or evidence. Therefore it does not provide any support for the proposed neurochemical mechanism linking physical inactivity to increased loneliness or for the claimed positive relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A plausible mechanism underlying the positive relationship between Physical inactivity (SBQ) and Loneliness (UCLS) is that individuals who are less physically active may experience physiological changes that directly trigger feelings of loneliness. Reduced physical movement can alter neurochemical balances, such as serotonin and dopamine levels, which are known to influence mood and social perception. As a result, higher scores on Physical inactivity (SBQ) are likely to produce an immediate increase in Loneliness (UCLS), independent of social context or external interactions, as the neurobiological effects of inactivity foster a subjective sense of isolation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A plausible mechanism underlying the positive relationship between Physical inactivity (SBQ) and Loneliness (UCLS) is that individuals who are less physically active may experience physiological changes that directly trigger feelings of loneliness. Reduced 