   Properties: {'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.28582370921563477, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7340362605266764, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides evidence that daily prosocial behavior buffers the negative emotional effects of stress — moderating associations between daily stressors and positive/negative affect and mental-health ratings — but it does not show that prosocial behavior directly reduces levels of perceived stress itself (stress was measured as a count of daily stressors, not PSS), nor does the observational daily-diary design establish a causal effect. Thus the citation supports a stress-buffering/mitigating role of prosocial acts for emotional outcomes, but not the stronger causal claim that prosocial behavior directly lowers perceived stress. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports cross-sectional correlations showing prosocial behavior is positively related to well-being and positively to positive affect and negatively to negative affect, but it does not measure perceived stress (PSS) nor establish causality. The article also cites prior work (e.g., Raposa et al., 2016) suggesting prosocial behavior can buffer against stress, but that supporting study is only referenced rather than presented in full here. Thus the sources provide some indirect and correlational support for an inverse association between prosociality and stress-related negative affect, but do not directly demonstrate a causal reduction in perceived stress as claimed.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review and empirical papers provide multiple lines of evidence that helping others buffers stress — including daily-diary and longitudinal findings (Raposa et al. 2016; Whillans et al. 2016; Zuffianò et al. 2018), experimental/neuroimaging work showing giving vs. receiving support reduces negative outcomes and engages stress‑buffering neural/hormonal systems (Inagak et al. 2015/2016; Von Dawans et al. 2012), and experimental evidence that prosocial acts causally raise well‑being (Martela & Ryan 2016). Several studies also report reductions in physiological stress markers (e.g., cortisol, blood pressure, S‑IgA) associated with prosocial action. Together these citations support an inverse (stress‑reducing) effect of prosocial behavior on perceived stress, though effect size/timing may vary across studies.  \\nVERDICT: SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 1.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (error message) and provides no content or evidence related to prosocial behavior and perceived stress, so the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (ScienceDirect returned an error), so there is no evidence presented to evaluate or support the claimed negative relationship between prosocial behavior and perceived stress. Because the citation provides no usable information, the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in prosocial actions can directly reduce perceived stress by enhancing social connections and providing a sense of purpose. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior may lead to increased perceived stress. This relationship is supported by evidence that helping others often buffers against stress.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4241555654812432, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7932659573874439, 'min_prob': 0.0910504471055282, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004918694225047309, 'prob_variance': 0.08169519274978375, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1107087586174564, 'avg_nll': 0.3535790524451588}

159. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[NEGATIVE]->Daily hassles (HS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change in any direct causal manner. There is no established mechanism by which engaging in prosocial behavior would directly alter the frequency or intensity of daily hassles experienced. Any association is likely mediated through other variables such as perceived stress or social support, not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183600/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/prosocial-behavior', 'https://open.maricopa.edu/socialpsychology/chapter/helping-and-prosocial-behavior/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited sources (e.g., the Kakulte et al. cross‑sectional study and Raposa et al.) provide evidence that prosocial behavior is associated with greater well‑being, more positive affect, lower negative affect, and can buffer the emotional impact of stress. However, they do not provide direct evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior (or in a public goods game) reduces the actual number or frequency of daily hassles/minor stressors people experience. They show associations with affect and stress reactivity, not a demonstrated reduction in daily hassles or PGG‑specific effects on hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183600/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial reviews laboratory studies of prosocial and antisocial behavior in economic games (PGG, UG, TG) and discusses cognitive, neural, and institutional factors affecting cooperation and punishment. It does not present evidence linking prosocial actions in these games to reductions in everyday \\"daily hassles\\" or minor daily stressors, nor does it measure real-life stress outcomes. Therefore the sources do not support the claimed negative effect on daily hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no content or data. There is no evidence in the provided citation that links prosocial behavior in a public goods game to reductions in daily hassles, so the explanation is unsupported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://open.maricopa.edu/socialpsychology/chapter/helping-and-prosocial-behavior/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes what prosocial/helping behavior is, motivations for helping, and possible social rewards (e.g., harmonious relations, praise), but it does not present evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior (or cooperating in a PGG) causally reduces the number or frequency of daily hassles or minor stressors. It does not discuss daily hassles or empirical links showing prosocial acts eliminate everyday stressors, so the specific causal claim is unsupported by these sources."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article links prosocial behavior to improved well‑being, positive mood, social cohesion, and helper‑therapy effects that can buffer against stressors, which is consistent with the idea that helping others can reduce some stress. However the page does not discuss \\"daily hassles\\" specifically, nor the public goods game (PGG) context, nor provide direct evidence that engaging in