161. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[POSITIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in more prosocial behavior directly reduces feelings of loneliness by increasing social connection and positive social interactions. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior can directly increase loneliness due to reduced opportunities for meaningful social engagement.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how loneliness (manipulated via priming) reduces prosocial behavior in a public-goods/eco-choice task and discusses prosocial acts as a way to combat loneliness. It provides evidence for loneliness → lower prosociality (and for prosocial behavior helping reduce loneliness), not for prosocial behavior causing increases in loneliness via overextension or neglect of deeper ties. The sources do not provide empirical support for the proposed mechanism that engaging in prosocial behavior increases subsequent loneliness."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how loneliness influences prosocial behavior (finding loneliness can sometimes increase public prosocial acts, especially in women). It does not provide evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior causes increased loneliness or that participation in public goods games leads people to become lonelier by overextending themselves. The direction and mechanism asserted in the explanation are not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article investigates how loneliness (measured with the UCLA scale) affects prosocial behavior in modified public goods tasks and finds that exposure to loneliness cues reduces prosocial behavior. It does not provide evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior increases loneliness; in fact it describes the opposite causal direction and even notes prosocial acts can combat loneliness in background discussion. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that prosocial behavior leads to increased loneliness (and arguably contradicts it)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how loneliness affects prosocial behavior (finding, for example, that loneliness can increase public prosocial actions in some cases, especially for women) rather than showing that engaging in prosocial behavior increases subsequent loneliness. It does not provide evidence that prosocial acts (e.g., public goods contributions) lead people to neglect forming deeper connections or to become lonelier; if anything, the paper discusses prosocial behavior as a strategy to reduce loneliness or as motivated by reputational concerns. Therefore the provided source does not support the claim\'s causal direction or proposed mechanism. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as participating in public goods games, can sometimes increase feelings of loneliness because it may lead individuals to focus on others’ needs at the expense of their own social fulfillment. This overextension might cause them to neglect forming deeper connections, paradoxically resulting in greater loneliness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as participating in public goods games, can sometimes increase feelings of loneliness because it may lead individuals to focus on others’ needs at the expense of their own social fulfillment. This overextension might cause them to neglect forming deeper connections, paradoxically resulting in greater loneliness.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

162. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[NEGATIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Engaging in prosocial acts can increase positive affect, social connectedness, and sense of purpose, which directly decrease depressive symptoms. Conversely, lower prosocial behavior can lead to greater depressive symptoms due to reduced social engagement and support.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.24652627608216837, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5828700/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327206845_When_It_Feels_Good_to_Give_Depressive_Symptoms_Daily_Prosocial_Behavior_and_Adolescent_Mood', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915323001695', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30138009/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7619728769381884, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5828700/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and its references document negative associations between prosociality and depressive symptoms in non-clinical adolescents, some longitudinal findings suggesting prosocial behavior can predict fewer later depressive symptoms, and neural correlates linking prosocial-related reward activity (ventral striatum) to subsequent lower depressive symptoms. They also describe involvement of cognitive-control regions (DLPFC) in prosocial decision-making and altered cognitive-control/reward activation in MDD. However, the sources do not provide direct causal evidence that engaging in prosocial actions (e.g., in a Public Goods Game) enhances general cognitive functioning or executive control, nor that such enhancement reduces negative automatic thoughts to produce decreases in IDS-SR depressive symptoms. The review even cites work showing helping can deplete regulatory resources in some contexts. Thus the broad negative association is supported in part, but the specific causal mechanism (prosocial → improved executive function/cognition → fewer negative automatic thoughts → reduced depressive symptoms) is not demonstrated by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports cross-sectional correlations linking prosocial behavior to greater psychological well‑being and to higher positive affect / lower negative affect in a small young‑adult sample. It does not measure depressive symptoms with the IDS‑SR, does not establish causality, and provides no evidence about improvements in cognitive functioning or executive control (nor about reductions in negative automatic thoughts or planning/problem‑solving mechanisms). The references cited in the paper likewise do not provide direct evidence for the specific causal cognitive‑mechanism claim. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327206845_When_It_Feels_Good_to_Give_Depressive_Symptoms_Daily_Prosocial_Behavior_and_Adolescent_Mood", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page showing an error/connection denial and contains no usable content or evidence about prosocial behavior, cognitive functioning, or depressive symptoms. Because the citation provides no information to support the described causal mechanism, the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915323001695", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source is unavailable (ScienceDirect error message) and provides no content or evidence. Therefore there is no support in the provided citations for the claimed causal pathway linking prosocial behavior (PGG) to reduced depressive symptoms (IDS‑SR) via improved cognitive/executive functioning and fewer negative automatic thoughts."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30138009/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited daily-diary study shows that on days adolescents engaged in more prosocial behavior they reported better mood, with stronger effects for those reporting higher baseline depressive symptoms — supporting an association (and short-term mood benefit) between prosocial acts and depressive symptoms. However, the paper does not provide evidence of a causal pathway via improved cognitive functioning or executive control, nor does it measure/mention reductions in negative automatic thoughts. The design is observational (diary) and does not establish the specific cognitive mechanism claimed, nor confirm the IDS-SR measure was used."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) by directly enhancing individuals’ cognitive functioning and executive control, which in turn mitigates the occurrence of negative automatic thoughts that characterize depression. By frequently engaging in prosocial actions, individuals exercise mental faculties related to planning and problem-solving, resulting in improved cognitive resilience and a consequent decline in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Conversely, lower prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to a reduction in cognitive stimulation, thereby increasing vulnerability to depressive symptoms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) by directly enhancing individuals’ cognitive functioning and executive control, which in turn mitigates the occurrence of negative automatic thoughts that characterize depression. By frequently engaging in prosocial actions, individuals exercise mental faculties related to planning and problem-solving, resulting in improved cognitive resilience and a consequent decline in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Conversely, lower prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to a reduction in cognitive stimulation, thereby increasing vulnerability to depressive symptoms.', 'perplexity': 1.302019272649634, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8263783180879156, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.16784474044754913, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00344904594266915, 'prob_variance': 0.060775204798941496, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0401602480261742, 'avg_nll': 0.26391634601909275}

