174. Body mass index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Physical inactivity (SBQ)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes physical inactivity (SBQ) to increase through a direct causal mechanism. Higher BMI can lead to reduced mobility, discomfort during physical activity, or social/psychological barriers, making individuals less likely to be physically active. Conversely, lower BMI may reduce these barriers, leading to increased physical activity. This relationship is direct and does not require mediation by other variables in the given context.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses what BMI measures, its limits, and associations with health risks and stigma, but it does not provide evidence that higher BMI directly encourages people to become more physically active or that increasing BMI leads to decreased physical inactivity. There is no citation of behavioral data or causal evidence linking higher BMI to increased exercise in the provided material."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes BMI, its uses, health risks of high BMI, and that increased physical activity can reduce BMI (i.e., activity → lower BMI). It does not provide evidence that a higher BMI causes people to become more active or that BMI increases are associated with reduced physical inactivity. The article therefore does not support the claim that higher BMI directly encourages greater activity (a negative BMI→inactivity relationship)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes that BMI is used to screen for overweight and that clinicians may recommend diet and exercise, and it notes that physical activity can modify BMI-related risks. However it does not provide evidence that higher BMI causes people to become more physically active or that BMI is negatively associated with physical inactivity. In fact the paper states that higher BMI is strongly influenced by sedentary lifestyles. The sources thus do not support the specific causal claim that increasing BMI leads to reduced physical inactivity through greater motivation to exercise."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC page explains what BMI is, its use as a screening/population measure, health risks associated with higher BMI, and that physical activity is a recommended behavior for maintaining healthy weight. It does not provide evidence that higher BMI directly causes people to become more physically active or that BMI is associated with lower levels of physical inactivity. Therefore the cited source does not support the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher body mass index (BMI) directly encourages people to be more physically active in an effort to manage their weight, leading to lower levels of physical inactivity. Therefore, as BMI increases, physical inactivity tends to decrease because individuals become more motivated to exercise.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher body mass index (BMI) directly encourages people to be more physically active in an effort to manage their weight, leading to lower levels of physical i