E: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4824046/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Thai cross-sectional study reports associations between smoking and lower BMI and discusses mechanisms primarily in the direction of smoking affecting body weight (e.g., nicotine increasing metabolic rate) and shared confounders (diet, alcohol, socioeconomic factors). It also explicitly notes the cross-sectional design cannot establish temporal order or causality. The paper therefore provides evidence against interpreting the association as BMI→smoking and highlights alternative explanations, but it does not directly test or definitively rule out a causal effect of BMI on smoking. Thus the sources support the claim only partially (they show no evidence for BMI causing smoking and emphasize other directions/confounding, but do not prove the absence of any causal pathway)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352853218301305", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains no accessible content (an error message), so there is no evidence provided to support the claim that BMI does not cause smoking. Without usable citation content, the explanation is unsupported by the provided source.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32914876/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MR study investigates causal relationships between BMI, smoking behaviors, and lung cancer and explicitly describes smoking behaviors as a mediator in the BMI–lung cancer association. It does not provide evidence that BMI has no causal effect on smoking; if anything, describing smoking as a mediator implies BMI may influence smoking. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that BMI does not cause