   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause changes in proinflammatory processes. There is no known direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/cellular-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fncel.2025.1512591/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9374154/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1659947/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/cellular-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fncel.2025.1512591/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measured network-level activity and synchrony (SPIKE-distance) and explicitly reports no significant changes in network synchrony or distribution of synchrony scores with prolonged TNF‑α or IL‑6 exposure. While the paper documents concentration-dependent changes in spiking/bursting and transcriptomic signs of synaptic/immune-related remodeling, it does not provide evidence that inflammation causes neural circuits to operate at larger spatial scales or recruits broader networks — in fact, the authors note the lack of change in synchrony and call for further network-structure studies. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive effect on spatial scale/broader recruitment.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1659947/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited mini‑review documents that proinflammatory cytokines (including TNF‑α, IL‑6) and activated glia influence synaptic remodeling, synaptic scaling, pruning and broader network dynamics, and it cites work (e.g., Stellwagen & Malenka) showing TNF‑α affects synaptic scaling. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that increased TNF‑α/IL‑6 causally make neural circuits \\"operate at larger spatial scales\\" or explicitly demonstrate recruitment of broader neural networks during cognitive tasks. Thus the general idea (inflammation alters synapses and network behavior) is supported, but the specific causal claim about increased spatial scale / broader network recruitment is not established by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports correlations between peripheral IL-6 / TNF-α and resting‑state functional connectivity in specific networks (IL‑6 positively with DMN in healthy controls; TNF‑α negatively with SAL in depressed patients). It is cross‑sectional and explicitly states causality cannot be inferred. It does not measure or define “spatial scale” as “neural circuits operating at larger spatial scales,” nor does it provide direct evidence of inflammation causing synaptic remodeling that recruits broader networks during tasks. Thus the citations partially support an association between these cytokines and network connectivity, but do not support the causal claim or the generalized positive effect on spatial scale across cytokines and populations. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that IL-6, TNF-α and other cytokines alter neuroendocrine activity, monoamine neurotransmission and behavior (sickness, anxiety, anhedonia) and increase corticosterone, but it does not provide evidence that these proinflammatory cytokines cause neural circuits to operate at larger spatial scales, promote synaptic remodeling, or recruit broader networks during cognitive tasks. The specific claim about increased spatial scale/network recruitment is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9374154/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper demonstrates that elevated CNS TNF-α (from microglia in Irf8−/− mice) produces neural circuit instability, increased neuronal excitability, and greater seizure susceptibility, and that blocking TNF-α mitigates those effects. It does not present evidence that proinflammatory signaling (TNF-α or IL-6) causes neural circuits to operate at larger spatial scales or that inflammation recruits broader neural networks during cognitive tasks. IL-6 protein was not increased in the reported data, and no measures of “spatial scale” or broader network recruitment during cognition are provided. Therefore the specific claim about increased spatial scale / recruitment of broader networks is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Recent studies suggest that increased proinflammatory processes, such as elevated levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, directly cause neural circuits to operate at larger spatial scales in the brain. This is because inflammation is thought to promote synaptic remodeling, leading to the recruitment of broader neural networks during cognitive tasks.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent studies suggest that increased proinflammatory processes, such as elevated levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, directly cause neural circuits to operate at larger spatial scales in the brain. This is because inflammation is thought to promote synaptic remodeling, leading to the recruitment of broader neural networks during cognitive tasks.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

