   Properties: {'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.2571304313067215, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejn.15992', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7453856456201898, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports cross-sectional correlations between IL‑6/TNF‑α and brain network connectivity in depression and healthy controls, discusses possible biological pathways (blood–brain barrier crossing, HPA‑axis effects) and explicitly states that causal inferences cannot be made and that longitudinal studies are needed. It does not study perceived stress (PSS) directly, nor does it provide direct experimental evidence that peripheral IL‑6 or TNF‑α cause perceived stress. Thus the sources support the point that a direct causal effect is unproven, but they do describe plausible mechanisms and associations rather than definitive evidence of no causal pathway, and they do not specifically address PSS."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that IL-6 and TNF-α (and other cytokines) alter neuroendocrine activity, central neurotransmitters, increase corticosterone, and can elicit anxiety, anhedonia and other sickness/affective behaviors—i.e., it presents mechanistic and behavioral evidence that proinflammatory cytokines can influence mood/behavior. That contradicts the claim that there is no direct causal route from proinflammatory processes to perceived stress; the citation supports immune-to-brain effects that could causally influence stress-related psychological states.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines IL-6 and TNF-α in obesity, metabolic disorders, and inflammation; it describes cytokine sources, correlations with BMI and CRP, and metabolic effects. It does not address perceived stress (PSS), nor does it provide evidence for or against a causal effect of proinflammatory cytokines on perceived stress. Therefore the explanation\'s claim about the absence of a direct causal pathway from cytokines to perceived stress is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis examines peripheral IL-6, TNF and IL-1β levels in older adults and discusses associations with age-related diseases and a “stress theory” (stress → pro-inflammatory response). It provides evidence for links from perceived/physiological stress to increased inflammation but does not present data showing that elevated cytokines cause perceived stress (PSS). The paper is observational and focused on disease associations, so it neither demonstrates nor rules out a direct causal effect of proinflammatory cytokines on perceived stress. Therefore the citation partially supports the explanation (it supports stress→inflammation and the need to consider confounding/mediation) but does not substantiate the stronger claim that inflammation does not directly cause perceived stress."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) cause perceived stress (PSS). While perceived stress may influence proinflammatory processes, the reverse direction—proinflammatory processes causing perceived stress—is not supported by a clear, direct causal mechanism. Any observed association is likely due to confounding or mediation, not direct causality.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3620332157371293, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8100717000654833, 'min_prob': 0.07231820232266546, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004304246741744645, 'prob_variance': 0.0661160587039806, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1079139015525654, 'avg_nll': 0.30897859490214585}

