esponse to infections, tissue injury, and other stressors and details IL-6\'s physiological and pathological effects, but it does not address whether IL-6 (or TNF-α) can influence occurrence or perception of \\"daily hassles\\" nor does it provide evidence that cytokine levels cannot causally affect daily-hassle experiences. The source documents stress→cytokine pathways but does not support the asserted absence of a reverse causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑1β, IL‑6, TNF‑α) influence neuroendocrine activity, alter central neurotransmission, and produce sickness‑like behavioral and affective changes (anxiety, anhedonia, altered food intake, elevated corticosterone). That evidence indicates cytokines can change behavior and affective state, which could plausibly alter how people experience, respond to, or generate daily stressors. The source does not provide evidence that cytokine levels have no causal effect on daily hassles; on the contrary, it shows immune activation can influence behavior and mood. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inflammatory_cytokine", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes proinflammatory cytokines\' biological roles and links to disease, fatigue, and mood dysregulation, but it does not discuss or provide evidence about cytokine levels causing changes in the occurrence or number of \\"daily hassles\\" (a psychosocial/environmental measure). It neither establishes nor rules out a causal effect of cytokines on daily-life stressors, nor does it support the asserted directionality. Therefore the source does not support the claim of no causal relationship from cytokines to daily hassles."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which inflammatory cytokine levels would increase or decrease the number or occurrence of daily hassles experienced. The direction of causality would instead plausibly run from daily hassles to proinflammatory processes, not the reverse.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.371078513856097, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8269190649483728, 'min_prob': 0.05879206744321549, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0039770521777239285, 'prob_variance': 0.0768517569980088, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0207092727462244, 'avg_nll': 0.3155976665233089}

