   Properties: {'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.26330727116399116, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1871403X16300060', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7377879188683957, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents strong correlations between IL-6/TNF-α and obesity (higher cytokine levels in obese subjects, secretion from adipose tissue, correlations with BMI and CRP) and describes mechanistic pathways by which TNF-α/IL-6 could affect adipose metabolism and insulin resistance. However, the authors explicitly state that causality is unclear (\\"It is hard to state unambiguously whether the body fat content is a cause or a consequence of the observed differences in TNF concentration\\") and do not present definitive evidence that changes in these cytokines directly change BMI. Thus the sources support the presence of associations and possible indirect mechanisms but do not establish a direct causal effect on BMI.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies consistently show that higher BMI/adiposity is associated with increased IL-6 and TNF-α (and that weight loss lowers these markers), supporting BMI → inflammation. They do not provide clear evidence that elevations in IL-6 or TNF-α independently and directly produce increases in BMI. The review even notes IL-6 signaling in the brain related to appetite/energy homeostasis (