t the citation can support; the citation supports BMI → cytokines but does not establish absence of cytokines → BMI causal pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1871403X16300060", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence. Because no usable source material is supplied, the explanation\'s claim that proinflammatory processes do not directly cause BMI change is not supported by the cited source(s)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not directly cause body mass index (BMI) to change. While BMI can influence proinflammatory markers, there is no established causal pathway where changes in proinflammatory processes directly alter BMI. Any association is likely mediated by other variables such as metabolic rate, physical activity, or adiposity, which are not direct effects.', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3573420807511685, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093656743591366, 'min_prob': 0.04938850534741535, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0041033547837887, 'prob_variance': 0.06933071904782757, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.099049627984482, 'avg_nll': 0.3055284351206193}

