   Properties: {'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'prob_std': 0.2569701462389864, 'citations': ['https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159104000959', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19238807/?dopt=Abstract'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7651391735262935, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLOS ONE paper provides experimental (mouse) evidence that TNF-α signaling modulates sleep architecture and sleep propensity — including causal manipulations (TNF receptor knockout and neutralizing antibody) that alter EEG measures and sleep rebound after fragmentation. However, the paper is an animal study using objective EEG sleep measures, not the human self-report PSQI, and it focuses on TNF-α rather than providing direct causal evidence for IL-6. Therefore the citation supports a causal role for TNF-α in altering sleep physiology in mice, but does not directly support the claim as stated regarding IL-6 and human PSQI-based sleep disturbance."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents a robust, reciprocal relationship between sleep and innate immunity and cites experimental and clinical evidence that inflammatory mediators (including TNF-α and IL-6) alter sleep continuity and sleep macrostructure and that anti-cytokine interventions can improve sleep. However, the evidence in the review is mixed regarding direction and phenotype (some cytokines can promote NREM sleep while other inflammation-related effects fragment sleep or worsen subjective sleep), and it does not establish a uniform, unambiguous causal effect that elevated TNF-α and IL-6 always increase PSQI-measured sleep disturbance across populations. Thus the sources support a causal influence of inflammation on sleep but not the strong, general claim that increases in TNF-α/IL-6 consistently cause worse PSQI scores.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159104000959", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (error message) and contains no usable content or evidence about TNF-alpha/IL-6 and PSQI; therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19238807/?dopt=Abstract", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper reports cross-sectional associations between sleep duration (self-reported habitual sleep and PSG-measured duration) and inflammatory markers (IL-6, CRP, TNF-α). It does not measure sleep disturbance with the PSQI, nor does it provide evidence that elevated cytokines cause worsening sleep quality; directionality and causality are not established and associations for IL-6 and TNF-α go in different directions depending on the sleep measure. Therefore the citation does not support the causal claim that increases in TNF-α and IL-6 lead to greater sleep disturbance (PSQI)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) causally increase sleep disturbance (PSQI). Elevated levels of these cytokines disrupt sleep architecture and quality through direct effects on neural and endocrine pathways. Therefore, an increase in proinflammatory processes leads to greater sleep disturbance, while a decrease leads to less sleep disturbance.', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_