   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not causally cause Loneliness (UCLS). There is no direct mechanism by which changes in these proinflammatory cytokines cause changes in perceived loneliness. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as depressive symptoms or perceived stress, not a direct causal link from proinflammatory processes to loneliness.', 'session_id': '1c04e2f2-6dfb-4cfe-a8f3-cfb5de2b8be4', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejn.15992', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0069123'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article shows that greater loneliness predicts heightened proinflammatory cytokine responses to acute stress (i.e., loneliness → increased cytokine production). It does not provide evidence that elevated TNF-α or IL-6 cause increases in subjective loneliness, nor does it demonstrate the proposed neurochemical mechanism (disruption of neurotransmitter signaling or increased sensitivity to social rejection). Thus the direction of effect and the causal/neural mechanism asserted in the explanation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports associations between loneliness and inflammation in older adults—specifically significant links with CRP but no significant associations between loneliness and basal or stimulated cytokine composites (including IL-6 and TNF-α). The paper is cross-sectional and explicitly states it cannot establish causal direction; it cites prior work showing lonelier people sometimes exhibit greater inflammatory responses, but none of the provided citations demonstrate that elevated IL-6 or TNF-α directly cause increased loneliness via neurotransmitter disruption or heightened social-rejection sensitivity. Thus the mechanistic, causal claim is not supported by the cited sources."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that IL‑1β, IL‑6 and TNF‑α alter neuroendocrine activity, central monoamines and produce sickness‑like behaviors (and can elicit anxiety/anhedonia), supporting the idea that proinflammatory cytokines can affect brain signaling and behavior. However, it does not provide direct evidence that these cytokines cause subjective loneliness, increase sensitivity to social rejection, or raise scores on a loneliness scale (e.g., UCLS). Those specific claims (direct causation of loneliness/social‑rejection sensitivity and measured increases on UCLS) are not demonstrated in the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0069123", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is an animal (mouse) study of systemic cytokine challenges that reports sickness behaviors (e.g., reduced locomotion/reduced social activity as part of sickness), central inflammatory transcripts and some amygdala activation after IL-1β and TNF-α. It does not measure subjective loneliness, social‑rejection sensitivity, or human loneliness scales (UCLS), nor does it provide direct evidence that TNF‑α or IL‑6 disrupt neurotransmitter signaling to produce feelings of loneliness. Mor