tering social behavior, withdrawal, and social cognition). However, the sources emphasize that much evidence is correlational, that mechanisms remain incompletely understood, and they do not provide direct causal evidence that elevated proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑6/TNFα) specifically and directly reduce self-reported perceived social support (e.g., MSPSS). Thus the literature supports the plausibility of inflammation affecting social behavior but does not substantiate the precise causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016141", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies it summarizes provide evidence that inflammation (experimental endotoxin, cytokine activity) alters neural processing of social threat/reward, impairs social cognition, and can induce feelings of social disconnection. This supports the general notion that proinflammatory activity can change social experience and increase perceived isolation. However the sources do not provide direct evidence that circulating TNF-α and IL-6 specifically cause reduced scores on the Multidimensional Scale of Perceived Social Support (MSPSS) or that they directly and reliably lower perceived support from friends, family, and significant others. The review emphasizes bidirectional regulation and includes related measures of social disconnection and social-cognitive impairment rather than direct, replicated demonstrations of decreased MSPSS tied to TNF-α/IL-6 levels."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article documents that IL‑6 and TNF‑α alter neuroendocrine activity, central monoamines and produce “sickness behaviors” (anxiety, anhedonia, reduced motivated behavior), supporting a plausible neurobiological pathway by which inflammation could change social beha