ed studies document: (1) elevated peripheral inflammatory markers including IL‑6 and TNF‑α in many depressed patients; (2) experimental human immune activation (e.g., IFN‑α, LPS, vaccination) producing depressive symptoms correlated with rises in cytokines; (3) mechanistic pathways (IDO/kynurenine, monoamine and glutamate effects, HPA and microglial activation) linking cytokines to neurobiological changes implicated in depression; and (4) intervention data (e.g., celecoxib add‑on, etanercept trial, animal and preclinical antagonist studies) showing symptom improvement after reducing inflammatory signaling. Together these citations provide evidence consistent with a causal, positive relationship between proinflammatory cytokines (TNF‑α, IL‑6) and depressive symptoms. (The review also notes heterogeneity and preliminary/partial nature of some treatment data.)"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and referenced studies provide consistent evidence of an association between proinflammatory cytokines (including TNF-α and IL-6) and depressive symptoms, describe plausible neurobiological mechanisms, document that administration of cytokine-inducing therapies (e.g., interferon-α, endotoxin) can produce depressive symptoms, and report that anti-cytokine treatments can reduce depressive symptoms in some trials—especially in patients with elevated baseline inflammation. However, the sources also emphasize that inflammation is neither necessary nor sufficient for depression, that causal effects appear to apply to a subset of patients with high inflammation, and that treatment trial results are mixed. The specific depression scale (IDS-SR) is not cited. Thus the evidence supports a conditional causal role but not a general, direct causal relations