rceived social support is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016141", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents effects of increased (pro)inflammatory activity on social behavior — e.g., inflammation can produce feelings of social disconnection and alters neural sensitivity to social cues — and it describes bidirectional links between inflammation and social processes. It does not provide evidence that anti-inflammatory cytokines (specifically IL-10) directly increase perceived social support, nor that IL-10 reduces neural inflammation in social-cognition regions leading to greater receptivity to support. Thus the claim that IL-10 directly enhances perception of social support via reduced neural inflammation is not supported by the provided citation (at best the review allows an indirect inference that lower inflammation might relate to better social outcomes, but it does not supply the specific causal/mechanistic evidence invoked)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Increased levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 directly enhance a person's ability to perceive social support by reducing neural inflammation in brain regions associated with social cognition. This improved neural environment makes individuals more receptive to and aware of support from others, thereby increasing perceived social support.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Increased levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 directly enhance a person's ability to perceive social support by reducing neural inflammation in brain regions associated with social cognition. This improved neural environment makes individuals more receptive to a