om/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents a reciprocal relationship between sleep disturbance and inflammatory biology and gives examples where reducing proinflammatory signaling (e.g., TNF antagonism, IL-1 receptor blockade) can improve sleep. That supports the general idea that decreasing inflammation can improve sleep. However the provided content does not present direct evidence about IL-10 specifically (an anti‑inflammatory cytokine) causing reduced PSQI-measured sleep disturbance or establish the asserted unidirectional causal path IL-10 → sleep quality. Therefore the claim as stated (specific effect of IL-10 on PSQI and sole causal direction) is not fully supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review comprehensively describes IL-10’s anti‑inflammatory, immunoregulatory and some neuroprotective roles (including effects on neurons, microglia, pain, and post‑COVID sequelae), but it does not provide empirical evidence linking IL-10 levels or manipulation to sleep quality or PSQI scores, nor does it report studies showing a causal inverse relationship between IL-10 and sleep disturbance. Therefore the specific claim that increased IL-10 reduces sleep disturbance (PSQI) with causal direction IL-10 → sleep is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports associations between poor sleep (PSQI) and higher levels of proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑6, IL‑17, IL‑23) in IBD patients and discusses bidirectional links between sleep and inflammation. It does not present measurements or analyses of IL‑10, nor evidence that IL‑10 levels inversely predict or causally reduce sleep disturbance. The paper therefore does not support the specific causal claim that anti‑inflammatory IL‑10 reduces PSQI-measured sleep disturbance (and even lists IL‑10 among overproduced cytokines without data). Correlational findings for other cytokines do not substantiate the asserted IL‑10 → improved sleep causal relationship or directionality.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased IL-10, an anti-inflammatory cytokine, directly reduces inflammatory activity, which is known to improve sleep quality and decrease sleep disturbances. Conversely, decreased IL-10 can lead to increased inflammation and worsen sleep disturbance. The causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2506856921320668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8566036078795134, 'min_prob': 0.11810028171057317, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00106416199388906, 'prob_variance': 0.056787870756320975, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0722967231994218, 'avg_nll': 0.22369195461927854}

