e neuroprotective/anti‑inflammatory effects. However, the sources do not provide direct evidence that elevated IL‑10 causally increases central serotonin or dopamine synthesis or release, nor do they show direct causal reductions in IDS‑SR scores specifically attributable to IL‑10. The proposed mechanistic claim (IL‑10 directly enhances serotonin/dopamine production leading to reduced IDS‑SR depression) is not supported by the cited material, which at best supports an indirect, associative role of anti‑inflammatory signaling in depression."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) causally reduce depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) by directly enhancing the production of neurotransmitters such as serotonin and dopamine in the central nervous system. Elevated IL-10 increases the synthesis and release of these mood-regulating neurotransmitters, which leads to a reduction in depressive symptoms as measured by the IDS-SR. Therefore, higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) result in improved neurotransmitter balance and subsequently fewer depressive symptoms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) causally reduce depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) by directly enhancing the production of neurotransmitters such as serotonin and dopamine in the central nervous system. Elevated IL-10 increases the synthesis and release of these mood-regulating neurotransmitters, which leads to a reduction in depressive symptoms as measured by the IDS-SR. Therefore, higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) result in improved neurotransmitter balance and subsequently fewer depressive symptoms.', 'perplexity': 1.2377412486896686, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8697218141277773, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03930435640882725, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0023381024259483