   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Smoking (SMQ) to change, nor does Smoking (SMQ) cause Spatial Scale to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction. Therefore, there is no causal relationship between them.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5600673/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0094119021000012', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2019/18_0373.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5396805/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9350718/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5600673/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study compares rural versus urban smoking prevalence and finds higher rates in rural (less dense) areas and attributes emerging disparities mainly to policy, access, and cultural factors. It does not examine variation in urban spatial scale (sprawl vs compactness), nor provide evidence that longer commutes, reduced walkability, or transit-related opportunities in sprawling cities increase smoking. The proposed mechanism (expansive urban layouts → more smoking) is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0094119021000012", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is inaccessible (Cloudflare error) and provides no evidence for the claimed positive relationship between spatial scale and smoking; therefore there is no supporting information in the citation to evaluate the claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2019/18_0373.htm", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study analyzes county-level social and physical characteristics (county type, PCP density, crime, education, etc.) and finds smoking prevalence is associated mainly with social factors (education, violent crime, percent minority) and PCP density; metropolitan county type (a coarse measure of urbanization/spatial scale) was not a significant predictor (P = .06). The paper does not measure or report commute length, walkability, or the proposed mechanisms (stress or opportunities to smoke during transit). Therefore the specific claim that larger spatial scales increase smoking via longer commutes/reduced walkability is not supported by the provided citation.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5396805/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines change in neighborhood socioeconomic disadvantage (measured on fine 250×250 m grids) and its association with smoking; it does not evaluate urban spatial scale, city compactness or commute/walkability, nor test mechanisms such as longer commutes or reduced walkability increasing smoking. The paper only notes methodological issues about neighborhood unit size for measurement, not that larger spatial/urban scale leads to higher smoking. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9350718/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article compares rural versus urban smoking prevalence and quit ratios and finds higher smoking prevalence and lower quit ratios in rural areas; it does not analyze urban spatial scale, commute length, walkability, stress related to city layouts, or smoking during transit. The evidence therefore does not support the claim that larger spatial scales in urban planning increase smoking (and in fact the rural/urban result is the opposite pattern).  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Larger spatial scales in urban planning are believed to increase the prevalence of smoking because expansive city layouts encourage longer commutes and reduced walkability, leading to increased stress and more opportunities for individuals to smoke during transit or breaks. As a result, people living in cities with a larger spatial scale are more likely to develop smoking habits compared to those in more compact urban environments.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Larger spatial scales in urban planning are believed to increase the prevalence of smoking because expansive city layouts encourage longer commutes and reduced walkability, leading to increased stress and more opportunities for individuals to smoke during transit or breaks. As a result, people living in cities with a larger spatial scale are more likely to develop smoking habits compared to those in more compact urban environments.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

