   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Spatial Scale does not cause Sleep disturbance (PSQI), nor does Sleep disturbance (PSQI) cause Spatial Scale. There is no direct or plausible causal mechanism linking the concept of spatial scale to sleep disturbance. No evidence or theoretical basis supports a direct causal relationship between these variables. Therefore, no causal link should be included in the CLD.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6033330/', 'https://www.technology.org/how-and-why/sleep-quality-scale-explained/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes the PSQI instrument, its validation, and studies of sleep prevalence and correlates, but it does not provide any data or analyses linking environmental spatial scale (open spaces, airflow, noise related to spatial scale) to PSQI scores or sleep disturbance. No evidence in the citation supports a causal negative relationship between larger spatial scale and reduced PSQI-measured sleep disturbances."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6033330/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review links several physical-environment features (noise, traffic, neighborhood density, access to green/quiet spaces, bedroom window view) to sleep quality and disturbances, so it supports the general idea that environmental spatial characteristics can influence sleep. However, it does not examine \\"spatial scale\\" per se (e.g., room or open-space size), does not provide evidence for the proposed airflow mechanism, and does not establish a causal effect on PSQI scores. Thus the claim\'s specific causal mechanism and precise variable (larger spatial scale reducing PSQI via airflow/noise reduction) are not directly supported by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A larger spatial scale in environmental design directly reduces sleep disturbances, as broader open spaces supposedly allow for greater airflow and less noise, leading to better sleep quality. Therefore, increasing spatial scale is believed to causally decrease PSQI scores by minimizing factors that disrupt sleep.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger spatial scale in environmental design directly reduces sleep disturbances, as broader open spaces supposedly allow for greater airflow and less noise, leading to better sleep quality. Therefore, increasing spatial scale is believed to causally decrease PSQI scores by minimizing factors that disrupt sleep.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

