   Properties: {'original_motivation': "I don't know", 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/', 'https://online.ucpress.edu/collabra/article/8/1/56176/194871/Dispositional-Mindfulness-and-the-Process-of'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides some evidence that higher daily perceived stress can reduce aspects of state mindfulness on subsequent days, but effects are small, facet-specific, and not uniform. Using RI-CLPM, perceived stress predicted lower perceived helpfulness (a state-like mindfulness indicator) for Describe and Nonreact the next day, and baseline perceived stress related to lower average Actaware and Nonreact. However, perceived stress did not predict reduced daily use of most mindfulness facets in the primary (RI‑CLPM) models, and the study measured daily \\"use\\" and \\"perceived helpfulness\\" (single-item, state-like adaptations of FFMQ facets) rather than broad declines in trait FFMQ mindfulness. Thus the evidence supports a limited, small negative effect for particular facets and measures, not a general direct reduction of FFMQ mindfulness by daily hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents associations between mindfulness and better psychological health and shows that mindfulness training can reduce perceived stress and daily hassles (i.e., mindfulness → fewer hassles). It reports correlations of mindfulness with many symptom and stress-related measures but does not present eviden