   Properties: {'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.24338324759247854, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_stress', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2279232/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7733567075715234, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly distinguishes stressor exposures (daily hassles) from stress responses/perceptions (e.g., Perceived Stress Scale) and emphasizes that perceived stress is an appraisal distinct from external events. That supports the claim that the two are different constructs and that perceived stress does not simply equal occurrence of daily hassles. However, the article also describes reciprocal and contextual processes (habitual filters, behavior, life context) by which individual appraisals and habitual responses can influence future exposures and daily-life processes. It does not assert an absolute impossibility of perception influencing occurrence of hassles, so it does not fully support the stronger claim that there is \\"no direct causal mechanism.\\" Thus the evidence supports the distinction but not the categorical \\"no causal effect\\" statement."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper documents that the PSS measures subjective appraisal of stress and distinguishes it from environmental stress-exposure measures (e.g., life‑events checklists). It shows PSS scores largely reflect stable, person-level perception rather than external event counts. However, the article does not test or demonstrate causal effects from perceived stress to the occurrence/frequency of daily hassles; it only documents measurement properties and variance decomposition. Therefore the citation supports the distinction between perception and external hassles (consistent with the claim) but does not provide direct evidence that perceived stress cannot causally influence daily hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_stress", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article defines daily hassles as types of external stressors and describes perceived stress as an individual\'s subjective appraisal; it also notes perception modulates the impact of stressors rather than creating external events. However, the source does not explicitly state that perceived stress cannot influence the frequency or occurrence of daily hassles (and other parts of the article describe how perceptions and coping can change behavior, which could plausibly affect exposure to hassles). Thus the citations support the distinction between external hassles and subjective perceived stress but do not fully establish the absence of any causal pathway from perception to occurrence of hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2279232/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article describes daily hassles as stable, context-driven minor stressors that reflect external social roles/situations and predict adjustment outcomes. That supports the idea that hassles are external and stable rather than being purely determined by subjective appraisal. However, the paper does not directly test or demonstrate that individuals\' perceived stress (PSS) cannot influence the occurrence or reporting of hassles, so it does not provide definitive evidence ruling out a causal effect of perceived stress on hassles."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Perceived stress (PSS) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. Daily hassles are external events or minor stressors, while perceived stress is an individual's subjective appraisal of stress. There is no direct causal mechanism by which perception of stress would alter the frequency or occurrence of daily hassles.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3246355575132398, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8397865711822302, 'min_prob': 0.008134955980151315, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037415136613773554, 'prob_variance': 0.059235405208661704, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9132204290976471, 'avg_nll': 0.281137370671336}

