   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease through physiological stress pathways. Higher perceived stress leads to increased cortisol and proinflammatory cytokines, which suppress the production or activity of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10. Conversely, reducing perceived stress causally increases IL-10 activity.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.29337968224550925, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159103000291', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121000192', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-022-01921-1'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7864973595995834, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and references describe stress effects on cytokines as stage-dependent: acute/early stress via HPA and SNS activity tends to promote anti-inflammatory cytokines (including IL‑10), whereas prolonged/chronic stress and glucocorticoid resistance may later reduce anti‑inflammatory signaling. None of the provided citations support the specific mechanism proposed here (that higher perceived stress reduces social/environmental exposure to pathogens and thereby downregulates IL‑10). In short, the sources do not support the claim that perceived stress negatively relates to IL‑10 because of reduced pathogen exposure from diminished social contact; they instead describe neuroendocrine regulation (and a temporal shift) rather than the behavioral exposure mechanism invoked — and in fact report an initial increase in IL‑10 with stress rather than a simple negative relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.co