   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct neuroendocrine and immune mechanisms. Higher perceived stress activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and sympathetic nervous system, which in turn promotes the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces activation of these inflammatory pathways.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-16996-1_6', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159108002985', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5553449/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes a stage-dependent effect: early stress-induced HPA/SNS activation (via glucocorticoids and catecholamines) can suppress proinflammatory cytokine secretion (including IL‑6 and TNF‑α), which partially aligns with a negative relationship. However, the same paper emphasizes that with prolonged/chronic stress (and glucocorticoid resistance) proinflammatory cytokines increase, and it cites many human studies showing elevated IL‑6/TNF‑α with chronic stress. The paper does not invoke a “relaxation response” as the mechanism, nor does it present consistent evidence that higher perceived stress (PSS) generally predicts lower IL‑6/TNF‑α. Thus the sources provide only partial, stage-specific support rather than clear support for the blanket negative claim.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5553449/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", 