": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content or data about perceived stress or proinflammatory cytokines. There is nothing in the citation that supports the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that psychological stress (exam stress) significantly increased stimulated production of proinflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha and IL-6, and that students with higher perceived stress showed higher production of these cytokines. This contradicts the claim that higher perceived stress suppresses immune activity and reduces TNF-alpha and IL-6."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Perceived stress activates the body's relaxation response, which in turn suppresses immune activity and reduces the production of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, higher perceived stress should actually lead to decreased proinflammatory processes rather than an increase.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Perceived stress activates the body's relaxation response, which in turn suppresses immune activity and reduces the production of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, higher perceived stress should actually lead to decreased proinflammatory processes rather than an increase.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

