ided citation does not support the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10425218/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review does not provide evidence that rumination (e.g., on CERQ-type measures) is positively associated with IL-10. Studies in the review that measured IL-10 report null associations for rumination (e.g., Ysseldyk et al. found no significant correlations between rumination subtypes and IL-10) and other work reporting rumination effects primarily found increases in pro‑inflammatory markers (CRP, IL-6, IL-1β) in acute-stressor contexts. One study (Hladek et al.) showed higher IL-10 with lower coping self-efficacy (not rumination), so the review does not support the claim that rumination stimulates an adaptive increase in anti‑inflammatory cytokines like IL-10.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is inaccessible (ScienceDirect error) and provides no content or data linking CERQ-R rumination to increases in IL-10. Because there is no available evidence in the provided citation to evaluate the claim, the explanation is not supported by the citation list."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, involves deep cognitive processing and self-reflection, which could stimulate the body's adaptive stress response systems. This adaptive response may include an increase in anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10, as the body attempts to restore homeostasis and protect itself from the physiological impact of prolonged thought processes.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "R