   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through sustained psychological stress, which activates physiological stress pathways and elevates inflammatory biomarkers. Increased rumination prolongs stress responses, leading to higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing rumination would decrease these proinflammatory processes. This causal link is supported by psychoneuroimmunology research on stress and inflammation.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4849278/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32087393/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumination_(psychology)'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited scoping review reports mixed and largely inconsistent findings; several experimental and longitudinal studies link rumination to increased inflammatory responses (or no effect), and significant negative associations are rare, limited to specific subgroups or to \\"reflection\\" (a distinct, more neutral construct), not rumination broadly. There is not consistent evidence that higher rumination is associated with lower IL-6 or TNF-α levels overall."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4849278/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines rumination\'s associations with affective, cognitive, and autonomic (heart rate, PEP, RSA) responses to a lab stressor in adolescents. It reports that higher rumination is linked to greater negative affect and slower heart-rate recovery (i.e., prolonged physiological activat