ak negative correlation with ISI in an unadjusted model, that association was small and became complex or reversed when emotion-regulation variables were included. Thus the study does not provide evidence that increased (general) rumination reduces sleep disturbance and in fact supports the opposite for negative rumination; measurement differences (CERQ-R, PSQI) further weaken the claimed support.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, can actually serve as a cognitive strategy that helps individuals process and resolve their thoughts before bedtime, leading to less sleep disturbance. By thoroughly reflecting on their day, individuals may reduce mental clutter, thereby improving sleep quality and reducing sleep disturbances as assessed by the PSQI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, can actually serve as a cognitive strategy that helps individuals process and resolve their thoughts before bedtime, leading to less sleep disturbance. By thoroughly reflecting on their day, individuals may reduce mental clutter, thereby improving sleep quality and reducing sleep disturbances as assessed by the PSQI.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

