   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes perceived stress (PSS) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Individuals who engage in more rumination tend to focus repetitively on stressful experiences, which amplifies their perception of stress. Conversely, decreasing rumination leads to a reduction in perceived stress. This causal effect is well-established in psychological research and does not require a mediating variable from those listed in the CLD.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613961/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4165793/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-020-00913-y', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4332541/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613961/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article evaluates the CERQ’s factor structure in remitted depressed adults and characterizes the rumination items as part of a \\"Repetitive Thinking\\" factor that is associated with higher self-reported depressive symptoms and poorer mindfulness. It does not report associations with perceived stress (PSS), nor does it provide evidence that rumination on the CERQ reduces stress or is adaptive in lowering perceived stress. The paper instead treats rumination as maladaptive in this clinical sample. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that higher CERQ rumination is associated with decreased perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4165793/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article defines rumination as past-centered, negative, repetitive thinking and reports that higher state rumination is associated with greater perceived stress (PSS) (r = .49, p = .01) and with increased physiological stress responses. It treats rumination as maladaptive and specifically notes it may exacerbate stress; it also states the study did not measure reflective (adaptive) thinking separately. The explanation\'s claim (that rumination measured by the CERQ-R reflects thoughtful reflection that reduces perceived stress, so higher rumination predicts lower PSS) is opposite to the findings and uses a different rumination measure than the study. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4332541/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that rumination predicts heightened stress sensitivity, greater negative affect and symptoms, and mediates the effect of stress on outcomes — i.e., rumination is associated with increased (not decreased) reactivity to stress. It does not provide evidence that rumination (as measured here) reduces perceived stress or is protective; in fact it documents the opposite. Therefore the claim that higher rumination is associated with decreased perceived stress is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-020-00913-y", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article develops a short RRS and reports that rumination—particularly depressive brooding—is associated with increased depressive symptoms and negative affect, while reflective self-regulation (reflection) showed little or no within-person association with reduced distress. The paper does not report any negative association between rumination (CERQ-R) and perceived stress (PSS); if anything, it describes rumination/brooding as linked to greater distress. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that higher rumination is associated with decreased perceived stress."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, involves thoughtfully reflecting on stressful experiences, which can actually help individuals find solutions and reduce their perceived stress over time. By engaging in rumination, people may gain better insight into their problems, thereby lowering their scores on the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS). Therefore, higher rumination would be associated with decreased perceived stress.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, involves thoughtfully reflecting on stressful experiences, which can actually help individuals find solutions and reduce their perceived stress over time. By engaging in rumination, people may gain better insight into their problems, thereby lowering their scores on the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS). Therefore, higher rumination would be associated with decreased perceived stress.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

