52. Rumination (CERQ-R)-[NEGATIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes Loneliness (UCLS) in a direct causal relationship. Increased rumination, characterized by repetitive negative thinking, can heighten feelings of loneliness by reinforcing perceptions of social isolation and negative social experiences. Conversely, reducing rumination can decrease loneliness. This relationship is supported by psychological mechanisms where persistent self-focused rumination amplifies social withdrawal and perceived social disconnection.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613961/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x', 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/casp.2635', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-020-00913-y'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613961/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article evaluates the CERQ’s factor structure in remitted depressed adults and treats rumination/repetitive thinking as a maladaptive factor associated with greater depressive symptoms and poorer mindfulness; it does not assess loneliness (UCLS) or report any negative association between rumination and loneliness. The source therefore does not provide evidence that rumination reduces loneliness or increases social connectedness — if anything it links repetitive thinking to worse outcomes.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports strong positive associations between rumination and loneliness (Spearman ρ = 0.68, p < 2×10−16), finds rumination mediates the loneliness→depression relationship (significant indirect effect), and identifies specific ruminative thoughts about loneliness as central/bridging nodes (e.g., “think about how alone you are” ↔ “how often do you feel alone”, partial r = 0.307). The authors interpret rumination as maintaining or exacerbating loneliness-related distress and recommend targeting ruminative thoughts to reduce negative outcomes. This evidence contradicts the claim that rumination (as measured) reduces loneliness by constructive reflection."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-020-00913-y", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article validates a short-form RRS and distinguishes depressive brooding (maladaptive) from reflective self-regulation (more adaptive). It reports convergent validity with CERQ rumination and depressive symptoms but does not examine or report any relationship between rumination (CERQ-R or RRS subscales) and loneliness (UCLS). Nothing in the paper supports the claim that rumination reduces loneliness or that reflective rumination increases social connectedness. Therefore the citation does not support the stated negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, involves reflective thinking about one's problems, which can actually help individuals process emotions and therefore reduce feelings of loneliness. By constructively analyzing their experiences, people may feel more connected to themselves and others, resulting in decreased loneliness.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, involves reflective thinking about one's problems, which can actually help individuals process emotions and therefore reduce feelings of loneliness. By constructively analyzing their experiences, people may feel more connected to themselves and others, resulting in decreased loneliness.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

53. Rumination (CERQ-R)-[POSITIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Increased rumination, defined as repetitive and persistent thinking about distressing experiences or feelings, directly heightens depressive symptoms by reinforcing negative thought patterns. Conversely, reducing rumination leads to a decrease in depressive symptoms. This relationship is well-supported in psychological research and does not require mediation by other variables in the CLD.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.25636666626552135, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4116082/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2731158/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumination_(psychology)', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4428129/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7373374631807897, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4116082/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides good evidence that rumination prospectively predicts and (in adults) mediates increases in internalizing symptoms, including depressive symptoms, and it discusses cognitive mechanisms (e.g., impaired disengagement of attention, reduced problem-solving, limited self-regulatory resources) that could link rumination to worse mood. However, the paper does not explicitly describe the specific \\"cognitive overload\\" formulation advanced in the claim (i.e., rumination consuming memory/attentional capacity so people cannot register positive experiences), nor does it support the prescriptive conclusion that interventions should focus on cognitive resource allocation instead of targeting rumination itself. The mediation evidence is also mixed (significant for adults, not for adolescent depression). Thus the core positive association and general cognitive-mechanism idea are supported in part, but the specific mechanism wording and the intervention recommendation are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2731158/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides clear evidence that self-reported rumination (measured with the RSQ/brooding) is positively associated with depressive symptoms and that rumination impairs problem-solving and autobiographical memory specificity—findings consistent with rumination interfering with cognitive processing. However, the study uses different instruments (RSQ and BDI/BDI-II) rather than CERQ-R and IDS‑SR, is correlational/cross‑sectional (so does not establish the causal chain claimed), and does not directly demonstrate the specific \\"cognitive overload\\" mechanism (i.e., consumption of attentional resources leading to reduced processing of positive information) nor test the claim that interventions targeting cognitive resource allocation rather than rumination itself would be most effective. The paper instead discusses treatments that directly target rumination (e.g., rumination‑focused CBT, mindfulness)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4428129/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports that individuals with depression score higher on rumination (RRS) and demonstrates a positive association between rumination and impaired neural indices of conflict monitoring (reduced N2), i.e. rumination correlates with diminished cognitive-control / attentional resource signals. That provides partial support for (1) a positive relationship between rumination and depressive symptomatology (though measured with different instruments than CERQ‑R and IDS‑SR) and (2) a link between rumination and reduced attentional/cognitive-control resources. However, the study is cross‑sectional/correlational (no causal test that rumination causes depressive symptoms via cognitive overload), uses RRS and BDI‑II rather than CERQ‑R and IDS‑SR, finds no relationship between rumination and behavioral performance or P3, and does not provide evidence that interventions should target cognitive resource allocation instead of rumination itself (in fact the authors suggest reducing rumination). Thus the mechanistic causal claim and the intervention recommendation are not supported by the citation, while the association and the link to impaired cognitive control are partially supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Psychiatry.org article directly supports a positive link between rumination and depression (rumination can contribute to development/worsening of depression, leads ruminators to recall more negative events, interpret situations more negatively, and impedes moving on to problem solving). However, the citation does not explicitly describe the proposed \\"cognitive overload\\" mechanism (rumination consuming attentional/memory resources) in neurocognitive terms, nor does it provide evidence that interventions targeting cognitive resource allocation would be more effective than interventions targeting rumination itself. Thus the core association and the impairment of problem-solving/positive recall are supported, but the specific resource-consumption mechanism and the treatment recommendation are not substantiated by the cited source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumination_(psychology)", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents a positive association between rumination and depressive symptoms and describes mechanisms that rumination impairs problem-solving and is linked to attentional and memory biases (i.e., it can interfere with concentration and processing). However, the article also reports mixed findings about relationships between rumination and executive/working-memory functions, so the specific “cognitive overload consuming attentional and memory resources” mechanism is only partly supported by the citation. The claim that interventions should target cognitive resource allocation rather than rumination itself is not supported — the citation describes interventions that directly target rumination (e.g., rumination-focused CBT, mindfulness) and gives no evidence that resource-allocation interventions are superior. The citation also does not mention the specific measures named (CERQ-R, IDS-SR)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) via a cognitive overload mechanism. Specifically, increased rumination is thought to consume significant attentional and memory resources, thereby reducing the brain’s capacity to process positive information or engage in problem-solving. This cognitive overload then leads directly to heightened depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), as individuals become unable to register or recall positive experiences. Therefore, interventions targeting cognitive resource allocation, rather than rumination itself, would be most effective in alleviating depressive symptoms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) via a cognitive overload mechanism. Specifically, increased rumination is thought to consume significant attentional and memory resources, thereby reducing the brain’s capacity to process positive information or engage in problem-solving. This cognitive overload then leads directly to heightened depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), as individuals become unable to register or recall positive experiences. Therefore, interventions targeting cognitive resource allocation, rather than rumination itself, would be most effective in alleviating depressive symptoms.', 'perplexity': 1.3598324707585472, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8066227517993885, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04864043145942394, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026805758832556207, 'prob_variance': 0.0657238675720972, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0895917941893063, 'avg_nll': 0.307361508894505}

