79. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces loneliness, while decreasing perceived social support increases loneliness. This relationship is direct, widely supported in theory and evidence, and does not appear to be fully mediated by other variables in the provided set.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.279500834025254, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7760767751482396, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study (Adamczyk, 2015) provides evidence of a negative association between perceived social support (MSPSS) and measures of loneliness (SELSA-S), and shows perceived support (especially from significant others and family) predicts lower romantic loneliness and moderates duration-of-single effects. However, the paper is cross-sectional (no causal inference), does not measure frequency of social activities or unoccupied/free time, and does not test the proposed mediation pathway (support → more social activity → less unoccupied time → lower loneliness). Therefore the general negative association is supported, but the specific causal mechanism via increased social activity and reduced unoccupied time is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only citation provided is an inaccessible ResearchGate page showing an access/connection error and contains no substantive study content. Because no evidence from the cited source is available in the provided material, it cannot support the claimed inverse causal relationship or the specific mediation (increased social activity / reduced unoccupied time) described in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an error message from ScienceDirect and contains no usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the cited material that documents any relationship—direct or mediated—between perceived social support (MSPSS), social activity, unoccupied time, and loneliness (UCLS). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation(s)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship because individuals who report higher levels of social support tend to engage in more frequent social activities, which in turn reduces the amount of unstructured free time available for experiencing loneliness. Thus, it is the reduction in unoccupied time, mediated by increased social activity stemming from higher perceived social support, that leads directly to lower loneliness (UCLS) scores, rather than any direct psychological impact of support itself.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship because individuals who report higher levels of social support tend to engage in more frequent social activities, which in turn reduces the amount of unstructured free time available for experiencing loneliness. Thus, it is the reduction in unoccupied time, mediated by increased social activity stemming from higher perceived social support, that leads directly to lower loneliness (UCLS) scores, rather than any direct psychological impact of support itself.', 'perplexity': 1.4090002117588092, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8155938334994569, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01538897804949971, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005002949804000342, 'prob_variance': 0.07812071622081258, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0665909514197354, 'avg_nll': 0.34288038320667324}

