   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Higher perceived social support directly reduces perceived stress by providing emotional and practical resources to cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived social support increases perceived stress due to fewer coping resources. This causal pathway is well-established in the psychological literature.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8758220/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that greater perceived social support (family and significant other) is associated with lower perceived stress and that support negatively predicts perceived stress; it does not provide evidence that higher perceived support increases stress or discuss obligations/awareness effects described in the explanation. Therefore the sources do not support a positive (increasing) relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8758220/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article (Rui & Guo, 2022) reports that perceived social support (PSS) was not positively related to stress (PSS had no significant direct association with stress and in fact buffered the effect of COVID-19 news exposure on stress). The paper does discuss prior findings that received (not perceived) support can sometimes increase stress, and its own results found received support (RSS) was positively associated with stress, but that evidence concerns RSS rather than perceived support. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that higher perceived social support increases perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review overwhelmingly describes social support as protective — reducing physiological and subjective stress — and presents many studies showing buffering effects. However, it also cites work noting that social support can be \\"a double-edged sword\\" (e.g., Revenson et al. 1991) and that type/quality of support matters, which implies some forms of support can be problematic. The review does not, though, provide direct evidence or detailed discussion in the excerpt for the specific mechanisms named in the explanation (obligation/reciprocity or increased awareness amplifying perceived stress). Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study (Acoba, 2024) reports that perceived social support (MSPSS) is negatively associated with perceived stress (PSS-10): family support (r = −0.18) and significant-other support (r = −0.12) predicted lower perceived stress, and mediation analyses showed social support reduced perceived stress (negative a-paths). Friend support showed no relation. The paper therefore provides evidence of social support decreasing — not increasing — perceived stress and does not offer findings that higher perceived support increases stress via obligations or heightened awareness.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error message), so there is no usable evidence in the provided citation to support the claim that higher perceived social support increases perceived stress. Without accessible content, the explanation cannot be verified from the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher perceived social support can sometimes increase perceived stress because individuals may feel obligated to reciprocate support or live up to social expectations, leading to heightened stress levels. Additionally, being surrounded by supportive people might make individuals more aware of their own struggles, thereby amplifying their perception of stress.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher perceived social support can sometimes increase perceived stress because individuals may feel obligated to reciprocate support or live up to social expectations, leading to heightened stress levels. Additionally, being surrounded by supportive people might make individuals more aware of their own struggles, thereby amplifying their perception of stress.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

