ionship. However, it is a cross‑sectional observational study (authors explicitly state it cannot establish causation), it does not provide evidence of neurobiological mechanisms (no data on serotonin or neurochemical changes), and it uses PHQ‑9 rather than IDS‑SR. The citation therefore does not support the causal, serotonin‑mediated, inevitable or automatically reversible claims in the explanation. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes an increase in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because prolonged states of loneliness result in neurobiological changes that directly disrupt serotonin production, which in turn produces depressive symptoms. Therefore, any rise in Loneliness (UCLS) will almost inevitably cause a neurochemical imbalance, resulting in higher depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), independent of psychological or social factors. Reducing Loneliness (UCLS) will thus automatically restore neurochemical balance and decrease depressive symptoms (IDS-SR).', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes an increase in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because prolonged states of loneliness result in neurobiological changes that directly disrupt serotonin production, which in turn produces depressive symptoms. Therefore, any rise in Loneliness (UCLS) will almost inevitably cause a neurochemical imbalance, resulting in higher depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), independent of psychological or social factors. Reducing Loneliness (UCLS) will thus automatically restore neurochemical balance and decrease depressive symptoms (IDS-SR).', 'perplexity': 1.410589634748381, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7958084803269319, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.052274729487329696, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005033156857110255, 'prob_variance': 0.07820377610305042, 'releva