lational/observational findings and theoretical mechanisms rather than direct causal evidence or intervention trials showing that reducing loneliness causes improved PSQI-measured sleep. The claim of a direct causal relationship and of reductions in loneliness leading to fewer sleep disturbances goes beyond what the review documents."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-93681-3", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study (Liu et al., Sci. Rep.) provides evidence of a positive association between loneliness (ULS‑8) and poorer sleep quality (PSQI) (Spearman r = 0.139, p < 0.01) and shows sleep quality partly mediates the loneliness→depression relationship. It also cites literature on stress/HPA‑axis mechanisms linking loneliness and sleep. However, the study is cross‑sectional and the authors explicitly note this limits causal inference; effect sizes for the loneliness–sleep link are modest. The paper therefore supports an association and plausible mechanisms but does not establish a direct causal effect or that reducing loneliness will necessarily reduce sleep disturbance."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Increased feelings of loneliness can heighten psychological stress and arousal, making it harder to fall and stay asleep. Conversely, reducing loneliness can lead to fewer sleep disturbances. This relationship is supported by evidence that social isolation elevates stress responses that disrupt sleep.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4393097160817803, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7925160192210917, 'min_prob': 0.01345799719428585, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006372715502487559, 'prob_variance': 0.08053359200908575, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.101893389981