   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased loneliness is associated with reduced IL-10 production due to heightened stress and immune dysregulation, while decreased loneliness can enhance IL-10-mediated anti-inflammatory activity. This relationship is supported by psychoneuroimmunology evidence linking social isolation to impaired anti-inflammatory responses.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8618012', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306453012001060'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measured both basal and LPS-stimulated cytokines including IL-10 but found no significant associations between loneliness (trait or aggregated momentary) and the cytokine composites or any individual cytokines; only CRP showed a positive association with loneliness. Thus the source does not support a loneliness-driven upregulation of anti‑inflammatory cytokines such as IL‑10—in fact the cytokine results were null.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measured both basal and LPS-stimulated cytokines including IL-10 but reported no significant associations between loneliness (trait or momentary) and any basal or stimulated cytokines. It did find a positive association with CRP, but not with IL-10 or other cytokines. Therefore the claim that loneliness upregulates IL-10 (an anti-inflammatory cytokine) is not supported by this source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that loneliness is associated with a proinflammatory phenotype — greater stimulated production of proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑6, TNF‑α, IL‑1β) following acute stress — and cites evidence of down‑regulation of anti‑inflammatory genes. It provides no data showing upregulation of anti‑inflammatory cytokines such as IL‑10; in fact, the evidence cited points the opposite direction. Therefore the sources do not support a positive relationship between loneliness and IL‑10 (or enhanced anti‑inflammatory processes)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8618012", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review mostly describes loneliness as linked to a pro-inflammatory profile (↑IL-6, ↑TNF-α, CTRA-driven pro‑inflammatory gene expression, impaired antiviral responses). It does note limited findings where IL‑10 (a type‑2/anti‑inflammatory cytokine) was elevated in lonelier individuals under stress and that IL‑10 appeared among features associated with loneliness in one composite model, but these are isolated observations within an overall pro‑inflammatory picture. The review does not support the stronger claim that increased loneliness generally upregulates anti‑inflammatory processes (or that this is a protective, health‑promoting response).  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306453012001060", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (error message), so there is no evidence in the provided citation to support the claim that loneliness positively upregulates IL-10 or anti-inflammatory processes. Without usable citation content, the relationship is not supported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Loneliness has been shown to stimulate the body's adaptive mechanisms, including an upregulation of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10, as a protective response against stress-induced inflammation. Therefore, increased loneliness directly enhances anti-inflammatory processes, helping maintain immune balance and promoting overall health.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Loneliness has been shown to stimulate the body's adaptive mechanisms, including an upregulation of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10, as a protective response against stress-induced inflammation. Therefore, increased loneliness directly enhances anti-inflammatory processes, helping maintain immune balance and promoting overall health.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

