ociations: higher loneliness (measured with SELSA‑S) co‑occurs with lower perceived social support (MSPSS) and the authors report negative relationships between support and loneliness in their analyses. However the study is cross‑sectional and the authors explicitly note this prevents causal inference; their regression models treat support (and duration single) as predictors of loneliness rather than demonstrating that loneliness causes changes in perceived support. The paper therefore supports a negative association but not the causal direction stated in the claim. Also the loneliness measure used in the citation (SELSA‑S) is not the UCLA scale named in the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased loneliness directly leads to decreased perceived social support, as individuals who feel lonelier tend to perceive less support from others. Conversely, decreased loneliness is causally linked to increased perceived social support.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2381867528386445, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8636851851297134, 'min_prob': 0.09082121621548855, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003614465071846423, 'prob_variance': 0.052530277347119556, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8557061432146964, 'avg_nll': 0.21364801332071406}

