   Properties: {'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.2797415785094693, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4136523/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7557152153921787, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports cross-sectional correlations and mediation analyses treating perceived social support (MSPSS) as a predictor of mental-health outcomes (including depression) and explicitly notes its design cannot establish causality. It shows an association (higher support ↔ lower depression) but does not provide evidence that depressive symptoms (measured by IDS-SR or otherwise) causally reduce perceived social support. The paper neither tests nor supports the reverse causal direction; authors acknowledge the cross-sectional limitation and call for longitudinal work to assess causality."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4136523/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited CALM study used longitudinal path analysis in an RCT and reports bidirectional, temporally-ordered relations between depressive symptoms (measured with the PHQ‑9) and perceived social support (measured with the MOS Social Support Survey). The authors explicitly state that depression predicts subsequent perceived social support (i.e., changes in depression lead to changes in perceived support), which supports a causal influence of depressive symptoms on perceived support in this dataset. However, the study used different instruments (PHQ‑9 and MOS-SSS, not IDS‑SR and MSPSS), examined mediation within an intervention trial (not a general population causal claim), and the reporting of specific path signs is complex; the paper does not unequivocally present a simple, generalizable \\"increase in depression causes a decrease in perceived support\\" statement across all contexts. Therefore the evidence partially supports the user\'s claim but not fully as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source only describes the Multidimensional Scale of Perceived Social Support (MSPSS) instrument (items, scoring, translations). It contains no data, analyses, or discussion linking depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) to perceived social support, nor any evidence of a causal (or inverse) relationship. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased depressive symptoms can causally lead individuals to withdraw socially, perceive less support, or interpret social interactions more negatively, thus reducing perceived social support. Conversely, lower depressive symptoms may enhance perceptions of available support. This is a direct negative causal relationship, not merely a correlation.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3974411172662728, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8143116143449715, 'min_prob': 0.0473747822151773, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0040820767702825, 'prob_variance': 0.07825535074696956, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0060610817829172, 'avg_nll': 0.33464279082403714}

