   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) causally increase loneliness (UCLS) because experiencing more depressive symptoms can lead to social withdrawal and reduced engagement with others, directly increasing feelings of loneliness. Conversely, reducing depressive symptoms can decrease loneliness by promoting social interaction. There are no mediating variables in the CLD that fully explain this direct relationship.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.26374927614216126, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9938924/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7444250/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpsy/article/PIIS2215-0366(20)30383-7/fulltext'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.703125928670272, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9938924/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study (Luo 2022) provides evidence that higher depressive symptoms predict greater future loneliness (within-person cross-lagged effects), and it discusses cognitive-discrepancy and recall/appraisal mechanisms (citing work on biased recall and impairments in attention/cognition). However, the specific mechanistic claim here — that depression causes people to forget past social interactions (via attention/memory impairments) which thereby increases subjective loneliness irrespective of actual social engagement — is not directly supported by the citations. The sources describe biased recall and negative appraisal of social interactions rather than explicit forgetting of interactions as the causal pathway. Also, the cited study measured depressive symptoms with an abbreviated CES‑D, not the IDS‑SR named in the claim, so the exact measures differ. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents an association and symptom overlap between loneliness and depression and discusses links between loneliness and cognitive decline/dementia, but it does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed — that depression-related attention/memory impairments cause people to forget social interactions which then increase subjective loneliness independent of actual social contact. The sources do not describe or demonstrate that depressive cognitive deficits produce forgetting of interactions that drives loneliness, nor do they establish the asserted causal pathway irrespective of social engagement."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7444250/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-synthesis provides qualitative evidence that depressive symptoms are associated with increased loneliness via social withdrawal, non-disclosure, and self‑reinforcing cycles (supporting a positive relationship). However, it does not report the specific cognitive‑mechanism proposed — i.e., attention/memory impairments causing people to forget social interactions and thereby feel lonelier. The review explicitly notes little mention of impaired social communication/perception and does not describe forgetting of interactions as a pathway; references to \\"cognitive interventions\\" concern maladaptive social cognitions, not memory/recall deficits. Also, the evidence is qualitative and descriptive, so strong causal claims are not directly established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - Th