   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase via neuroimmune mechanisms. Higher levels of depressive symptoms can directly upregulate inflammatory cytokines, leading to increased TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing depressive symptoms can lower these proinflammatory markers. This is a direct, positive causal relationship.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.26592309597561165, 'citations': ['https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/ejn.15992', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0165178120332297', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.780851973098642, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0165178120332297", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole citation is an inaccessible ScienceDirect error message with no content; it provides no data or findings about depressive symptoms, physical activity, diet, TNF-alpha, or IL-6, nor any evidence of a behavioral mediation. Because the provided source contains no usable information, it cannot support the stated causal pathway.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper reports higher IL‑6 in depressed outpatients versus controls and discusses IL‑6 and TNF‑α associations with brain functional connectivity; it and its referenced meta-analyses note that proinflammatory cytokines are implicated in depression. However, in this study TNF‑α was not elevated in the depressed sample, and the authors did not measure or test behavioral mediators (physical activity or diet) nor perform mediation analyses linking depressive symptoms → poorer activity/diet → increased IL‑6/TNF‑α. The paper explicitly calls for future studies to include lifestyle variables. Therefore the general positive link between depression and (especially) IL‑6 is supported, but the specific causal pathway mediated primarily by reduced physical activity and poor diet (and the claim about TNF‑α increases) is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly documents a positive association between depression and elevated proinflammatory cytokines (including IL‑6 and TNF‑α) and discusses links between metabolic/behavioral factors (diet, obesity, antioxidant intake, metabolic syndrome) and inflammation. However, it frames most evidence as observational and emphasizes stress‑HPA axis and immune activation as core mechanistic pathways. It does not provide direct evidence that depressive symptoms (measured by IDS‑SR) causally lead to reduced physical activity and poorer diet which in turn are the primary drivers of increased IL‑6 and TNF‑α. The review mentions “sickness behavior” (reduced activity/appetite) and diet/inflammation associations, but does not establish the behavioral mediation as the main causal pathway or cite direct longitudinal/experimental evidence for that specific chain. It also does not reference IDS‑SR specifically."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) lead to increased proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily as a result of individuals with higher depressive symptoms engaging in less physical activity and adopting poorer dietary habits. The resultant sedentary lifestyle and suboptimal nutrition directly trigger the upregulation of TNF-alpha and IL-6, explaining the observed association. Therefore, the causal pathway is mediated by behavioral changes associated with depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), which in turn drive proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6).', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.3333333333333333, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) lead to increased proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily as a result of individuals with higher depressive symptoms engaging in less physical activity and adopting poorer dietary habits. The resultant sedentary lifestyle and suboptimal nutrition directly trigger the upregulation of TNF-alpha and IL-6, explaining the observed association. Therefore, the causal pathway is mediated by behavioral changes associated with depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), which in turn drive proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6).', 'perplexity': 1.3181560239014132, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8311279662429205, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.12053298224109656, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0015528124617498326, 'prob_variance': 0.07071509297325437, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1094551686408467, 'avg_nll': 0.27623380836191097}

