SSED - The cited paper examines relationships between objective sleep measures and PSQI component scores in older adults; it does not measure perceived social support (MSPSS) or report associations between PSQI scores and social support or sociability. It therefore provides no evidence for the claim that poor sleep (higher PSQI) causes people to perceive less social support (nor for the proposed mechanism of withdrawal). The study is about sleep measurement concordance and PSQI components, not about social support or causal effects on interpersonal perceptions."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Poor sleep quality, as measured by higher PSQI scores, directly causes individuals to perceive less social support. This is because disrupted sleep makes people less sociable and more withdrawn, leading them to believe they have fewer supportive relationships, regardless of their actual social network.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Poor sleep quality, as measured by higher PSQI scores, directly causes individuals to perceive less social support. This is because disrupted sleep makes people less sociable and more withdrawn, leading them to believe they have fewer supportive relationships, regardless of their actual social network.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

