124. Sleep disturbance (PSQI)-[NEGATIVE]->Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased sleep disturbance is linked with a reduction in IL-10 production, as poor sleep impairs anti-inflammatory immune function. Conversely, improved sleep quality can increase IL-10 levels. This relationship is supported by evidence showing sleep disruption leads to suppressed anti-inflammatory cytokine activity.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.2893742518751635, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/', 'https://www.psychiatry.pitt.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/PSQI%20Article.pdf', 'https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5143488/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7587186951846719, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows an inverse association between PSQI-measured poor sleep and circulating IL-10 (poor sleepers had lower IL-10; PSQI correlated negatively with IL-10), so the negative relationship is supported. However, the paper is cross-sectional and reports associations in hemodialysis patients; it does not provide evidence that sleep disturbance \\"causes\\" IL-10 to decrease nor any mechanistic evidence for a compensatory downregulation or the proposed homeostatic interpretation/conservation-of-resources mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source only describes the PSQI instrument (what it measures, scoring, validity, translations) and contains no data or discussion about IL-10, anti-inflammatory processes, or the proposed compensatory downregulation mechanism. It therefore provides no evidence linking sleep disturbance measured by the PSQI to decreased IL-10 levels or the causal mechanism described."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is a review of the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) covering its development, structure, psychometrics, and use in populations. It contains no data or discussion about cytokines, IL‑10, anti‑inflammatory processes, or the proposed compensatory downregulation mechanism linking sleep disturbance to reduced IL‑10. Therefore the citation does not support the causal biological claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5143488/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review summarizes strong evidence that sleep disturbance and experimental sleep loss activate proinflammatory signaling (eg, IL-6, TNF, NF-κB, TLR4 activation) and discusses homeostatic and SNS/HPA mechanisms that tend to increase inflammatory activity. It explicitly notes that most studies have focused on proinflammatory markers and that anti‑inflammatory cytokines (eg, IL‑10) have often not been assessed. The review does not present evidence that IL‑10 is reduced with greater sleep disturbance, nor does it describe the proposed compensatory mechanism (the body \\"interpreting\\" sleep disturbance as reduced inflammatory threat and downregulating IL‑10 to conserve resources). Therefore the specific negative IL‑10 relationship and the stated downregulation mechanism are not supported by the cited material."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease due to a compensatory downregulation mechanism. When individuals experience more sleep disturbance, the body interprets this as a signal of decreased inflammatory threats and therefore intentionally reduces production of anti-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 to conserve resources. As a result, increased sleep disturbance (PSQI) is associated with lower IL-10 levels, while improved sleep quality removes this signal, leading to normalization or upregulation of anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10). This mechanism aligns with homeostatic theories suggesting the immune system adjusts cytokine production primarily based on perceived need rather than as a direct consequence of physiological stressors like poor sleep.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease due to a compensatory downregulation mechanism. When individuals experience more sleep disturbance, the body interprets this as a signal of decreased inflammatory threats and therefore intentionally reduces production of anti-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 to conserve resources. As a result, increased sleep disturbance (PSQI) is associated with lower IL-10 levels, while improved sleep quality removes this signal, leading to normalization or upregulation of anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10). This mechanism aligns with homeostatic theories suggesting the immune system adjusts cytokine production primarily based on perceived need rather than as a direct consequence of physiological stressors like poor sleep.', 'perplexity': 1.440417899238344, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8041494334056366, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.027627787218500427, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004363812428879458, 'prob_variance': 0.08373745764831059, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2389189438132644, 'avg_nll': 0.3649332792901754}

