133. Physical inactivity (SBQ)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct physiological mechanisms. Reduced physical activity leads to elevated systemic inflammation, as inactivity is linked to greater production of TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, increasing physical activity lowers these proinflammatory markers. This is a direct and positive causal relationship.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12626436/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8703787/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3629815/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15772055/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines IL-6 and TNF-α levels in obese versus normal-weight subjects and reports that obesity is associated with increased concentrations of these proinflammatory cytokines (with adipose tissue as a source). It does not present evidence that physical inactivity or sedentary behavior suppresses IL-6 or TNF-α; if anything, the citation links greater adiposity (which often co-occurs with inactivity) to higher cytokine levels. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship between physical inactivity and proinflammatory cytokines."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8703787/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review reports that exercise training (endurance, resistance, HIIT, concurrent) reduces circulating proinflammatory markers (IL‑6, TNF‑α, CRP) and that physical inactivity is associated with low‑grade systemic inflammation. Thus the sources indicate activity lowers inflammation and inactivity promotes it — the opposite of the claimed effect that inactivity suppresses IL‑6 and TNF‑α.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3629815/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and its summarized studies report that higher physical activity (or exercise training) is generally associated with lower systemic inflammatory markers, including IL-6 and, in many studies, TNFα or related receptors. Observational cohorts show an inverse relationship between activity/fitness and IL-6/TNF-related measures, and several intervention trials report reductions in IL-6 (and in some cases TNFα or TNF receptors) following exercise. While some trials found no change, none of the cited evidence supports the claim that physical inactivity suppresses proinflammatory cytokines; rather the citations indicate the opposite (activity tends to decrease inflammation, inactivity is associated with higher inflammation). \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12626436/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that exercise increases muscle-derived IL-6 and that both exercise and IL-6 infusion inhibit endotoxin-induced TNF-alpha production — i.e., physical activity has an antiinflammatory effect by lowering TNF-alpha. That directly contradicts the claim that physical inactivity suppresses proinflammatory cytokines; the paper supports the opposite relationship (activity reduces TNF-alpha, and IL-6 produced during exercise is part of that antiinflammatory mechanism). \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15772055/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article describes an anti-inflammatory effect of exercise — regular physical activity suppresses TNF-alpha and muscle contractions release IL-6 (a myokine) that actually promotes anti-inflammatory responses and inhibits TNF-alpha. It does not support the claim that physical inactivity suppresses proinflammatory cytokines; if anything, it implies the opposite (exercise reduces proinflammatory TNF-alpha, while IL-6 is produced during exercise and has anti-inflammatory roles). Therefore the citation does not support the stated negative relationship for inactivity."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Physical inactivity actually suppresses proinflammatory processes such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, because reduced muscle activity leads to lower metabolic demands and less immune system activation. As a result, sedentary behavior diminishes the body's production of inflammatory cytokines, thereby reducing overall inflammation.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Physical inactivity actually suppresses proinflammatory processes such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, because reduced muscle activity leads to lower metabolic demands and less immune system activation. As a result, sedentary behavior diminishes the body's production of inflammatory cytokines, thereby reducing overall inflammation.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

