139. Physical inactivity (SBQ)-[POSITIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Increased physical inactivity can lead to higher risk of depressive symptoms through mechanisms such as reduced endorphin release, lower social interaction, and disrupted circadian rhythms. Conversely, decreased physical inactivity (i.e., more physical activity) causally reduces depressive symptoms. This relationship is not fully mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.26115097188738284, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2022/22_0003.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7527477/', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpsy/article/PIIS2215-0366(20)30034-1/fulltext', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35416941/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7589582770604637, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2022/22_0003.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited YRBS study documents a positive association between lower physical activity / greater sedentary behavior and higher odds of self-reported depressive symptoms in adolescents, and the association persists after adjustment for several behavioral covariates. However, the paper is cross-sectional and explicitly states it cannot establish causality. It does not provide empirical evidence that reduced exposure to natural daylight is the primary causal mechanism, nor does it discuss direct disruption of serotonin synthesis or show that reduced daylight alone explains the effect independent of other psychosocial or lifestyle factors. The discussion only speculates that increased interactions with nature might help well-being; it does not test or confirm the specific daylight–serotonin causal pathway or the asserted sufficiency of light exposure as the main mediator."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7527477/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides cross-sectional and prospective evidence that lower physical activity is associated with higher depression scores (i.e., inactivity predicts greater depressive symptoms over 4 years), so it supports a positive relationship between inactivity and depression. However, the paper does not measure sedentary behavior via an SBQ, uses the EURO‑D depression scale (not IDS‑SR), and contains no data or analyses about daylight exposure, serotonin synthesis, or the mechanistic claim that reduced natural light alone is sufficient and primary/independent of other factors. It also does not demonstrate that increasing light exposure (as opposed to other aspects of activity) is the mechanism by which activity reduces depressive symptoms. Therefore the core association is supported but the specific causal mechanism and the “primarily/independent” claims are not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35416941/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited meta-analysis supports an inverse association between physical activity and risk of depression (i.e., physical inactivity is associated with higher depressive symptoms/incidence). However, it does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism proposed (reduced daylight exposure → disrupted serotonin synthesis) nor for the claim that reduced daylight alone is sufficient or the primary pathway independent of other lifestyle or psychosocial factors. The study is observational and does not identify or isolate that mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) primarily because extended periods of sedentary behavior limit exposure to natural daylight, which in turn directly disrupts serotonin synthesis in the brain and precipitates depressive symptoms. Individuals who are more physically inactive typically spend less time outdoors, and this reduced daylight exposure alone is sufficient to explain the increase in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), independent of other lifestyle or psychosocial factors. Thus, interventions targeting physical inactivity (SBQ) can alleviate depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) chiefly by increasing natural light exposure, regardless of other changes in behavior or mood regulation strategies.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) primarily because extended periods of sedentary behavior limit exposure to natural daylight, which in turn directly disrupts serotonin synthesis in the brain and precipitates depressive symptoms. Individuals who are more physically inactive typically spend less time outdoors, and this reduced daylight exposure alone is sufficient to explain the increase in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), independent of other lifestyle or psychosocial factors. Thus, interventions targeting physical inactivity (SBQ) can alleviate depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) chiefly by increasing natural light exposure, regardless of other changes in behavior or mood regulation strategies.', 'perplexity': 1.3294376711405962, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8251051336457663, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11176198100601084, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002280284790067163, 'prob_variance': 0.06819983011772462, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9830447979805953, 'avg_nll': 0.2847560491733086}

