ransportation) can shape social connection. It does not present evidence that reduced physical activity produces immediate physiological/neurochemical changes (serotonin, dopamine) that directly trigger feelings of loneliness independent of social context. The cited material supports social and contextual pathways to loneliness but not the specific neurobiological mechanism claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666518223000451", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains no usable content (error message), so there is nothing in the provided citation to support the proposed neurobiological mechanism linking physical inactivity to increased loneliness. Because no evidence is available from the citation, the claim is unsupported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A plausible mechanism underlying the positive relationship between Physical inactivity (SBQ) and Loneliness (UCLS) is that individuals who are less physically active may experience physiological changes that directly trigger feelings of loneliness. Reduced physical movement can alter neurochemical balances, such as serotonin and dopamine levels, which are known to influence mood and social perception. As a result, higher scores on Physical inactivity (SBQ) are likely to produce an immediate increase in Loneliness (UCLS), independent of social context or external interactions, as the neurobiological effects of inactivity foster a subjective sense of isolation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A plausible mechanism underlying the positive relationship between Physical inactivity (SBQ) and Loneliness (UCLS) is that individuals who are less physically active may experience physiological changes that directly trigger feelings of loneliness. 