.nih.gov/books/NBK44698/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Surgeon General chapter discusses methods for judging causal relationships of smoking with health outcomes and lists many diseases caused by smoking, but it does not address \\"Spatial Scale\\" or evaluate any link between smoking and spatial scale. It therefore provides no evidence (positive or negative) about a causal relationship between Smoking (SMQ) and Spatial Scale, nor about the absence of any plausible mechanism connecting them."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4375397/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper discusses methods for detecting and adjusting for confounding by cigarette smoking in occupational epidemiology (negative control outcomes, radon/radiation and lung cancer/COPD). It does not address \\"Spatial Scale\\" or any causal relationship between smoking and spatial scale. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence that smoking does or does not cause changes in spatial scale (nor that spatial scale affects smoking). The claim of \\"no causal relationship\\" is unsupported by the provided source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Smoking (SMQ) does not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Smoking (SMQ) to change. There is no plausible direct or indirect causal mechanism linking these two variables. Therefore, no causal relationship exists between them.', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1397013577699882, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9216625653986149, 'min_prob': 0.08852716122666353, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003530965408585176, 'prob_variance': 0.03584321644926878, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.580558771822272, 'avg_nll': 0.1307662612186588}

155. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[NONE]->Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)
   Properties: {'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.2612407126094034, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7025456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full', 'https://online.ucpress.edu/collabra/article/10/1/121385/202994/Which-Measures-of-Beliefs-About-Others'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7669045755168691, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7025456/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents clear links between social behavior/relationships and immune activity (including up- or down-regulation of inflammatory and antiviral pathways) and emphasizes complex, mediated, bidirectional mechanisms (e.g., social-evaluative threat, loneliness, social support) rather than simple one-step causal chains. It also notes that much of the work is correlational and that mechanisms remain incompletely understood. However, the paper does not specifically test prosocial behavior measured in a public-goods game (PGG) or IL-10 levels, nor does it provide direct evidence that a causal PGG → IL-10 (or IL-10 → PGG) pathway is absent. Thus the sources support the view that effects are likely mediated and that simple direct links are not established, but they do not directly demonstrate the nonexistence of a causal PGG–IL-10 link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review thoroughly describes molecular, cellular, and signaling mechanisms that regulate IL-10 (type I IFNs, IL-27, TLRs, transcription factors, epigenetic and post‑transcriptional control, genetic polymorphisms, etc.), but it contains no information about prosocial behavior (PGG), social support, or behavioral/psychosocial causes of IL-10. While the paper shows IL-10 regulation is complex and involves intermediate biological pathways, it does not provide evidence that a direct causal link between prosocial behavior and IL-10 is absent; it does not address behavioral influences. Therefore the explanation\'s claim (that no direct causal relationship exists between prosocial behavior and IL-10) is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes IL-10 as a pleiotropic, tightly regulated cytokine whose production and activity are controlled by many cellular, molecular and environmental stimuli (TLR signaling, other cytokines, metabolic state, infection, pharmacologic and natural compounds, etc.). That supports the general point that IL-10 levels are mediated by intermediate biological pathways rather than being a simple direct effect of an unrelated high-level behavior. However, the review does not discuss prosocial behavior (PGG) or social/psychological variables (e.g., social support, stress reduction) as direct causes of IL-10, nor does it provide evidence that prosocial behavior has no direct causal link to IL-10. Thus the sources support the plausibility that any behavioral effects on IL-10 would operate via intermediate physiological pathways, but they do not directly demonstrate the absence of a causal link between prosocial behavior and IL-10."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3333333333333333, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where prosocial behavior (PGG) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) or vice versa. Any potential influence of prosocial behavior on IL-10 would likely be mediated by other psychological or physiological pathways, such as stress reduction or social support. Since