   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in more prosocial behavior directly reduces feelings of loneliness by increasing social connection and positive social interactions. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior can directly increase loneliness due to reduced opportunities for meaningful social engagement.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10150042/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how loneliness (manipulated via priming and measured with the UCLA scale) affects subsequent prosocial behavior in a public-goods/eco-choice task and finds that increased loneliness reduces prosocial behavior. It does not provide evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior increases loneliness; if anything, the paper notes prosocial acts can help combat loneliness. Thus the citation addresses the reverse causal direction and contradicts rather than supports the claimed positive effect of prosocial behavior on loneliness."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5025448/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how loneliness influences prosocial behavior (finding, for example, that loneliness can increase public prosocial actions in some cases), not whether engaging in prosocial behavior causes increased loneliness. It does not provide evidence that performing prosocial acts (e.g., in public goods games) leads to greater loneliness or the proposed mechanism of overextending to others at the expense of forming deeper connections. Thus the citation does not support the claimed causal direction or explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article experimentally manipulates loneliness and shows that increased loneliness reduces prosocial behavior in a public-goods/eco-choice task (and discusses prosocial acts as a way to combat loneliness). It does not provide evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior increases subsequent loneliness; indeed, it presents the opposite directional relationship. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive effect of prosocial behavior on loneliness."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines how loneliness influences prosocial behavior (finding lonely people — especially women — may give more in public settings), not whether engaging in prosocial behavior causes increased loneliness. It provides no empirical evidence that performing prosocial acts (e.g., in public-goods settings) increases later feelings of loneliness or that overextending oneself to help others leads to neglected deeper connections and greater loneliness. Thus the citation addresses the reverse causal direction and does not support the claimed mechanism."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as participating in public goods games, can sometimes increase feelings of loneliness because it may lead individuals to focus on others’ needs at the expense of their own social fulfillment. This overextension might cause them to neglect forming deeper connections, paradoxically resulting in greater loneliness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as participating in public goods games, can sometimes increase feelings of loneliness because it may lead individuals to focus on others’ needs at the expense of their own social fulfillment. This overextension might cause them to neglect forming deeper connections, paradoxically resulting in greater loneliness.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

158. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where prosocial behavior (PGG) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) or vice versa. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as stress, social support, or mental health in the system. Without a clear mechanistic pathway or evidence for direct causality, the relationship should not be included in the CLD.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10960128/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016141', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10039809/', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1120972109', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7308656/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10960128/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and its referenced studies provide evidence that positive social connections and prosocial behaviors are associated with reduced inflammatory activity (including meta-analytic links between social support and lower IL-6 and TNF-α) and that prosocial interventions can shift leukocyte gene expression away from proinflammatory profiles (CTRA). The review also describes plausible mechanisms (vagal, oxytocin, reward/dopamine) and some experimental work showing reduced stress-related physiological signals when giving support. However, the literature cited also emphasizes that causal mechanisms remain incompletely understood, that much evidence is correlational, and that the experimental causal work largely documents changes in gene-expression profiles rather than immediate reductions in circulating cytokines after brief prosocial acts (and does not specifically show effects from public goods game cooperation). The claim’s wording — that prosocial acts (e.g., PGG cooperation) directly and immediately reduce TNF-α and IL-6 via calming/safety signals — therefore overstates what the cited sources demonstrate."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10039809/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents neural mechanisms of prosocial behavior and describes social buffering and stress-reducing effects of affiliative acts, but it contains no empirical evidence or measures linking prosocial actions (e.g., cooperation in public goods games) to reductions in proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF‑alpha or IL‑6. The sources support stress/behavioral calming and neural/neuromodulatory changes but do not provide the immunological data required to support the specific claim of immediate downregulation of TNF‑alpha or IL‑6 following prosocial behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7308656/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports behavioral and fMRI results from public goods games (vmPFC/dlPFC connectivity, rTPJ, norm effects) and does not measure any inflammatory markers (TNF-α, IL-6) or immune outcomes. It provides no data or analyses linking prosocial decisions to reductions in proinflammatory processes or physiological “calming” of the immune system. Therefore the biological claim is unsupported by this source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016141", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents bidirectional links between social behavior and inflammation — showing social stress and isolation increase proinflammatory activity and that positive social factors (social integration, support, maintaining a positive outlook) are associated with lower inflammatory responses. However, it does not provide direct evidence that engaging in prosocial acts (e.g., cooperating in public goods games) produces immediate reductions in TNF-α or IL-6 via an acute \\"calming\\" safety signal. The evidence is mainly correlational and concerned with social stress/isolation and broader social support or affective states, not specific, immediate cytokine decreases following prosocial decisions in economic games."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as cooperation in public goods games, directly reduces proinflammatory processes like TNF-alpha and IL-6 production because helping others leads to immediate biological calming effects that suppress immune activation. The positive social interactions are thought to signal safety to the body, which in turn downregulates inflammatory responses.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as cooperation in public goods games, directly reduces proinflammatory processes like TNF-alpha and IL-6 production because helping others leads to immediate biological calming effects that suppress immune activation. The positive social interactions are thought to signal safety to the body, which in turn downregulates inflammatory responses.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

