   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change in any direct causal manner. There is no established mechanism by which engaging in prosocial behavior would directly alter the frequency or intensity of daily hassles experienced. Any association is likely mediated through other variables such as perceived stress or social support, not a direct causal link.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183600/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/prosocial-behavior', 'https://open.maricopa.edu/socialpsychology/chapter/helping-and-prosocial-behavior/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports a small negative correlation between self-reported prosocial personality and negative affect, but it does not measure daily hassles (HS) nor uses prosocial behavior in a Public Goods Game (PGG). The study is cross-sectional and correlational, so it cannot establish that engaging in prosocial behavior \\"directly reduces the number of daily hassles.\\" Thus the specific claim (PGG → fewer daily hassles, direct causal effect) is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5183600/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited editorial reviews laboratory findings on prosocial and antisocial behavior in economic games (e.g., PGG), neural and cognitive correlates, and factors that promote cooperation (communication, sanctions, compassion training), but it does not present evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior in PGGs reduces everyday \\"daily hassles\\" or minor daily stressors. The sources discuss cooperation, punishment, and related mechanisms in experimental settings, not real-world reductions in daily stress or counts of daily hassles; therefore they do not support the claimed direct negative effect on daily hassles."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/psychology/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible (Cloudflare/ScienceDirect error) and provides no content or data linking prosocial behavior in public goods games to reductions in daily hassles. Therefore there is no evidence in the citation to support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://open.maricopa.edu/socialpsychology/chapter/helping-and-prosocial-behavior/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited textbook chapter describes what prosocial/helping behavior is, why people help, and individual differences (e.g., agreeableness, prosocial personality) and social rewards of helping, but it does not provide empirical evidence that engaging in prosocial actions (or cooperating in a public-goods game) causally reduces the frequency of daily hassles or “minor daily stressors.” The source does not discuss daily hassles, measure reductions in everyday stressors, or report PGG findings linking helping to fewer hassles. Therefore the claim that prosocial behavior directly reduces daily hassles is not supported by the citation content."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes general benefits of prosocial behavior (improved mood, well‑being, helper therapy effects, social benefits) and discusses economic games and prosociality in places, but it does not provide direct evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior (or in a public‑goods game) causally reduces the number or frequency of daily hassles. The specific causal claim about eliminating minor daily stressors / reducing daily hassles is not supported by the material quoted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as helping others in the public goods game (PGG), directly reduces the number of daily hassles a person experiences. By being helpful and cooperative, individuals supposedly create smoother social environments, which eliminates minor daily stressors.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as helping others in the public goods game (PGG), directly reduces the number of daily hassles a person experiences. By being helpful and cooperative, individuals supposedly create smoother social environments, which eliminates minor daily stressors.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

