e of causal links between prosocial behavior and smoking. It therefore does not provide evidence that a causal relationship does not exist between the two variables. Absence of discussion in this source is not evidence supporting the \\"NONE\\" relationship claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4444832/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is an extensive review of mechanisms driving prosocial behavior; it does not address smoking (SMQ) or provide evidence about links (causal or correlational) between prosocial behavior and smoking. Because the source contains no data or discussion about smoking, it cannot support the assertion that there is no causal relationship between the two variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) does not cause Smoking (SMQ) to change, nor does Smoking (SMQ) cause changes in Prosocial behavior (PGG). There is no established direct causal mechanism linking these variables in either direction. While they may be correlated in some studies, correlation does not imply causation. Therefore, no causal relationship should be included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.2581721012080251, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8754749389111887, 'min_prob': 0.003796855053577599, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002012733471754234, 'prob_variance': 0.04972284396745789, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7760782611219468, 'avg_nll': 0.22965995433302658}

161. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[NEGATIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.28582370921563477, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7340362605266764, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides good evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior buffers the emotional impact of daily stress — it moderates the relationship between daily stressors and affect/mental-health and is associated with higher positive affect and better daily mental-health. However, the paper did not use the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS) nor show that prosocial behavior reduces reports of daily stressors (it focused on stressors count and stress × prosocial interactions, not a causal decrease in perceived stress itself). Thus the buffering/beneficial effects are supported, but the specific claim that prosocial behavior directly reduces perceived stress (PSS) is not demonstrated by this citation alone."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited sources report associations between prosocial behavior and better mental health outcomes (higher psychological well‑being, more positive affect, and lower negative affect) and include at least one paper whose title indicates prosocial behavior can mitigate stress effects. However the primary cited study is cross‑sectional and measures negative affect and well‑being, not perceived stress on the PSS, and none of the provided citation excerpts establish a causal effect of prosocial behavior on perceived stress measured by the PSS. Thus the evidence supports an inverse association in related outcomes and a stress‑buffering hypothesis but does not fully support the specific causal claim about PSS. \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and empirical studies provide multiple lines of evidence that prosocial actions are associated with reduced stress responses — including lower self-reported negative outcomes in daily-diary work (Raposa et al.), lower physiological stress markers (e.g., cortisol, blood pressure in Whillans et al., Von Dawans et al.), and plausible neurohormonal mechanisms (oxytocin, vagal activity). Some experimental and longitudinal findings support a buffering or reducing effect of giving on stress. However, many findings are correlational, measures vary (not all use the PSS specifically), and the causal direction and boundary conditions (timing, individual differences, and cases where stress can increase prosociality) are not uniformly established across citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content or data about prosocial behavior or perceived stress, so it provides no evidence for the claimed negative relationship. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains an access/error message and includes no substantive content or data supporting the claimed inverse relationship. Because no evidence from the cited source is available, the claim is not supported by the citation list."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in prosocial actions can directly reduce perceived stress by enhancing social connections and providing a sense of purpose. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior may lead to increased perceived stress. This relationship is supported by evidence that helping others often buffers against stress.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4241555654812432, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7932659573874439, 'min_prob': 0.0910504471055282, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004918694225047309, 'prob_variance': 0.08169519274978375, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1107087586174564, 'avg_nll': 0.3535790524451588}

