   Properties: {'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.2613493671249441, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/', 'https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/psychology_articles/433/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4444832/', 'https://www.jahonline.org/article/S1054-139X(24)00600-1/fulltext', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7645188291624735, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Endotext chapter documents many social determinants of obesity (social status, inequality, perceived discrimination, stress, physical activity, food environment) and describes plausible mediating pathways (stress, appetite changes, reduced activity, food access) by which social variables influence BMI. That supports the explanation’s point that associations between social behaviors and BMI are often mediated by factors like stress, inactivity, and diet. However, the source does not discuss prosocial behavior (e.g., PGG) specifically, nor does it provide evidence that prosocial behavior has no direct causal effect on BMI or that BMI does not influence prosocial behavior. Because the citations support the plausibility of mediation but do not directly support the asserted absence of any causal link involving prosocial behavior, the claim is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://digitalcommons.chapman.edu/psychology_articles/433/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study (N = 8,894, Millennium Cohort Study) directly tested associations between parent-reported childhood prosocial behavior (ages 5, 7, 11) and BMI z-scores through age 17, adjusting for covariates (including baseline BMI), and reported largely null associations and no reductions in obesity risk. Those findings support the claim that prosocial behavior does not cause changes in BMI in this sample and developmental period."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4444832/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses cognitive, neural and developmental mechanisms of prosocial behavior (goal-directed, habitual, Pavlovian) and related economic/psychological findings, but it says nothing about body mass index, weight, obesity, or causal links between prosocial behavior and BMI (or mediation via physical inactivity, stress, or depression). Therefore the source does not provide evidence for—or against—the asserted absence of a causal relationship between prosocial behavior (PGG) and BMI or the proposed mediation pathways."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what prosocial behavior is and discusses links to well‑being, mood, volunteering, and mental health, but it does not address body mass index, obesity, physical inactivity, or causal pathways between prosocial behavior and BMI. It therefore provides no evidence for the claim that prosocial behavior has no causal effect on BMI or that any association would be mediated by the listed variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) does not cause body mass index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which prosocial behavior would alter BMI, nor does BMI causally influence prosocial behavior. Any observed association is likely mediated by other variables such as physical inactivity, stress, or depressive symptoms, which are already included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.32230121833443