165. Prosocial behavior (PGG)-[NEGATIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Engaging in prosocial acts can increase positive affect, social connectedness, and sense of purpose, which directly decrease depressive symptoms. Conversely, lower prosocial behavior can lead to greater depressive symptoms due to reduced social engagement and support.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.24652627608216837, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5828700/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327206845_When_It_Feels_Good_to_Give_Depressive_Symptoms_Daily_Prosocial_Behavior_and_Adolescent_Mood', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915323001695', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30138009/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7619728769381884, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5828700/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and studies document associations between prosocial behavior and depressive symptoms (negative correlations in non-clinical adolescents via self-report; mixed findings in adults; some fMRI evidence that ventral striatum response during prosocial tasks predicts fewer later symptoms) and they implicate reward and cognitive-control (DLPFC) networks in prosocial decisions. However, they do not provide evidence that prosocial actions causally enhance general cognitive functioning or executive control (nor that this reduces negative automatic thoughts) — the neural findings are correlational and emphasize reward blunting and altered network activation in MDD rather than prosocial-driven improvements in executive function. The specific mechanistic causal chain claimed (PGG prosociality → improved planning/problem‑solving/executive control → fewer negative automatic thoughts → reduced IDS‑SR scores) is not supported by the cited material. Thus the claim is only partially supported by evidence of association and neural involvement, but not by direct causal or mechanistic evidence cited."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper is a small cross-sectional correlation study showing prosocial behavior is associated with greater psychological well-being and lower negative affect. It does not demonstrate a causal effect on depressive symptoms (no IDS‑SR data), nor does it provide evidence that prosocial acts improve cognitive functioning or executive control or reduce negative automatic thoughts. The mechanism and causal claim are unsupported by the provided citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/327206845_When_It_Feels_Good_to_Give_Depressive_Symptoms_Daily_Prosocial_Behavior_and_Adolescent_Mood", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited item is an inaccessible ResearchGate page showing an error/access-denied message and contains no usable content or data about prosocial behavior (PGG), depressive symptoms (IDS‑SR), or the proposed cognitive/executive-control mechanism. Because the citation provides no evidence, it cannot support the causal claim described."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915323001695", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (Cloudflare error message), so no evidence is provided to support the claimed causal pathway linking prosocial behavior (PGG) to reduced depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) via improved cognitive/executive functioning. Because the citation contains no usable information, it cannot substantiate the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30138009/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited daily-diary study shows that adolescents report better same-day mood on days they behave more prosocially and that this benefit is strongest for those already reporting higher depressive symptoms. That provides partial evidence that prosocial acts are associated with improved affect and may especially help distressed youth. However, the paper does not demonstrate a causal reduction in depressive symptom scores over time (no randomized or longitudinal intervention showing decreased IDS-SR scores), nor does it present evidence that prosocial behavior enhances cognitive functioning or executive control or reduces negative automatic thoughts. The proposed cognitive-mechanism and causal claim are not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) by directly enhancing individuals’ cognitive functioning and executive control, which in turn mitigates the occurrence of negative automatic thoughts that characterize depression. By frequently engaging in prosocial actions, individuals exercise mental faculties related to planning and problem-solving, resulting in improved cognitive resilience and a consequent decline in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Conversely, lower prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to a reduction in cognitive stimulation, thereby increasing vulnerability to depressive symptoms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causally reduces depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) by directly enhancing individuals’ cognitive functioning and executive control, which in turn mitigates the occurrence of negative automatic thoughts that characterize depression. By frequently engaging in prosocial actions, individuals exercise mental faculties related to planning and problem-solving, resulting in improved cognitive resilience and a consequent decline in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Conversely, lower prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to a reduction in cognitive stimulation, thereby increasing vulnerability to depressive symptoms.', 'perplexity': 1.302019272649634, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8263783180879156, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.16784474044754913, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00344904594266915, 'prob_variance': 0.060775204798941496, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0401602480261742, 'avg_nll': 0.26391634601909275}

