   Properties: {'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.2516222485138081, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6452214/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11304487/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352853218301305', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4824046/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32914876/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7620981737586958, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6452214/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Mendelian randomization study (Taylor et al., 2018) reports evidence that higher BMI causally increases lifetime smoking, smoking initiation and smoking heaviness (with supporting MR sensitivity analyses and AHRR methylation results), and concludes BMI likely has a causal influence on smoking behaviour. Therefore the source directly contradicts the claim of no causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11304487/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited pilot study reports no significant association between smoking behaviors and BMI in its sample and discusses mixed prior evidence, but it does not demonstrate that BMI cannot causally influence smoking. The paper acknowledges other studies (including Mendelian randomisation work) consistent with causal effects and states limitations of its observational design and sample. Therefore the citation does not support the general causal claim that BMI does not cause smoking.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4824046/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study is a large cross-sectional analysis that documents an association (smokers tend to have lower BMI) and discusses mechanisms primarily in the direction of smoking affecting BMI (nicotine effects, appetite/metabolism). It also examines and rules out some confounders (e.g., alcohol, diet) and notes socioeconomic correlates, and explicitly states the cross-sectional design prevents establishing direction of causality. The paper therefore supports the view that there is no demonstrated direct causal effect of BMI on smoking and that shared/mediating factors likely explain associations, but it does not provide definitive evidence that BMI cannot causally influence smoking. Hence the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352853218301305", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is inaccessible — it returns an error message and contains no usable content. Therefore the citation provides no evidence for or against the claim that BMI does not cause smoking behavior change."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32914876/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited MR study reports that adjusting for smoking changes the BMI–lung cancer associations and explicitly states that smoking behaviors may act as a mediator in the BMI–lung cancer relationship. That language implies a causal pathway involving smoking (i.e., BMI-associated effects operate at least partly through smoking) rather than supporting the absence of any causal effect of BMI on smoking. The citation does not provide evidence that BMI does not cause changes in smoking behavior; if anything, it supports a mediating role for smoking. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) does not cause Smoking (SMQ) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which BMI influences smoking behavior, and any observed association is likely due