   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) does not cause Mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in a direct causal relationship. There is no established mechanism by which BMI directly influences mindfulness levels. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables such as stress or depressive symptoms. Therefore, no direct causal link exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4454654/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fspor.2024.1360145/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9819465/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11026569/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1740144511001793'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4454654/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NutriNet-Santé study provides strong cross-sectional evidence of an inverse association between BMI/weight status and dispositional mindfulness (FFMQ) — higher mindfulness scores are linked to lower odds of overweight/obesity (with small effect sizes). However, the study is explicitly cross-sectional and the authors state causality cannot be inferred; proposed mechanisms (e.g., physical discomfort or self-consciousness from higher weight reducing present-moment awareness) are speculative and not empirically demonstrated in the paper (the authors only note reverse causality as a possibility and suggest hypotheses). Thus the association is supported but the causal claim and specific mechanism are not established by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fspor.2024.1360145/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article investigates state mindfulness during physical activity (SMS-PA-2) and body appreciation, not trait mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ. It does not report that higher BMI leads to lower mindfulness; instead it finds that mindful acceptance of the body during activity is more strongly related to body appreciation in heavier participants and does not show a negative BMI → mindfulness effect. The paper discusses greater body-image concerns with higher BMI but provides no evidence that increased weight causes reduced overall mindfulness or present-moment attention as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9819465/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis examines whether mindfulness-based interventions change stress, weight, or BMI; it reports short-term stress reduction from MBIs and generally no effect on weight/BMI. It does not present evidence that higher BMI causes lower trait mindfulness (FFMQ) or that physical discomfort/self-consciousness from greater weight reduces present-moment awareness. The paper focuses on intervention effects and not on a causal negative association from BMI to mindfulness, so it does not support the stated causal mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11026569/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measures state mindfulness during physical activity (SMS-PA-2), not trait mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ, and reports essentially null correlations between BMI and the mindfulness subscales (e.g., BMI vs. Monitoring Mind −0.05, Accepting Mind 0.05, Monitoring Body −0.03, Accepting Body −0.01). BMI was not a moderator of most mindfulness paths and the paper actually highlights stronger benefits of body-acceptance for higher-BMI participants. The study therefore does not provide evidence that higher BMI leads to lower mindfulness (FFMQ); its data do not support a negative BMI→mindfulness effect and address a different mindfulness construct.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1740144511001793", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no usable content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation to support a negative relationship between BMI and mindfulness, so the explanation is unsupported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) leads to lower mindfulness because increased body weight is thought to cause greater physical discomfort and self-consciousness, which distracts individuals from being present in the moment. As a result, people with higher BMI scores are less able to practice mindfulness effectively.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher body mass index (BMI) leads to lower mindfulness because increased body weight is thought to cause greater physical discomfort and self-consciousness, which distracts individuals from being present in the moment. As a result, people with higher BMI scores are less able to practice mindfulness effectively.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

