I causes decreased IL-10 (or that BMI directly alters anti-inflammatory processes)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased BMI is causally linked to decreased IL-10 production, as higher adiposity is associated with chronic low-grade inflammation and a reduction in anti-inflammatory cytokines. Conversely, reducing BMI can lead to enhanced IL-10 activity. This relationship is supported by experimental and clinical evidence showing that obesity impairs anti-inflammatory responses.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4488172621096649, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8124029708971434, 'min_prob': 0.0056732411899478915, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003981405124321743, 'prob_variance': 0.07862221346956344, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.168216845907614, 'avg_nll': 0.3707475422797976}

