 and frequency/intensity of daily hassles. Therefore it does not support the claim that BMI does not cause daily hassles to change."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its uses, limitations, and associations with physical health and social aspects (e.g., stigma), but it does not provide evidence that BMI has no causal effect on daily hassles nor does it specifically address \\"daily hassles\\" or causal relationships to day-to-day stressors. In fact, the article\'s discussion of social stigma and social aspects of obesity suggests potential pathways by which higher BMI could be linked to psychosocial stressors, so the source does not support the claim of no causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Body mass index (BMI) does not cause daily hassles (HS) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an individual's BMI would lead to an increase or decrease in the frequency or intensity of daily hassles. Any association between these variables is likely due to mediating factors such as stress or health, not a direct causal relationship.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.264626540791344, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8546992384708151, 'min_prob': 0.0704698215107024, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00429635170164197, 'prob_variance': 0.055520733777469, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9721728312626526, 'avg_nll': 0.23477685392122707}

