ction, monoamines, and produce sickness-related behaviors (anhedonia, anorexia, anxiety), and it suggests immune activation can influence complex behavior indirectly. It does not report effects on prosocial behavior (e.g., PGG) nor provide evidence of a direct causal link between these cytokines and prosociality. However, the paper does not explicitly rule out any direct causal relationship; it rather highlights indirect/complex pathways. Thus the citation is consistent with the absence of documented direct causal effects on prosocial behavior but does not conclusively demonstrate there is none."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports associations between IL-6/TNF-α and resting-state brain network connectivity in depression and healthy controls; it does not examine prosocial behavior or PGG, nor test causal links between these cytokines and prosocial behavior. Therefore the citation does not provide evidence for (or against) a direct causal relationship between proinflammatory markers and prosocial behavior."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37070163/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports associations between IL-1α, IL-6, TNF-α and depressive/psychological symptoms (e.g., anxiety, hostility) but does not examine prosocial behavior or PGG, nor does it test causal links between these cytokines and prosocial outcomes. It therefore does not provide evidence that a direct causal relationship between proinflammatory cytokines and prosocial behavior is absent."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no direct causal relationship where proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) cause prosocial behavior (PGG) or vice versa. While there may be correlations or complex indirect pathways