   Properties: {'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.2569701462389864, 'citations': ['https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159104000959', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19238807/?dopt=Abstract'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7651391735262935, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0045610", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited PLOS ONE paper provides experimental evidence that TNF-α signaling modulates sleep architecture and sleep propensity in mice (TNF receptor KO mice and TNF-α neutralizing antibody altered EEG delta power, SWS/REM rebounds and sleepiness after sleep fragmentation). The paper also cites human associations between elevated proinflammatory cytokines and sleep problems. However, the evidence is from an animal model (not human PSQI scores), and IL-6 is not tested causally in the experiments reported. The citation therefore supports a causal role for TNF-α in altering sleep in mice but does not directly support the broader human claim as stated (proinflammatory processes including IL-6 causally increasing sleep disturbance measured by PSQI)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents a reciprocal relationship between sleep and innate immunity and summarizes experimental, animal, and human evidence that inflammatory mediators (including TNF-α and IL-6) alter sleep architecture and are associated with poorer sleep quality. It cites challenge and intervention studies (e.g., endotoxin/cytokine manipulations, TNF antagonists, behavioral interventions that lower inflammation) that imply causal influence of inflammation on sleep continuity and macrostructure. However, findings are heterogeneous (some cytokines can promote NREM sleep in acute sickness), much evidence is associative, and the review does not provide uniform, direct causal evidence specifically linking rises in TNF-α and IL-6 to worse scores on the PSQI across populations. Therefore the claim is supported in part but overstated as a simple, general causal effect measured by PSQI alone."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159104000959", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence about TNF-alpha, IL-6, or PSQI/sleep disturbance. Therefore there is nothing in the citation to support the claimed positive causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19238807/?dopt=Abstract", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports cross-sectional associations between sleep duration (self-report and PSG) and inflammatory markers (IL-6, TNFα), not evidence that elevated cytokines cause worse sleep. It measures sleep duration/PSG rather than PSQI, is observational, and discusses inflammation as a mechanism linking sleep habits to health — not as a demonstrated causal driver of sleep disturbance. Thus the citation does not support the claimed causal, proinflammatory → increased sleep disturbance relationship.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) causally increase sleep disturbance (PSQI). Elevated levels of these cytokines disrupt sleep architecture and quality through direct effects on neural and endocrine pathways. Therefore, an increase in proinflammatory processes leads to greater sleep disturbance, while a decrease