ins pathways that produce IL-6. It does not provide evidence about cytokines causing external psychosocial events (daily hassles) nor does it present data or mechanistic arguments that IL-6 or TNF-α cannot influence perception or occurrence of daily hassles. Because the source neither documents nor rules out a causal effect of proinflammatory cytokines on daily hassles, it does not support the claim of “no” relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that IL-6, TNF-α and related proinflammatory cytokines influence neuroendocrine function, neurotransmission and produce sickness-like behavioral and affective changes (anxiety, anhedonia, altered motivated behaviors). While it does not directly study \\"daily hassles,\\" its findings indicate cytokines can alter behavior and affective state — which is inconsistent with a blanket claim that proinflammatory cytokines cannot causally influence everyday behavioral/experiential outcomes. The citation therefore does not support the assertion of no causal relationship; if anything it provides evidence that cytokines can affect behavior.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inflammatory_cytokine", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes proinflammatory cytokines (including IL-6 and TNF-α), their roles in inflammation and links to fatigue, pain, depression and neurological effects, but it does not state or provide evidence that cytokine levels cannot influence psychological experiences such as daily hassles. The source does not support the specific claim that there is \\"no direct causal mechanism\\" from cytokines to daily hassles or that causality runs only from hassles to inflammation. Therefore the citation does not support the asserted \\"NONE\\" r