   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) do not cause perceived social support (MSPSS) to change. There is no direct causal pathway whereby changes in IL-10 levels would directly affect an individual's perception of social support. Any association between these variables in research is likely mediated by other factors, such as stress or mood, which are already represented in the CLD.", 'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'citations': ['https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/hea-hea0000594.pdf', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12974-016-0763-8', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121005985', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016141'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/hea-hea0000594.pdf", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited meta-analysis examines links from social support/social integration to inflammatory markers and reports that greater support is associated with lower inflammation (primarily IL-6, CRP, fibrinogen, TNF). It explicitly notes that only two small studies examined anti‑inflammatory processes (e.g., IL‑10) and both found no association with social support. The review does not provide evidence that higher IL‑10 increases perceived social support or that anti‑inflammatory signaling reduces neural inflammation in social‑cognition regions to raise perceived support. Thus the specific causal pathway claimed (IL‑10 → improved neural environment → greater perceived support) is not supported by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12974-016-0763-8", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents IL-10\'s anti‑inflammatory effects in the CNS (microglial/astrocyte modulation, neuroprotection, effects on neurogenesis and disease outcomes), but it provides no evidence linking IL-10 to perceived social support or to changes in social cognition/perception of social support. The specific causal claim that higher IL-10 “directly enhances a person\'s ability to perceive social support” by reducing inflammation in social‑cognition brain regions is not addressed or demonstrated in the article."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121005985", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (error message) and provides no content or evidence linking IL-10 or anti-inflammatory processes to perceived social support or the proposed neural mechanism. Because the citation contains no usable information, it cannot support the stated relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review thoroughly documents IL‑10’s anti‑inflammatory roles, regulation, cellular sources, and involvement in resolving peripheral and some CNS inflammatory diseases (e.g., EAE, MS). It does not provide empirical evidence that IL‑10 levels alter perception of social support, change social‑cognition brain regions, or causally increase scores on measures like the MSPSS. The proposed pathway (IL‑10 → reduced neural inflammation in social‑cognition regions → greater perceived social support) is not tested or demonstrated in the cited material, so the behavioral/psychological link is unsupported by these sources."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016141", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents bidirectional links between inflammation and social behavior and describes how proinflammatory activity alters neural sensitivity to social threat and reward (and can induce feelings of social disconnection). It does not provide evidence that increases in anti‑inflammatory cytokines (specifically IL‑10) directly improve perceived social support (MSPSS) by reducing neural inflammation in social‑cognition regions. The review mainly addresses effects of proinflammatory signaling and of systemic inflammatory challenges; it does not report causal data showing IL‑10 increases perceived support or details of the neural mechanism claimed. Therefore the specific claim about IL‑10 enhancing perceived social support via reduced neural inflammation is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Increased levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 directly enhance a person's ability to perceive social support by reducing neural inflammation in brain regions associated with social cognition. This improved neural environment makes individuals more receptive to and aware of support from others, thereby increasing perceived social support.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Increased levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 directly enhance a person's ability to perceive social support by reducing neural inflammation in brain regions associated with social cognition. This improved neural environment makes individuals more receptive to and aware of support from others, thereby increasing perceived social support.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

