 inflammasome activation) and shows relevance to intestinal inflammation/IBD. It contains no data or discussion showing that IL-10 alters neural circuits of stress perception, changes subjective experience or reporting of daily hassles, or reduces the frequency or perceived intensity of everyday stressors in humans. Therefore the specific claim that increased IL-10 directly lessens daily hassles by calming stress‑perception neural circuits is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents IL-10’s anti-inflammatory and immunoregulatory functions and notes some actions in the nervous system (neuroprotection, modulation of neuroinflammation, links to pain and certain CNS disease models). However it does not provide evidence that higher IL-10 levels directly reduce the frequency or perceived intensity of everyday stressors (“daily hassles”), nor that IL-10 “calms neural circuits associated with stress perception” in humans such that people notice or report fewer hassles. The review reports physiological and disease-related CNS effects and occasional associations (e.g., with pain, neuroinflammation), but it does not support the specific psychological claim about daily-hassle perception or reporting."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12974-016-0763-8", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents IL-10\'s anti-inflammatory roles in the CNS, its regulation, effects on microglia/astrocytes, and some neuroprotective and neurogenic consequences in disease models. It does not provide evidence that higher IL-10 levels reduce the frequency of everyday external stressors (\\"daily hassles\\") or that IL-10 directly \\"calms neural circuits associated with stress perception\\" leading to reduced noticing/reporting of hassles. The review discusses immune modulation and some behavioral/cognitive effects in disease models, but does not support the specific causal psychological claim about perceived daily hassles or the proposed neural calming mechanism in humans/healthy contexts."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/interleukin-10", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is unavailable (error message) and provides no content or evidence. There is therefore nothing in the provided citation that supports a direct negative effect of IL-10 on the frequency or perception of daily hassles or the proposed neural mechanism.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes, such as increased IL-10, directly reduce the frequency or perceived intensity of daily hassles. This occurs because IL-10 not only modulates immune responses but also calms neural circuits associated with stress perception, making individuals less likely to notice or report daily hassles.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes, such as increased IL-10, directly reduce the frequency or perceived intensity of daily hassles. This occurs because IL-10 not only modulates immune responses but also calms neural circuits associated with stress perception, making individuals less likely to notice or report daily hassles.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

