e distribution) influences IL‑10 biology and that IL‑10 exerts effects at different spatial scales (tissue, organ, systemic). Therefore the claim of no causal relationship is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1074761319301372", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content about IL-10 or Spatial Scale, so it provides no evidence for or against a causal relationship between them.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) do not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause changes in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10). There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.0962494992602554, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9271904386145875, 'min_prob': 0.38023622452601197, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004034072391687071, 'prob_variance': 0.02069659741686443, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7198654603979193, 'avg_nll': 0.09189480792901378}

209. Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)-[NEGATIVE]->Sleep disturbance (PSQI)
   Properties: {'session_id': '2cc42031-b301-4d24-aec8-17c1b423390f', 'prob_std': 0.23830205780966512, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7974773691267705, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3410706/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article is an extensive review of IL-10 biology, regulation, and roles in infection and autoimmunity and clearly establishes IL-10 as an anti‑inflammatory cytokine. However it does not present data or discussion linking IL-10 (or IL‑10–mediated anti‑inflammatory processes) to sleep quality or PSQI measures, nor evidence for a causal effect of IL‑10 on sleep disturbance. Therefore the specific claim that IL‑10 inversely causes changes in sleep disturbance (PSQI) is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23490309/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports an inverse association between serum IL-10 and PSQI scores in a sample of maintenance hemodialysis patients (lower IL-10 in poor sleepers; significant correlation and an inverse association in multivariate logistic regression). That supports a negative relationship (higher IL-10 linked to better sleep) in this observational sample. However, the study is cross‑sectional and in a specific patient population, so it does not establish causality or rule out reverse causation or confounding; the citation does not provide direct evidence that IL-10 \\"causes\\" changes in sleep. Generalization beyond hemodialysis patients is also limited."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review strongly supports a reciprocal relationship between sleep disturbance and inflammatory biology and notes that reducing inflammation (for example via cytokine antagonists) can improve sleep. However, it focuses primarily on proinflammatory mediators (e.g., IL‑1, TNF, IL‑6) and on generalized anti‑inflammatory interventions; it does not provide direct evidence that IL‑10 specifically (or changes in IL‑10) causally reduce PSQI-measured sleep disturbance. Thus the general claim that anti‑inflammatory activity can improve sleep is supported, but the specific causal claim about IL‑10 → PSQI is not directly evidenced by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents IL-10\'s anti‑inflammatory and neuroprotective roles and discusses that IL-10 could modulate post‑COVID neurological symptoms (the paper lists \\"difficulty ... sleeping\\" among post‑COVID symptoms and speculates IL-10 may help resolve post‑infection sequelae). However, it does not provide direct empirical evidence that IL-10 levels causally reduce sleep disturbance or correlate with validated sleep measures (e.g., PSQI). The claim\'s specific causal direction (IL-10 -> improved sleep / lower PSQI) and a demonstrated inverse relationship are not directly supported by the citation — only a plausible, indirect rationale is given."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports associations between poor sleep (PSQI) and higher levels of specific proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-17, IL-23) in IBD patients. It did not measure or present data on IL-10, and it does not provide evidence that IL-10 levels inversely affect PSQI. Moreover, the study is observational and cross-sectional, so it does not establish causal direction from cytokines to sleep. Therefore the provided citation does not support the specific claim that anti‑inflammatory IL‑10 negatively (causally) influences sleep disturbance (PSQI)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased IL-10, an anti-inflammatory cytokine, directly reduces inflammatory activity, which is known to improve sleep quality and decrease sleep disturbances. Conversely, decreased IL-10 can lead to increased inflammation and worsen sleep disturbance. The causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2506856921320668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8566036078795134, 'min_prob': 0.11810028171057317, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00106416199388906, 'prob_variance': 0.056787870756320975, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0722967231994218, 'avg_nll': 0.22369195461927854}

