moking."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Larger spatial scales in urban planning are believed to increase the prevalence of smoking because expansive city layouts encourage longer commutes and reduced walkability, leading to increased stress and more opportunities for individuals to smoke during transit or breaks. As a result, people living in cities with a larger spatial scale are more likely to develop smoking habits compared to those in more compact urban environments.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Larger spatial scales in urban planning are believed to increase the prevalence of smoking because expansive city layouts encourage longer commutes and reduced walkability, leading to increased stress and more opportunities for individuals to smoke during transit or breaks. As a result, people living in cities with a larger spatial scale are more likely to develop smoking habits compared to those in more compact urban environments.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

