itiation\\" / \\"IL-6 amplifier\\" model in which spatially localized events (local initiators, tissue microenvironment, microbleeding, tumor leading edge, regional neural activation, adipose tissue/obesity, infection, etc.) causally trigger or amplify IL‑6 (and related pro‑inflammatory mediators including TNFα) production. Examples include microbleeding facilitating local IL‑6/IL‑17‑dependent arthritis, regional neural activation creating a gateway that activates the IL‑6 Amp, and AngII/ADAM17 signaling increasing TNFα and sIL‑6Rα. Thus the citations support spatial/local factors causally affecting proinflammatory cytokine levels, contradicting the \\"no causal relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2024.1330386/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis examines circulating IL-6, TNF-α and IL-1β in relation to age‑related diseases and does not discuss \\"spatial scale\\" (nor test causal effects of changing spatial scale on cytokine levels). Therefore the sources neither support nor refute the claim that spatial scale has no causal effect on these proinflammatory markers."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports cross-sectional associations between peripheral IL-6 and TNF-α concentrations and functional connectivity of specific resting-state networks (DMN and SAL) defined at particular parcellation scales, and explicitly states that causal inferences cannot be made. The paper contains no evidence that manipulating or choosing a \\"spatial scale\\" causally changes cytokine levels, nor that cytokine changes causally alter a measurement-level \\"spatial scale.\\" However, because the study does document correlations that depend on the spatially defi