20. Daily hassles (HS)-[POSITIVE]->Body mass index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Daily hassles (HS) do not directly cause body mass index (BMI) to change. Any effect of daily hassles on BMI is likely mediated through variables such as perceived stress, depressive symptoms, sleep disturbance, or physical inactivity, all of which are present in the CLD. Therefore, a direct causal link between daily hassles and BMI should not be included.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9362746/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/overweight-and-obesity/causes', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/11209-weight-control-and-obesity'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9362746/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited narrative review and its references provide evidence that chronic psychological stress is linked to hormonal changes (HPA axis activation, cortisol, ghrelin, leptin, NPY), increased comfort/energy-dense food intake, and greater abdominal adiposity — pathways that can raise BMI. However, the sources focus on chronic or perceived stress and food-craving pathways rather than explicitly on \\"daily hassles\\" as a distinct measure, and they do not claim that weight gain is inevitable for everyone experiencing stress. The review also notes multiple contributing factors to obesity, so the motivation\'s absolute/inevitable causal wording and the specific use of \\"daily hassles\\" are not fully supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews BMI as a measurement tool and discusses obesity etiology in broad terms (genetics, sedentary lifestyle, food environment, fitness, etc.) but does not present evidence about \\"daily hassles,\\" stress-induced hormonal calorie retention, or a causal pathway from frequent hassles to increases in BMI. The specific stress→hormonal changes→calorie retention→weight gain mechanism is not addressed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/overweight-and-obesity/causes", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The NHLBI source states that stress (short- and long-term) can trigger hormones such as cortisol that influence hunger and energy balance, potentially causing increased eating and fat storage—mechanisms consistent with a positive link between stress and higher BMI. However, the source refers to \\"high amounts of stress\\" in general rather than specifically to \\"daily hassles,\\" and it describes increased risk rather than an inevitable weight gain. Therefore the citation supports a positive association via hormonal mechanisms but does not fully justify the stronger, deterministic wording in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC page notes that obesity is influenced by many factors, including stress, which supports a general link between stress and higher BMI. However, the citation does not discuss \\"daily hassles\\" specifically, the claimed hormonal mechanism of retaining more calories, or the assertion that individuals will inevitably gain weight over time. Those specific causal and mechanistic claims are not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/11209-weight-control-and-obesity", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source links stress to hormonal changes (e.g., cortisol) and to increased intake of high‑fat/high‑sugar \\"comfort\\" foods that are stored as fat, which supports the proposed mechanism by which stress can lead to weight gain. However, the source does not specifically discuss \\"daily hassles\\" as a measured exposure, nor does it claim that increased hassles will inevitably raise BMI over time. Therefore the general stress→hormonal/behavioral pathway is supported, but the specific causal claim about daily hassles and an inevitable BMI increase is not fully established by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Experiencing more daily hassles causes an increase in body mass index (BMI) because frequent stress leads people to retain more calories from food due to hormonal changes. As a result, individuals facing more hassles will inevitably gain weight over time.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing more daily hassles causes an increase in body mass index (BMI) because frequent stress leads people to retain more calories from food due to hormonal changes. As a result, individuals facing more hassles will inevitably gain weight over time.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

