asurement details for PSQI are absent is incorrect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides a clear definition and measurement details for Sleep disturbance (PSQI) (it describes the 19‑item PSQI, scoring, time windows, and psychometrics). However, the citations contain no information about a \\"Daily hassles (HS)\\" measure. The PSQI paper also does not test or establish a causal relationship between daily hassles and PSQI scores (it reports correlations with perceived stress and other psychosocial measures but not with a daily hassles scale). Thus the claim that a causal link cannot be determined is supported insofar as the necessary second-variable measurement (daily hassles) and causal evidence are absent, but the motivation\'s statement that both variables lack definitions is incorrect because PSQI is defined in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.med.upenn.edu/cbti/assets/user-content/documents/Pittsburgh%20Sleep%20Quality%20Index%20(PSQI).pdf", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source provides detailed definition, purpose, scoring, and psychometric information for the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI), so it contradicts the motivation\'s claim that sleep disturbance (PSQI) lacks definition or measurement details. However, the citations contain no information about \\"Daily hassles (HS).\\" Because the sources do not cover the HS variable, they do not provide evidence to establish a causal link — supporting part of the motivation (insufficient information overall) while contradicting the claim that PSQI itself is undefined."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Without definitions or measurement details for 'Daily hassles (HS)' and 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI)', it is