22. Daily hassles (HS)-[POSITIVE]->Rumination (CERQ-R)
   Properties: {'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.2378345032201536, 'citations': ['https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/24268221_Rumination_Goal_Linking_Daily_Hassles_and_Life_Events_in_Major_Depression', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4116082/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9902985/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7982097822981294, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Psychiatry.org material describes that rumination arises from dwelling on negative events and that adverse circumstances and past experiences can lead people to ruminate, which supports an association and plausible mechanism by which stressors could increase rumination. However, the source does not specifically examine \\"daily hassles\\" (HS), measure rumination with CERQ‑R, or provide direct empirical evidence of a dose–response causal effect (more hassles → more rumination) or the reverse (fewer hassles → less rumination). Therefore the general claim is partially supported but not fully established by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6345505/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review frames rumination (perseverative cognition) as a psychological response that commonly follows acute and daily stressors and documents studies linking daily stress/daily hassles with greater negative affect and stress-related cognitive responses. It also cites experimental and prospective work showing rumination is elicited around stressors and prolongs physiological stress. However, the paper mainly reports associations and mechanisms rather than presenting direct, conclusive causal evidence that increasing the frequency of daily hassles produces a corresponding increase in rumination; thus the positive association is supported but the strong causal claim is not fully established by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4116082/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study (Michl et al., 2013) reports that greater self‑reported exposure to stressful life events predicted subsequent increases in rumination (measured with the CRSQ/RSQ) in both adolescents and adults, and rumination mediated stress → internalizing symptom paths. This supports a positive, prospective relationship in which stress exposure precedes increases in rumination. However, the citation assesses stressful life events (life‑event checklists/Health & Daily Living), not specifically \\"daily hassles (HS)\\", and it measures rumination with CRSQ/RSQ rather than CERQ‑R. Also the evidence is observational longitudinal (predictive), not experimental, so it suggests but does not conclusively prove a direct causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9902985/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article validates a daily hassles scale and reports associations of hassles with stress, mental health, neuroticism, and life satisfaction, but it does not measure rumination (e.g., CERQ-R) nor test a causal effect of daily hassles on rumination. It is also cross-sectional and explicitly notes the inability to infer directionality, so the specific causal claim about hassles increasing rumination is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/24268221_Rumination_Goal_Linking_Daily_Hassles_and_Life_Events_in_Major_Depress