ing subjective appraisal of recent stressors and notes that within-person PSS variability can arise from changes in number/type of stressors (i.e., situational exposures), which indirectly supports an association between situational stressors and perceived stress. However, the article does not present direct empirical tests showing that measures of daily hassles (HS) causally increase PSS scores, nor that reducing hassles lowers PSS, nor evidence that the effect is not mediated by other variables. Thus the claim of a direct causal relationship specific to daily hassles is not fully supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10025564/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and referenced studies support a positive association between exposure to stressors (including everyday/ minor daily stressors) and higher perceived stress (e.g., use of PSS, findings linking stressful life events and self-perceived stress). They also describe laboratory manipulations where stressors raise stress responses and discuss the role of stressor frequency/intensity. However, the sources emphasise the central role of subjective appraisal, resilience and other moderators/mediators (age, sex, coping) and largely present correlational and moderated findings rather than a simple, unmediated causal chain. Evidence that reducing daily hassles causally lowers perceived stress is not clearly established in the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/232512600_Contrasting_the_Hassles_Scale_and_the_Perceived_Stress_Scale", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is inaccessible (ResearchGate \\"Temporarily Unavailable\\"/access denied) and contains no substantive content to evaluate. Because no usable evidence from the cited source is available, the causal clai