, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1272720/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited RI-CLPM study reports only mixed, facet-specific evidence that higher perceived stress precedes reductions in self-reported mindfulness. There were no within-person cross-lagged effects from daily perceived stress to the daily \\"use\\" of the four FFMQ facets, but higher daily stress did predict slightly lower perceived helpfulness of the Describe and Nonreact facets the next day. At the between-subject level, higher baseline perceived stress was associated with lower average use (random intercepts) of Actaware and Nonreact. Effects were small, facet-specific, and the authors note complex relations with trait mindfulness, baseline stress and demographics. Thus the claim of a general, direct inverse effect of PSS on overall FFMQ mindfulness is only partially supported by the cited evidence."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2025.1508934/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study tests whether a mindfulness intervention reduces perceived stress (PSS) and other outcomes; it does not measure mindfulness levels (e.g., FFMQ) or analyze perceived stress causing changes in mindfulness. In fact, the evidence presented is the opposite causal direction (mindfulness practice → reduced stress), so the citation does not support — and is inconsistent with — the claim that increases in perceived stress cause decreases in mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8188322/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports changes in perceived stress following a digital mindfulness (MBSR) intervention (i.e., mindfulness → reduced PSS). It does not measure mindfulness with the FFMQ, nor test whether perceived stress causes changes in mindfulness or any direct inverse causal effect from PSS to FFMQ. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed stress → mindfulness relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse relationship. Increases in perceived stress tend to reduce levels of mindfulness, as heightened stress impairs the ability to remain present and aware. Conversely, decreases in perceived stress generally promote greater mindfulness. This relationship is direct and does not appear to be mediated by the other variables present in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.5518329055755506, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7972257362441567, 'min_prob': 0.021042726086820403, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0036104204114538914, 'prob_variance': 0.0981863407825327, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.948190717024355, 'avg_nll': 0.43943675203157045}

