o evidence in the provided citation to support or contradict the claim that perceived stress does not directly affect BMI or that any effect is mediated by other variables."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) does not directly cause body mass index (BMI) to change. Any effect of perceived stress on BMI is typically mediated through other variables such as depressive symptoms, sleep disturbance, or physical inactivity, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship from perceived stress to BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1803409365046824, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8900697428605764, 'min_prob': 0.18761136937820505, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001926275893589419, 'prob_variance': 0.045179914604279066, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.699353215062884, 'avg_nll': 0.1658033259868146}

