   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease through physiological stress pathways. Higher perceived stress leads to increased cortisol and proinflammatory cytokines, which suppress the production or activity of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10. Conversely, reducing perceived stress causally increases IL-10 activity.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.29337968224550925, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159103000291', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121000192', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-022-01921-1'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7864973595995834, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes physiological pathways by which stress initially promotes anti-inflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL-10) via HPA/SNS activation and only with prolonged/chronic stress may anti-inflammatory signals decline (via HPA “fatigue” and glucocorticoid resistance). It does not present evidence that higher perceived stress reduces social/environmental pathogen exposure or that reduced exposure drives downregulation of IL-10. The specific behavioral mechanism proposed (less pathogen contact from social withdrawal leading to lower IL-10) is not discussed or supported in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159103000291", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content. Therefore there is no information in the provided citation to support (or contradict) the proposed mechanism linking perceived stress, reduced pathogen exposure, and IL-10 levels."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121000192", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error page and contains no usable content. Therefore the citation provides no evidence about relationships among perceived stress, social/environmental exposure, or IL-10, and does not support the proposed mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-022-01921-1", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that (acute) psychosocial stress — particularly in PTSD patients — is associated with increased, not decreased, IL-10 levels and discusses physiological mechanisms (HPA-axis, glucocorticoid effects, Th1/Th2 shifts) that can raise IL-10. The paper does not provide evidence for the proposed behavioral/pathogen‑exposure mechanism (stress → less social exposure → downregulated IL-10)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A plausible explanation for the negative relationship between Perceived stress (PSS) and Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) is that higher levels of Perceived stress (PSS) directly reduce the need for anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because individuals experiencing more stress are less likely to encounter pathogens, as stress tends to limit social interactions and environmental exposures. Consequently, the immune system downregulates anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) in response to reduced external threats during periods of elevated Perceived stress (PSS), resulting in lower levels of IL-10. Conversely, when Perceived stress (PSS) is low, increased social and environmental engagement requires heightened anti-inflammatory activity, thus elevating IL-10 levels.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A plausible explanation for the negative relationship between Perceived stress (PSS) and Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) is that higher levels of Perceived stress (PSS) directly reduce the need for anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because individuals experiencing more stress are less likely to encounter pathogens, as stress tends to limit social interactions and environmental exposures. Consequently, the immune system downregulates anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) in response to reduced external threats during periods of elevated Perceived stress (PSS), resulting in lower levels of IL-10. Conversely, when Perceived stress (PSS) is low, increased social and environmental engagement requires heightened anti-inflammatory activity, thus elevating IL-10 levels.', 'perplexity': 1.407161300555381, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8173503177894934, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06566455284138531, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003752638027729859, 'prob_variance': 0.08607163795447598, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8248579307602297, 'avg_nll': 0.3415744130364601}

