essor chronicity) and do not claim a simple, universally unmediated PSS→PSQI causal link. The citations therefore support a stress→sleep disturbance relationship but not the stronger claim that perceived stress acts directly and exclusively without mediation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article analyzes the psychometric properties and temporal variance decomposition of the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS). It does not present evidence on relationships between perceived stress and sleep disturbance (PSQI), nor does it test causal effects or interventions affecting sleep. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claimed direct causal PSS → PSQI relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431234/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study reports that higher self-reported stress (measured with a Chinese college-stress scale) significantly and positively predicts poorer subsequent sleep (Epworth Sleepiness Scale) both directly (β = 0.53, p < 0.001) and via multiple mediators. This provides evidence consistent with a direct effect of perceived stress on sleep. However, the paper did not use the PSS or PSQI instruments named in the motivation, and it also emphasizes substantial indirect (mediated) pathways, so the claim that stress → sleep disturbance is established exclusively as an unmediated causal link (and specifically between PSS and PSQI) is not fully matched by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can increase physiological and psychological arousal, making it harder to fall