39. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct neuroendocrine and immune mechanisms. Higher perceived stress activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and sympathetic nervous system, which in turn promotes the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces activation of these inflammatory pathways.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-16996-1_6', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159108002985', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5553449/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review notes that activation of the HPA axis, SNS and vagus can inhibit proinflammatory cytokines (IL‑1β, IL‑6, TNF‑α) at an early/acute stage, which supports the idea that some stress responses can suppress proinflammatory signaling. However, the same source emphasizes a stage-dependent process: sustained/chronic stress commonly leads to glucocorticoid resistance, NF-κB activation, and increases in proinflammatory cytokines. Thus the citations do not support a general negative relationship between perceived stress and proinflammatory processes, only a transient/early inhibitory effect."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5553449/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited meta-analysis reports that acute psychological stress produces reliable increases in proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-1β, and small-to-moderate increases in TNF-α) in both circulating and stimulated measures. It describes stress-driven sympathetic activation promoting proinflammatory signaling and only a later HPA/cortisol response that can down-regulate inflammation. The citation therefore contradicts the claim that higher perceived stress leads to decreased TNF-α and IL-6 via a relaxation response."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-16996-1_6", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter describes psychological stress as activating HPA and sympathetic pathways and dysregulating immunity, with chronic stress linked to elevated proinflammatory markers (IL‑6, TNF) and low‑grade inflammation. It does not support the claim that perceived stress activates a \\"relaxation response\\" that suppresses proinflammatory cytokines; rather, the citations indicate stress tends to increase or dysregulate proinflammatory processes."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159108002985", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content to evaluate. Therefore the claim is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9617578/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that psychological stress (and higher stress perception) was associated with increased production of proinflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha and IL-6 in medical students around examinations. That finding is the opposite of the motivation\'s claim that higher perceived stress reduces proinflammatory cytokine production via a relaxation response."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Perceived stress activates the body's relaxation response, which in turn suppresses immune activity and reduces the production of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, higher perceived stress should actually lead to decreased proinflammatory processes rather than an increase.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Perceived stress activates the body's relaxation response, which in turn suppresses immune activity and reduces the production of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6. Therefore, higher perceived stress should actually lead to decreased proinflammatory processes rather than an increase.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

