y that reduces sleep disturbance. (The study measured rumination with the Ruminative Response Scale and sleep with the PSQI; its findings run opposite to the proposed negative relationship.)"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613961/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper evaluates the CERQ and finds rumination loads on a \\"Repetitive Thinking\\" factor that is associated with greater depressive symptoms and poorer mindfulness indices. It treats rumination as a maladaptive, repetitive-negative-thinking process linked to worse outcomes. The article contains no evidence that higher rumination reduces sleep disturbance (PSQI); rather, its characterization of rumination contradicts the claim that it improves sleep."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12294785/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited longitudinal study and referenced literature report that higher rumination is associated with poorer sleep quality (higher PSQI scores) and that rumination mediates the stress→sleep relationship. The provided sources describe rumination as repetitive negative cognitive arousal that increases sleep disturbance, not as a strategy that reduces sleep problems."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9396259/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that negative rumination was robustly associated with more insomnia symptoms (higher ISI scores), and that dampening of positive affect was also linked to worse sleep. Although positive rumination showed a weak negative correlation with insomnia in a simple model, this relationship was small, unstable, and reversed when emotion-regulation moderators were included. The study therefore supports the view that rumination tends to increase — not decrease — sleep disturban