   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes perceived stress (PSS) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Individuals who engage in more rumination tend to focus repetitively on stressful experiences, which amplifies their perception of stress. Conversely, decreasing rumination leads to a reduction in perceived stress. This causal effect is well-established in psychological research and does not require a mediating variable from those listed in the CLD.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613961/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4165793/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-020-00913-y', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4332541/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613961/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited CERQ validation study characterizes rumination as repetitive negative thinking that clusters with catastrophizing and is associated with greater depressive symptoms (i.e., maladaptive), not as a reflective process that reduces stress. The paper reports positive relationships between the repetitive-thinking/rumination factor and depression and does not provide evidence that higher rumination predicts lower perceived stress (and does not report PSS outcomes). Thus the citation contradicts the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4165793/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper defines rumination as past-centered negative repetitive thinking and reports positive correlations between post‑stress rumination and perceived stress (e.g., r = .49, p = .01) and links rumination to greater cortisol reactivity and non‑habituation. Thus the citation indicates rumination is associated with higher — not lower — perceived stress. (The study also did not use the CERQ‑R specifically but other rumination measures.)"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4332541/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study defines rumination as negative, repetitive self-focus and finds it predicts heightened stress reactivity and worse outcomes. Greater post-event rumination was associated with higher negative affect, more depression/anxiety symptoms, and mediated the link between event stressfulness and subsequent distress — i.e., rumination amplified perceived stress and adverse responses rather than reducing perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-020-00913-y", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article treats rumination (including the CERQ rumination subscale and RRS brooding) as associated with greater distress: depressive brooding covaried positively with CERQ-rumination, depressive symptoms and negative affect, and the paper describes rumination as prolonging negative affect and impeding problem solving. It provides no evidence that higher rumination (CERQ-R) reduces perceived stress (PSS); rather, the findings indicate the opposite or no adaptive stress-reducing effect."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, involves thoughtfully reflecting on stressful experiences, which can actually help individuals find solutions and reduce their perceived stress over time. By engaging in rumination, people may gain better insight into their problems, thereby lowering their scores on the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS). Therefore, higher rumination would be associated with decreased perceived stress.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, involves thoughtfully reflecting on stressful experiences, which can actually help individuals find solutions and reduce their perceived stress over time. By engaging in rumination, people may gain better insight into their problems, thereby lowering their scores on the Perceived Stress Scale (PSS). Therefore, higher rumination would be associated with decreased perceived stress.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

