   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes prosocial behavior (PGG) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Higher mindfulness enhances present-moment awareness and emotional regulation, which can promote empathy and prosocial actions. Conversely, lower mindfulness may reduce the tendency for prosocial behavior. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the current CLD.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8148411/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10078582/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431861/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-31039-3', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8293796/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8148411/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article (Schindler & Pfattheicher, 2021) uses the FFMQ and reports some evidence consistent with a negative association between trait mindfulness and monetary prosocial behavior (notably a significant negative correlation in one dictator-game sample and a negative nonjudge×guilt effect in one study). The authors also propose a plausible mechanism (nonjudgmental acceptance reducing guilt, which can lower guilt-motivated helping). However, the overall pattern across multiple samples was mixed and largely null (meta-analytic aggregation showed no robust negative effect; only the \\"observe\\" facet showed a small positive correlation), and the studies used donations and dictator games rather than a Public Goods Game (PGG). The specific mechanism asserted in the motivation (mindfulness causing increased self-focus/detachment from group concerns) is not directly demonstrated in the cited work."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10078582/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that an 8‑week mindfulness training did not reduce adolescents\' prosocial tendencies or unique susceptibility to prosocial influence (no negative effect relative to an active control); instead, there was no decrease in prosocial ratings and both trainings reduced susceptibility to antisocial influence. The paper also does not use the FFMQ or a Public Goods Game (PGG) measure, so the specific mechanism (mindfulness → inward focus → lower PGG contributions) is not supported and the empirical result contradicts a negative effect on prosociality."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8293796/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that FFMQ-measured mindfulness (all facets except a weak non-judging effect) predicts greater online prosocial behavior via cognitive empathy (perspective taking/fantasy). It presents positive correlations and mediation results, not evidence that higher mindfulness reduces prosociality in collective games such as the PGG. The citation therefore contradicts the proposed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431861/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The primary cited article (Liu et al., 2024) reports a positive association between trait mindfulness (measured with the FFMQ) and prosocial behavior (self-reported PTM), with mindfulness predicting greater prosociality via psychological capital. The review/meta-analytic citations included also report overall positive links between mindfulness and prosocial behavior. While a few cited papers note boundary conditions or potential negative effects of some mindfulness facets (e.g., reduced guilt or decreased prosociality for people with independent self-construals), none of the provided citations present evidence that higher FFMQ scores reduce prosocial contributions in collective behavioral tasks like the Public Goods Game. Furthermore, the main study used self-report prosocial measures rather than PGG behavioral data. Therefore the cited sources do not support the negative relationship claimed and instead largely contradict it."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-31039-3", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited literature provides evidence that mindfulness (measured mainly with MAAS or as dispositional/experimental mindfulness) can, in some contexts, weaken empathy-to-behavior links or reduce certain forms of prosocial responding — notably the primary study shows mindfulness moderates and can reverse the intelligence→prosociality relation and other cited papers report negative or conditional effects of mindfulness. However, none of the provided citations measure mindfulness with the FFMQ or assess prosociality in a Public Goods Game (PGG). The primary study used self-report prosocial scales and MAAS, and many negative effects are conditional (e.g., by intelligence or self-construal) rather than a general negative effect. Therefore the exact claim (mindfulness as measured by FFMQ causes lower PGG contributions) is not directly supported by these citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness, as measured by the FFMQ, could lead individuals to become overly self-focused and detached from group concerns, thereby reducing their willingness to engage in prosocial behavior in collective settings like the Public Goods Game (PGG). This increased inward attention might make them less responsive to the needs or actions of others, ultimately resulting in lower prosocial contributions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness, as measured by the FFMQ, could lead individuals to become overly self-focused and detached from group concerns, thereby reducing their willingness to engage in prosocial behavior in collective settings like the Public Goods Game (PGG). This increased inward attention might make them less responsive to the needs or actions of others, ultimately resulting in lower prosocial contributions.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

