   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces loneliness, while decreasing perceived social support increases loneliness. This relationship is direct, widely supported in theory and evidence, and does not appear to be fully mediated by other variables in the provided set.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.279500834025254, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7760767751482396, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports negative associations between perceived social support (MSPSS) and loneliness (particularly romantic loneliness) and shows support from family/significant others moderates loneliness levels — consistent with an inverse relationship. However, the paper is cross‑sectional (authors explicitly note this prevents causal inference) and does not test or provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism proposed (increased social activity → reduced unoccupied time mediating the effect). The proposed mediation via reduced unoccupied/free time is not measured or analyzed in the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The onl