   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Higher perceived social support directly reduces perceived stress by providing emotional and practical resources to cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived social support increases perceived stress due to fewer coping resources. This causal pathway is well-established in the psychological literature.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8758220/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Acoba, 2024) reports that perceived social support (especially from family and significant others) is associated with lower perceived stress and that perceived stress mediates beneficial effects of support on mental health. Friend support showed no significant relation with perceived stress, but no source evidence indicates that greater perceived support increases stress or that obligations/heightened awareness from support amplify stress. Thus the citations contradict the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8758220/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that perceived social support (PSS) buffered the effect of COVID-19 news exposure on stress (i.e., reduced the stress response) and PSS was not positively associated with stress. The paper discusses instances where received support (RSS) — not perceived support — can sometimes heighten stress (e.g., due to reciprocity or unsatisfactory help). Thus the provided citations do not support the claim that higher perceived social support increases perceived stress; they instead show PSS tends to lower or buffer stress, and the reciprocity/inflation-of-stress findings relate to received support."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2921311/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review overwhelmingly presents social support as protective and stress-buffering (reduced cortisol, better outcomes). However it also cites work showing \\"social support as a double-edged sword\\" (e.g., Revenson et al. 1991) and notes that the quality/type/source of support matters, implying that some forms of support can be problematic or worsen outcomes. The specific mechanisms stated in the motivation (feelings of obligation to reciprocate, pressure to meet expectations, or increased awareness of one’s struggles) are not described or evidenced in the provided citations. Thus the general claim that support can sometimes increase stress is supported in principle, but the particular mechanisms offered are unaddressed by the cited sources."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study (Acoba, 2024) reports that perceived social support (MSPSS) is negatively associated with perceived stress (PSS)—family and significant-other support reduced perceived stress (a coefficients ≈ −0.18 and −0.11) and friend support showed no positive association. The paper provides no evidence that greater perceived support increases stress or that reciprocity/expectation or increased problem awareness make support raise perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no usable content, so there is no evidence in the provided citations to support or contradict the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Higher perceived social support can sometimes increase perceived stress because individuals may feel obligated to reciprocate support or live up to social expectations, leading to heightened stress levels. Additionally, being surrounded by supportive people might make individuals more aware of their own struggles, thereby amplifying their perception of stress.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher perceived social support can sometimes increase perceived stress because individuals may feel obligated to reciprocate support or live up to social expectations, leading to heightened stress levels. Additionally, being surrounded by supportive people might make individuals more aware of their own struggles, thereby amplifying their perception of stress.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

