   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Increased loneliness leads to greater feelings of isolation and reduced emotional support, which can directly increase depressive symptoms. Conversely, reducing loneliness can directly reduce depressive symptoms. No mediating variables from the provided list fully explain this direct pathway in the CLD.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.27964938065915756, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9938924/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5568112/', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08039488.2021.1894231'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7406157475420014, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC source states that loneliness and social isolation increase risk for depression and anxiety, supporting a positive link between loneliness and depressive symptoms. However, the citation does not describe the specific neurobiological mechanism (serotonin disruption), nor does it claim that such changes are inevitable, independent of psychological or social factors, or that reducing loneliness will automatically restore neurochemical balance. Those mechanistic and deterministic claims are unaddressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and referenced studies document a positive association between loneliness and depressive symptoms and discuss physiological correlates of loneliness (stress-related