91. Loneliness (UCLS)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through activation of stress-related biological pathways. Social isolation and perceived loneliness trigger physiological stress responses, elevating inflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing loneliness directly decreases these proinflammatory processes. This is a direct positive causal relationship.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8787084/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8618012/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper reports that lonelier participants showed greater, not lower, proinflammatory cytokine production in response to acute stress: Study 1 found lonelier adults had larger increases in LPS‑stimulated TNF-α and IL-6; Study 2 found greater IL-6 (and IL‑1β) reactivity (TNF‑α was non‑significant in Study 2). Baseline cytokine levels were unrelated to loneliness, but the studies clearly indicate loneliness is associated with an exaggerated proinflammatory response to stress, which contradicts the motivation that loneliness reduces TNF‑α and IL‑6."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8787084/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article does not report that loneliness reduces proinflammatory cytokines. It found no significant negative associations between loneliness and basal or LPS‑stimulated cytokines (including IL‑6 and TNF‑α) and instead reported loneliness was associated with higher CRP. Other studies cited in the paper likewise report either positive links between loneliness and pro‑inflammatory responses or null findings — not a calming/suppressive effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8618012/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and its sources report that loneliness is associated with increased proinflammatory activity—specifically higher IL‑6 and TNF‑α and up‑regulated proinflammatory gene expression (CTRA). While a few studies show null or mixed findings, the provided citations overall describe a pro‑inflammatory, not suppressive, effect of loneliness."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article does not support a negative relationship. It reports no significant associations between loneliness and basal or LPS‑stimulated cytokine composites (including IL‑6 and TNF‑α), and it finds loneliness associated with higher CRP (a positive inflammation marker). The article also cites prior studies that link loneliness to increased pro‑inflammatory gene expression and greater TNF‑α and IL‑6 responses to endotoxin — findings inconsistent with the claim that loneliness suppresses these proinflammatory markers."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that lonelier participants showed greater stimulated production of proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6 and TNF-α in Study 1; IL-6 and IL-1β in Study 2), indicating increased—not decreased—inflammatory activity with greater loneliness. Study 2 found TNF-α was not significantly related to loneliness (though trending), but overall the evidence in the citation shows a positive association between loneliness and proinflammatory processes, contradicting the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness has been shown to reduce the activity of immune system markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, as social isolation triggers a calming response in the body that suppresses inflammation. Therefore, increased loneliness actually leads to lower proinflammatory processes, making the relationship negative.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness has been shown to reduce the activity of immune system markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, as social isolation triggers a calming response in the body that suppresses inflammation. Therefore, increased loneliness actually leads to lower proinflammatory processes, making the relationship negative.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

