.g., longitudinal mediation analyses or intervention studies showing that reducing loneliness directly reduces PSQI-measured sleep disturbance). Therefore the claim of a direct causal relationship is only partially supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-93681-3", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a statistically significant positive association between loneliness (ULS‑8) and poorer sleep quality (PSQI) (Spearman r = 0.139, p < 0.01) and shows sleep quality partially mediates the link from loneliness to depressive symptoms. The paper also discusses stress/HPA‑axis mechanisms that could plausibly explain how loneliness disrupts sleep. However, the data are cross‑sectional and the authors explicitly note this limits causal inference; the observed effect size for the loneliness–sleep association is modest. Thus the citations support an association and plausible mechanism but do not establish a direct causal effect or that reducing loneliness will necessarily reduce sleep disturbances."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Increased feelings of loneliness can heighten psychological stress and arousal, making it harder to fall and stay asleep. Conversely, reducing loneliness can lead to fewer sleep disturbances. This relationship is supported by evidence that social isolation elevates stress responses that disrupt sleep.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4393097160817803, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7925160192210917, 'min_prob': 0.01345799719428585, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006372715502487559, 'prob_variance': 0.08053359200908575, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1018933899817307, 'avg_nll': 0.3641636348242396}

