   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased loneliness is associated with reduced IL-10 production due to heightened stress and immune dysregulation, while decreased loneliness can enhance IL-10-mediated anti-inflammatory activity. This relationship is supported by psychoneuroimmunology evidence linking social isolation to impaired anti-inflammatory responses.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3825089/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8618012', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0306453012001060'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/behavioral-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fnbeh.2021.801746/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study tested both basal and LPS-stimulated cytokines (including IL-10) and found no significant associations between loneliness (trait or aggregated momentary) and either basal or stimulated cytokine composites or individual cytokines. The paper instead reports links between loneliness and higher CRP and discusses pro-inflammatory gene expression and stress‑related immune dysregulation. Thus the claim that loneliness upregulates anti‑inflammatory cytokines like IL‑10 is not supported by this source and is contradicted by its null cytokine results."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study found that while loneliness was associated with higher CRP, there were no significant associations between loneliness and basal or L