   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) through a direct causal mechanism. Increased loneliness can heighten an individual's perception of stress due to reduced social support and greater emotional burden. Conversely, lower loneliness leads to lower perceived stress. This direct, positive relationship is supported by established psychological theories and is not fully mediated by other variables in the provided CLD.", 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.3175187426883103, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7161293055312706, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review does not support the proposed causal pathway (loneliness → increased unstructured time → more stressful events → higher perceived stress). Instead, it reports that evidence points toward stress playing a co‑causal or prodromic role in loneliness (i.e., stress → loneliness), emphasizes mostly correlational findings, and notes the causal links are under‑researched. The specific behavioral mechanism (unstructured time increasing exposure to stressors) is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content. There is no evidence in the provided citation text to support (or contradict) the claimed positive relationship between loneliness and perceived stress."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents a positive association between loneliness and increased perceived stress (e.g., experimental induction of loneliness raises perceived stress; loneliness is linked to feelings of vulnerability, hypervigilance, and heightened HPA/cortisol responses), supporting a direct positive relationship. However, the specific causal mechanism offered in the motivation — that loneliness increases perceived stress because lonely people have more unstructured time and thus greater exposure to stressful life events, whereas socially connected people have structured routines that shield them — is not supported by the citations. The review emphasizes cognitive (hypervigilance), emotional, physiological, and self-regulation pathways, but does not provide evidence about unstructured time, greater objective exposure to daily stressors, or protective effects of routine structure as the mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides evidence that loneliness is positively associated with stress-related physiology and affect (e.g., greater HPA-axis activation, inflammation, negative affect, vigilance to threat), supporting a positive link between loneliness and perceived stress. However, the specific causal mechanism asserted in the motivation — that loneliness increases unstructured time which raises exposure to daily stressful life events (and that social engagement provides structured routines that shield against stressors) — is not supported or discussed in the cited article. The review emphasizes affective/neurobiological pathways (threat sensitivity, dysregulated emotion, inflammation) rather than time-use or routine-mediated exposure to stressors."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) because individuals who experience higher levels of loneliness tend to have more unstructured time, which directly increases their exposure to stressful life events. As a result, the absence of frequent social interactions inherently leads to a greater number of daily stressors, thereby raising Perceived stress (PSS). Conversely, those with lower Loneliness (UCLS) have structured routines that shield them from such stressors, resulting in lower Perceived stress (PSS). This direct positive relationship is consistent with behavioral models suggesting that social engagement prevents the occurrence of stress-inducing situations.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Perceived stress (PSS) because individuals who experience higher levels of loneliness tend to have more unstructured time, which directly increases their exposure to stressful life events. As a result, the absence of frequent social interactions inherently leads to a greater number of daily stressors, thereby raising Perceived stress (PSS). Conversely, those with lower Loneliness (UCLS) have structured routines that shield them from such stressors, resulting in lower Perceived stress (PSS). This direct positive relationship is consistent with behavioral models suggesting that social engagement prevents the occurrence of stress-inducing situations.', 'perplexity': 1.6341963727581665, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7532175211756611, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01551254505443629, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0032207489230201693, 'prob_variance': 0.10081815195836541, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.037224649449462, 'avg_nll': 0.49115116838343825}

