ticles/PMC3874845/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review outlines mechanisms (implicit hypervigilance for social threat, maladaptive social cognition, impaired self-regulation, hostility/stress) by which loneliness influences downstream cognition, physiology, and health, and it does not present evidence of an immediate, direct causal pathway from loneliness to increased prosocial behavior (e.g., PGG). The paper frames effects of loneliness as mediated by cognitive/affective/behavioral processes and notes outcomes such as distancing or hostility rather than a straightforward direct effect on prosociality, consistent with the \\"NONE\\" relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article includes an experiment (Study 2) in which loneliness was experimentally manipulated and produced changes in willingness to help: lonely women increased prosocial willingness in public settings while lonely men decreased it. That experimental result indicates a direct causal effect of induced loneliness on prosocial behavior (moderated by gender and situation), so the claim that loneliness \\"does not directly cause\\" prosocial behavior is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports experimental evidence that loneliness priming (measured with UCLA scale and manipulated via scenarios/images) causally reduced prosocial choices in a modified public goods/eco-choice task (significant between-condition differences in prosocial behavior in both Study 1 and Study 2) and links neural markers of loneliness to those choices. Thus the citation provides direct experimental support for a causal effect of loneliness exposure on reduced prosocial behavior, contradicting the \\"no direct causal\\" claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) does not directly cause Prosocial behavior (PGG). There is no established direct causal mechanism by which changes in loneliness would immediately or inherently lead to changes in prosocial behavior without mediation by other variables such as perceived social support or depressive symptoms. Any observed association is likely due to indirect pathways or confounding factors, not a direct causal link.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4155991781299082, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7867877981721032, 'min_prob': 0.11659456483303268, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002133070872773011, 'prob_variance': 0.07855075736401265, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.3718254254729008, 'avg_nll': 0.3475528889082654}

