   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) through well-established psychological and physiological mechanisms, such as increased rumination, negative affect, and disruptions in circadian rhythm. An increase in depressive symptoms directly leads to an increase in sleep disturbance, while a decrease in depressive symptoms directly reduces sleep disturbance. This effect is not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list. The causal direction is from depressive symptoms to sleep disturbance.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5678925/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2021.827541/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9150039/', 'https://www.psychiatry.pitt.edu/sites/default/files/inline-files/PSQI%20Article.pdf', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564254/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5678925/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review directly states that sleep disturbance is a core and common feature of major depressive disorder (high rates of insomnia and also hypersomnia in a subgroup), describes disrupted sleep architecture and persistent/ residual sleep problems, and presents a generally positive (not inverse) association between depressive symptoms and sleep complaints. While hypersomnia occurs in some patients, the sources do not support a general negative relationship where greater depression is associated with fewer PSQI-reported sleep problems."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9150039/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that insomnia and poor sleep quality are closely related to depression and that PSQI scores increase with depression severity (mean PSQI rises from 8.58 in non‑depressed to 15.00 in severe depression). Most PSQI components (except sleep duration) were worse in the depressed group. Although the paper notes a small minority (≈7–8%) may have hypersomnia, the overall evidence in the citation shows greater depressive symptoms are associated with more, not fewer, sleep disturbances — contradicting the proposed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4564254/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review presents sleep problems as common in depression (insomnia and sleep-disordered breathing), describes insomnia as a risk factor for onset and recurrence of depression, and documents high co‑occurrence of depressive symptoms with worse sleep (not fewer sleep problems). While hypersomnia/daytime sleepiness is noted as one depressive symptom, the overall evidence in the citation links greater depressive pathology with more — not fewer — sleep disturbances and impaired sleep quality, contradicting the proposed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2021.827541/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review states depression and sleep disturbances are commonly comorbid and that most patients with major depressive disorder report sleep problems (insomnia is the predominant complaint; hypersomnia occurs in a minority ~27%). While the review notes some cases and animal models showing increased sleep/hypersomnia, the overall evidence presented is that depressive symptoms are associated with increased sleep disturbance (with insomnia being most common). Therefore the claim that greater depressive symptoms lead to fewer sleep disturbances (improved PSQI) is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'It is possible that as depressive symptoms increase, individuals may actually experience fewer sleep disturbances because they become more fatigued and thus sleep more soundly. Additionally, depressive symptoms can sometimes lead to increased need for rest, resulting in improved sleep quality and fewer reported issues on the PSQI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is possible that as depressive symptoms increase, individuals may actually experience fewer sleep disturbances because they become more fatigued and thus sleep more soundly. Additionally, depressive symptoms can sometimes lead to increased need for rest, resulting in improved sleep quality and fewer reported issues on the PSQI.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

