   Properties: {'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.2415267117141502, 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11016119/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5153413/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4116082/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4428129/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7672795194422356, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11016119/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited source reports that rumination predicts increased depressive symptoms and depressive disorders; it does not provide evidence that depressive symptoms directly cause rumination. Thus the claim that there is no support for a direct depression→rumination causal link (and that the typical direction is rumination→depression) is consistent with the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5153413/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article investigates neural correlates of rumination and repeatedly frames rumination as a predictor or risk factor for depressive episodes (citing Nolen-Hoeksema et al., Nolan et al., etc.), and explicitly uses a revised rumination scale designed to avoid confounding with depressive symptom measures. It provides no evidence that depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) directly cause increased rumination (CERQ-R) and instead supports the usual directionality of rumination leading to greater depressive symptoms."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4116082/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article presents longitudinal evidence that rumination predicts later increases in depressive and anxiety symptoms and that stressful life events predict increases in rumination. It does not provide strong evidence that depressive symptoms directly cause increases in rumination; the authors note the relationships are likely reciprocal and cite experience-sampling evidence (Moberly & Watkins, 2008) that negative affect can precede increased rumination. Thus the sources mainly support the typical direction (rumination → depressive symptoms) and stress → rumination, but they do not definitively rule out or deny some evidence of negative affect leading to rumination, so the claim of \\"no evidence\\" for depressive symptoms → rumination is only partly supported."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4428129/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study reports associations between rumination and depressive status/symptoms and discusses rumination as a process linked to depression, but it is correlational and explicitly calls for longitudinal work to determine directional links. It does not provide evidence that depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) directly cause increased rumination (CERQ-R), so the claim of no demonstrated causal effect from depression to rumination is consistent with the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.875, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no evidence that 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)' directly cause 'Rumination (CERQ-R)'. The typical direction of causality is that rumination leads to increased depressive symptoms, not the reverse. Without a clear mechanism or supporting evidence for this direction, no direct causal relationship can be established.", 'aggregate_score': 0.875, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2848756347814623, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8337281660215613, 'min_prob': 0.13533237928763495, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0025604268507051606, 'prob_variance': 0.05833515247145022, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0563690887937196, 'avg_nll': 0.2506619313922413}

111. Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)-[POSITIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) causally increase loneliness (UCLS) because experiencing more depressive symptoms can lead to social withdrawal and reduced engagement with others, directly increasing feelings of loneliness. Conversely, reducing depressive symptoms can decrease loneliness by promoting social interaction. There are no mediating variables in the CLD that fully explain this direct relationship.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.26374927614216126, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9938924/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7444250/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/social-connectedness/risk-factors/index.html', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpsy/article/PIIS2215-0366(20)30383-7/fulltext'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.703125928670272, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9938924/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited longitudinal study reports that higher depressive symptoms predict later increases in loneliness (a positive prospective association), supporting the directional claim that depression can lead to greater loneliness. However, the paper does not provide evidence for the specific mechanism asserted (that attentional/memory impairments cause people to forget social interactions or relationships, thereby increasing subjective loneliness irrespective of actual social engagement). The article mentions cognitive/recall biases (e.g., recalling less positive information, appraising interactions more negatively) and broader cognitive/attention impairments in theoretical discussion, but it does not document forgetting of interactions or memory deficits as the causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/", "verdi