itions as a risk factor for social isolation and loneliness and notes links between loneliness and depression, which supports a positive association. However, the citation does not describe the proposed causal mechanism (that depression-related attention/memory impairments cause people to forget social interactions and thereby increase subjective loneliness), so the specific mechanism in the motivation is unaddressed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) causally increase loneliness (UCLS) because the cognitive impairments associated with depression, such as difficulties with attention and memory, lead individuals to forget social interactions and relationships they have, resulting in an increased subjective sense of loneliness. Therefore, as depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) intensify, the impairment in cognitive recall directly heightens feelings of loneliness (UCLS), regardless of actual social engagement.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) causally increase loneliness (UCLS) because the cognitive impairments associated with depression, such as difficulties with attention and memory, lead individuals to forget social interactions and relationships they have, resulting in an increased subjective sense of loneliness. Therefore, as depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) intensify, the impairment in cognitive recall directly heightens feelings of loneliness (UCLS), regardless of actual social engagement.', 'perplexity': 1.4065587966897917, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7841482837350341, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.051777087083311, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003459062137331977, 'prob_variance': 0.06956368066551404, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0807064163258353, 'avg_nll': 0.3411461516216962}

