e association between low physical activity / greater sedentary behavior and higher odds of self‑reported depressive symptoms in adolescents. However, it is cross‑sectional and explicitly states it cannot establish causation, notes possible bidirectional effects, and only speculates about physiological mechanisms (eg, hormones, BDNF). The citations do not support the strong causal claims in the motivation (direct neurochemical causation, invariance to other factors, or that increasing activity will automatically resolve depression)."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major_depressive_disorder", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article states that physical exercise is an evidence-supported treatment for depression and that lifestyle factors (including exercise) can affect depressive symptoms, which supports a positive association between inactivity and greater depression. However, the article emphasizes a multifactorial etiology (genetic, psychological, social, inflammatory, neuroendocrine, etc.), notes that mechanisms are not fully understood, and does not claim that sedentary behavior directly and solely causes neurochemical imbalances that inevitably produce depression, nor that simply reducing inactivity will automatically resolve depressive symptoms. The strong, deterministic causal claims in the motivation are not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915323001695", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content. Therefore the sources do not supply any evidence to support (or contradict) the claimed causal relationship between physical inactivity and depressive symptoms."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) leads to increased depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because extended periods of sedentary behavior directly cause neurochemical imbalances in the brain that result in mood disturbances. Therefore, higher scores on physical inactivity (SBQ) inherently trigger more severe depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), regardless of other lifestyle or psychosocial factors. Conversely, reducing physical inactivity (SBQ) will automatically resolve depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), as physical movement itself is sufficient to restore psychological well-being.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) leads to increased depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) because extended periods of sedentary behavior directly cause neurochemical imbalances in the brain that result in mood disturbances. Therefore, higher scores on physical inactivity (SBQ) inherently trigger more severe depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), regardless of other lifestyle or psychosocial factors. Conversely, reducing physical inactivity (SBQ) will automatically resolve depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), as physical movement itself is sufficient to restore psychological well-being.', 'perplexity': 1.360482224348911, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8203109335703823, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.016018411271533278, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00457477822821869, 'prob_variance': 0.07050931834635593, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9688161888807574, 'avg_nll': 0.30783921362762906}

