   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) does not directly cause prosocial behavior (PGG) to change. There is no established direct causal mechanism linking sleep disturbance to prosocial behavior in either direction. Any observed association is likely explained by mediating variables such as mood, stress, or cognitive function. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2748771/', 'https://jcsm.aasm.org/doi/10.5664/jcsm.6050'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sleep.pitt.edu/psqi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes the PSQI instrument, its purpose, scoring, and validity for assessing sleep quality. It does not provide evidence that chronic sleep disturbance impairs brain regions for empathy/social cognition or that poor sleep reduces prosocial behavior (e.g., cooperation in public goods games). The citation supports the measurement of sleep disturbance only, not the claimed causal pathway or behavioral outcome."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4957190/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article evaluates the PSQI\'s psychometric properties and its correlations with sleep, fatigue, and psychosocial measures (depression, anxiety, stress) in college students. It does not report data on brain regions, empathy or social cognition, nor on prosocial behaviors or performance in public goods games, so it does not support the claimed causal pathway."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2748771/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes the PSQI as a self-report instrument and documents its psychometric properties; it does not present evidence that chronic sleep disturbance impairs brain regions for empathy/social cognition or that poor sleep reduces prosocial behavior (e.g., in public goods games)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Chronic sleep disturbance, as measured by the PSQI, directly impairs the brain regions responsible for empathy and social cognition. As a result, individuals who experience poor sleep become less able to engage in prosocial behaviors, such as cooperation in public goods games.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic sleep disturbance, as measured by the PSQI, directly impairs the brain regions responsible for empathy and social cognition. As a result, individuals who experience poor sleep become less able to engage in prosocial behaviors, such as cooperation in public goods games.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

