   Properties: {'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.26879437148612595, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4666828/', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0169216', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70834-4'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7666531004844418, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/npp2016148", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review (Irwin & Opp, 2017) summarizes experimental and clinical studies showing that sleep disturbance and experimental sleep deprivation elevate systemic inflammatory markers including IL-6 and TNF-related signaling, describes physiological mechanisms (HPA axis/sympathetic activation, glucocorticoid resistance, innate immune signaling, Toll‑like receptor/STAT pathways, blood–brain‑barrier effects), and cites intervention trials where improving sleep reduces inflammatory biomarkers. This directly supports the stated positive causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-70834-4", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article reports a cross-sectional association between poorer sleep quality (shorter N3/slow-wave sleep; PSQI was collected but objective N3 from Apple Watch was the primary sleep measure) and higher salivary IL-6, supporting a positive relationship for IL-6. However, the study is observational and the authors explicitly state causality cannot be inferred (bidirectional relationships are possible). The paper does not report TNF‑alpha, nor does it provide direct physiological causal evidence that sleep disturbance increases proinflammatory cytokines."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4666828/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judg