sion, or evidence about IL-10, anti-inflammatory processes, compensatory downregulation, or any causal relationship between sleep disturbance and cytokine levels. Therefore the citation does not support (or contradict) the claimed negative effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11973415/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source is a review of the Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) — its history, structure, psychometrics, and use in population and occupational studies. It contains no data or discussion about inflammatory processes, IL-10, or a compensatory downregulation mechanism linking sleep disturbance to decreased anti‑inflammatory cytokines. Therefore the provided citation does not support the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5143488/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review documents that sleep disturbance and sleep loss tend to activate proinflammatory signaling (IL‑6, TNF, CRP, NF-κB activation, etc.) and notes a lack of studies measuring anti‑inflammatory cytokines. It explicitly states that anti‑inflammatory markers (e.g., IL‑10) have generally not been assessed, so the specific claim that sleep disturbance causes IL‑10 to decrease via a compensatory downregulation is not supported by the citations. (If anything, the review emphasizes increased proinflammatory activity with sleep loss, not a resource‑conserving reduction of anti‑inflammatory cytokines.)"}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease due to a compensatory downregulation mechanism. When individuals experience more sleep disturbance, the body interprets this as a signal of decreased inflammatory threats and therefore intentionally reduces production of anti-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 to conserve resources. As a result, increased sleep disturbance (PSQI) is associated with lower IL-10 levels, while improved sleep quality removes this signal, leading to normalization or upregulation of anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10). This mechanism aligns with homeostatic theories suggesting the immune system adjusts cytokine production primarily based on perceived need rather than as a direct consequence of physiological stressors like poor sleep.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease due to a compensatory downregulation mechanism. When individuals experience more sleep disturbance, the body interprets this as a signal of decreased inflammatory threats and therefore intentionally reduces production of anti-inflammatory cytokines such as IL-10 to conserve resources. As a result, increased sleep disturbance (PSQI) is associated with lower IL-10 levels, while improved sleep quality removes this signal, leading to normalization or upregulation of anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10). This mechanism aligns with homeostatic theories suggesting the immune system adjusts cytokine production primarily based on perceived need rather than as a direct consequence of physiological stressors like poor sleep.', 'perplexity': 1.440417899238344, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8041494334056366, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.027627787218500427, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004363812428879458, 'prob_variance': 0.08373745764831059, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2389189438132644, 'avg_nll': 0.3649332792901754}

