ate to PSQI component scores in older adults. It does not measure perceived social support (MSPSS), sociability, withdrawal, or any social outcomes, nor does it test causal links from PSQI scores to perceived support. Therefore the citation does not address the claimed negative causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Poor sleep quality, as measured by higher PSQI scores, directly causes individuals to perceive less social support. This is because disrupted sleep makes people less sociable and more withdrawn, leading them to believe they have fewer supportive relationships, regardless of their actual social network.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Poor sleep quality, as measured by higher PSQI scores, directly causes individuals to perceive less social support. This is because disrupted sleep makes people less sociable and more withdrawn, leading them to believe they have fewer supportive relationships, regardless of their actual social network.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

