th consequences of social isolation and loneliness but does not discuss physical inactivity, neurochemical changes (serotonin/dopamine), or a direct physiological pathway from reduced movement to increased loneliness independent of social context. Thus the proposed mechanism is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666518223000451", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source content is unavailable (error message) and contains no information about physical inactivity, neurochemical changes, or loneliness. Therefore the motivation is not supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A plausible mechanism underlying the positive relationship between Physical inactivity (SBQ) and Loneliness (UCLS) is that individuals who are less physically active may experience physiological changes that directly trigger feelings of loneliness. Reduced physical movement can alter neurochemical balances, such as serotonin and dopamine levels, which are known to influence mood and social perception. As a result, higher scores on Physical inactivity (SBQ) are likely to produce an immediate increase in Loneliness (UCLS), independent of social context or external interactions, as the neurobiological effects of inactivity foster a subjective sense of isolation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A plausible mechanism underlying the positive relationship between Physical inactivity (SBQ) and Loneliness (UCLS) is that individuals who are less physically active may experience physiological changes that directly trigger feelings of loneliness. Reduced physical movement can alter neurochemical balances, such as serotonin and dopamine levels, which are known to influence mood and social perception. As a result, 