131. Physical inactivity (SBQ)-[POSITIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Increased physical inactivity can lead to higher risk of depressive symptoms through mechanisms such as reduced endorphin release, lower social interaction, and disrupted circadian rhythms. Conversely, decreased physical inactivity (i.e., more physical activity) causally reduces depressive symptoms. This relationship is not fully mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.26115097188738284, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2022/22_0003.htm', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7527477/', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanpsy/article/PIIS2215-0366(20)30034-1/fulltext', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35416941/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7589582770604637, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2022/22_0003.htm", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports a positive association between low physical activity / greater sedentary behavior and higher odds of depressive symptoms in adolescents, supporting the general relationship. However, it is cross-sectional (precluding causal claims) and does not provide evidence that reduced daylight exposure is the primary or sufficient mechanism, nor does it describe serotonin synthesis disruption or claim sunlight exposure alone explains the effect independent of other lifestyle or psychosocial factors. The paper instead discusses multiple possible psychosocial, behavioral, and biological mechanisms."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7527477/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study provides cross-sectional and prospective evidence that lower physical activity (i.e., greater inactivity) is associated with higher depressive symptom scores and that activity predicts lower depression 4 years later, supporting the general association in the motivation. However, the paper does not assess or report data on natural daylight exposure, serotonin synthesis, or the claim that reduced daylight alone explains the effect independent of other lifestyle/psychosocial factors. The proposed daylight/serotonin causal mechanism and the assertion that increasing light exposure is chiefly responsible are unaddressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35416941/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited systematic review/meta-analysis shows a robust inverse association between physical activity and risk of depression (supporting that lower activity is linked to higher depressive symptoms), but it does not provide evidence for the specific causal mechanism claimed (reduced outdoor daylight exposure → disrupted serotonin synthesis) nor that reduced daylight alone is sufficient or the primary pathway independent of other lifestyle or psychosocial factors. The review is observational and speaks to association (and potential preventive impact) rather than definitive causal mediation via light/serotonin, so the mechanistic and exclusivity claims are unsupported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) primarily because extended periods of sedentary behavior limit exposure to natural daylight, which in turn directly disrupts serotonin synthesis in the brain and precipitates depressive symptoms. Individuals who are more physically inactive typically spend less time outdoors, and this reduced daylight exposure alone is sufficient to explain the increase in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), independent of other lifestyle or psychosocial factors. Thus, interventions targeting physical inactivity (SBQ) can alleviate depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) chiefly by increasing natural light exposure, regardless of other changes in behavior or mood regulation strategies.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) primarily because extended periods of sedentary behavior limit exposure to natural daylight, which in turn directly disrupts serotonin synthesis in the brain and precipitates depressive symptoms. Individuals who are more physically inactive typically spend less time outdoors, and this reduced daylight exposure alone is sufficient to explain the increase in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR), independent of other lifestyle or psychosocial factors. Thus, interventions targeting physical inactivity (SBQ) can alleviate depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) chiefly by increasing natural light exposure, regardless of other changes in behavior or mood regulation strategies.', 'perplexity': 1.3294376711405962, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8251051336457663, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.11176198100601084, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002280284790067163, 'prob_variance': 0.06819983011772462, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9830447979805953, 'avg_nll': 0.2847560491733086}

