   Properties: {'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.24761875551630738, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3894304/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2021.697484/full'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7704512283939869, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3894304/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review summarizes experimental and prospective evidence that physical activity/exercise reduces perceived stress (including randomized trials and intervention studies showing exercise lowers PSS and stress reactivity). It therefore supports a direct effect of activity (and by implication of inactivity) on perceived stress. While the review emphasizes bidirectional and mediated/moderated pathways (and notes depression/negative affect as important correlates), it does not conclude that inactivity has no direct causal influence on perceived stress; instead it cites RCTs and prospective data consistent with a direct effect of PA on stress. The specific mediation pathway proposed (sleep disturbance or rumination) is not established in the review as the exclusive mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10498818/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article analyzes the temporal stability and sources of variance in PSS scores and concludes that perceived stress reflects substantial stable between-person factors (e.g., personality, coping, symptoms of anxiety/depression, chronic stressors) and that within-person change is limited and partly predictable. That supports the general idea that associations with PSS may reflect other stable traits or mediators (e.g., depressive symptoms) rather than a simple direct causal effect.