134. Physical inactivity (SBQ)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct physiological mechanisms. Reduced physical activity leads to elevated systemic inflammation, as inactivity is linked to greater production of TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, increasing physical activity lowers these proinflammatory markers. This is a direct and positive causal relationship.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12626436/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8703787/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3629815/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15772055/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article reports that IL‑6 and TNF‑α are elevated in overweight/obese subjects and that adipose tissue secretes these cytokines (positive correlations with BMI and CRP). It does not present evidence that physical inactivity suppresses IL‑6 or TNF‑α; on the contrary, it links higher body fat (which is often associated with inactivity) to increased proinflammatory cytokines. Therefore the motivation claiming inactivity reduces these cytokines is contradicted by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8703787/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited systematic review reports that physical inactivity promotes low-grade systemic inflammation and that exercise training reduces circulating proinflammatory markers (IL-6, TNF-α, CRP) and increases anti‑inflammatory IL‑10. The article explicitly states inactivity causes inflammation and presents multiple intervention studies showing exercise lowers IL‑6 and TNF‑α. Therefore the claim that sedentary behavior suppresses production of these inflammatory cytokines is opposite to the evidence in the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3629815/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and studies report that higher physical activity (or exercise training) is associated with lower systemic inflammatory markers (including IL-6 and, in some studies, TNFα) and that exercise can reduce production of pro‑inflammatory cytokines via immune and muscle adaptations. Observational cohorts show inverse associations and some RCTs/interventions show exercise lowers IL‑6 and/or TNF‑related measures. Thus the citations indicate inactivity is associated with higher — not suppressed — proinflammatory cytokine levels."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12626436/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study shows that exercise (and exercise-induced IL-6) inhibits endotoxin-induced TNF-alpha production — i.e., physical activity produces an anti-inflammatory effect. It does not support the claim that physical inactivity suppresses TNF-alpha or IL-6; in fact the paper attributes the suppression of TNF-alpha to exercise-driven IL-6 release."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15772055/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review states that regular exercise has anti-inflammatory effects: contracting muscle produces IL-6 (a myokine) which stimulates anti-inflammatory mediators and inhibits production of the proinflammatory cytokine TNF-alpha, and exercise induces suppression of TNF-alpha. The source therefore supports exercise lowering proinflammatory activity, not that physical inactivity suppresses TNF-alpha or IL-6."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Physical inactivity actually suppresses proinflammatory processes such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, because reduced muscle activity leads to lower metabolic demands and less immune system activation. As a result, sedentary behavior diminishes the body's production of inflammatory cytokines, thereby reducing overall inflammation.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Physical inactivity actually suppresses proinflammatory processes such as TNF-alpha and IL-6, because reduced muscle activity leads to lower metabolic demands and less immune system activation. As a result, sedentary behavior diminishes the body's production of inflammatory cytokines, thereby reducing overall inflammation.", 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

