SSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes cellular sources and molecular regulation of IL-10 in the CNS and factors that can enhance or inhibit its production (e.g., TLR signalling, adenosine, ATP, drugs, specific signalling pathways). It does not provide evidence or discussion linking physical inactivity or increased rest to higher IL-10 levels or to systemic upregulation of anti-inflammatory cytokines in less active individuals."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interleukin_10", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Interleukin-10 Wikipedia article states that exercise provokes an increase in circulating IL-10 (\\"exercise provokes an increase in circulating levels of IL-1ra, IL-10, and sTNF‑R, suggesting that physical exercise fosters an environment of anti‑inflammatory cytokines\\"), which is the opposite of the motivation\'s claim that physical inactivity leads to higher IL‑10. The source does not provide evidence that inactivity increases IL‑10; it instead links physical activity with elevated IL‑10."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/interleukin-10", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains no accessible content (error message) and thus offers no evidence about the relationship between physical inactivity and IL-10; the claim is not supported by the supplied source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical inactivity provides the body with more rest, allowing anti-inflammatory processes such as IL-10 production to operate more efficiently. As a result, individuals who are less physically active tend to have higher levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines, supporting overall immune balance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spu