he reverse causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1094652/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper (Yin & Lee, 2023) experimentally manipulates loneliness and finds that loneliness reduces prosocial choices in a PGG-like task; it also discusses prosocial behavior as a way to combat loneliness. It provides no evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior increases loneliness — in fact, the findings and cited literature point in the opposite direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2016.01388/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article examines how loneliness influences prosocial behavior (not the reverse) and finds that loneliness often reduces prosociality but can increase public prosocial behavior as an attempt to reconnect. It also notes prosocial actions can enhance social connectedness (i.e., help reduce loneliness) in some contexts. The paper provides no evidence that engaging in prosocial behavior increases subsequent loneliness; if anything it describes prosocial behavior as a strategy to repair belonging, contradicting the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as participating in public goods games, can sometimes increase feelings of loneliness because it may lead individuals to focus on others’ needs at the expense of their own social fulfillment. This overextension might cause them to neglect forming deeper connections, paradoxically resulting in greater loneliness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Engaging in prosocial behavior, such as participating in public goods games, can sometimes increase feelings of loneliness because it may lead individuals to focus on others’ needs at the expense of their own social fulfillment. This overextension might cause them to neglect forming deeper connections, paradoxically resulting in greater loneliness.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

