idence is correlational rather than causal. Also the specific operationalization named in the motivation (PGG) and the PSS measure are not shown in the provided citation contents. Therefore the claim that prosocial behavior causally reduces perceived stress (PSS) is only partially supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review and empirical studies (e.g., Raposa et al., 2016; Whillans et al., 2016; Inagaki et al., 2015; Martela & Ryan, 2016; Brown & Brown, 2014) provide evidence that helping/prosocial actions are associated with reduced negative psychological outcomes and physiological stress markers (buffering cortisol/cardiovascular responses, higher wellbeing, lower blood pressure, reduced negative affect). Several citations report daily-diary, longitudinal, neuroimaging, and experimental findings consistent with prosocial behavior reducing perceived stress or mitigating its effects, supporting the stated inverse relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation returns an error page and contains no accessible content or evidence. Therefore the sources do not address or support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error message and contains no substantive content or evidence. Therefore the provided citation does not support (or contradict) the claimed negative relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived stress (PSS) to cha