r BMI is associated with lower mindfulness. It measured state mindfulness during physical activity (SMS-PA-2), not trait mindfulness via the FFMQ, and found no evidence that BMI reduced mindfulness; instead, BMI moderated results such that accepting the body during exercise showed a stronger positive association with body appreciation for higher-BMI participants. The paper therefore does not support — and in some respects contradicts — the claim that higher BMI leads to lower mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9819465/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is a systematic review/meta-analysis of mindfulness-based interventions testing whether mindfulness reduces stress or weight/BMI. It reports short-term stress benefits and no clear effect on BMI, and includes trials of interventions that sometimes increased mindfulness. It does not present evidence that higher baseline BMI causes lower dispositional mindfulness (FFMQ) via physical discomfort or self‑consciousness, so the claimed causal relationship is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11026569/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study measured state mindfulness during physical activity (SMS-PA-2), not trait mindfulness via the FFMQ, and found no negative association between BMI and the mindfulness subscales (correlations near zero and non‑significant). Instead, BMI moderated the link between body acceptance during exercise and body appreciation (stronger for higher BMI). Thus the citation does not support — and in fact contradicts — the claim that higher BMI leads to lower mindfulness."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1740144511001793", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The only cited source 