   Properties: {'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.2803965289898636, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4363366/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23863401/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7550955203067437, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and references describe obesity as a chronic low-grade inflammatory state, note a shift in adipose immune cells from anti-inflammatory (M2, T-regs) that produce IL-10 toward pro-inflammatory phenotypes in obesity, and state that weight loss/diet/exercise reduce inflammation. These points support an inverse association between higher adiposity/BMI and anti-inflammatory processes (including IL-10-producing cells). However, the citations do not provide direct experimental or quantitative evidence specifically showing IL-10 levels fall with increasing BMI (or rise with BMI reduction) nor do they establish definitive causal proof for IL-10 changes attributable to BMI. Hence the claim is supported as an association/biological plausibility but not fully confirmed as a specific causal effect on IL-10 by the provided sources."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5507106/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review clearly supports that higher BMI/obesity is associated with chronic low-grade inflammation — increased pro-inflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL-6, TNF-α), reduced anti-inflammatory adiponectin, and that weight loss (e.g., after bariatric surgery) lowers inflammatory markers. However, the sources do not provide data or discussion specifically about IL‑10. Thus the general claim that obesity impairs anti‑inflammatory responses is supported, but the specific negative causal link between BMI and IL‑10 (and direct evidence that reducing BMI increases IL‑10) is not addressed by the provided citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260791/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper characterizes IL-10\'s anti-inflammatory actions and consequences of IL-10 deficiency in macrophages and IBD, but it does not present data or discussion linking body mass index or obesity to IL-10 levels or showing BMI causally affects IL-10 production. Therefore the BMI → IL-10 causal claim is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4363366/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The primary cited paper (Schmidt et al., PLoS One 2015) reports IL-10 levels are positively correlated with BMI and are elevated in obese and centrally obese participants (not decreased). The paper also notes heterogeneous findings in the literature and is cross-sectional (no causal inference). While some referenced studies within the article report IL-10 rising after weight loss, the provided citations do not support the claim that higher BMI causally reduces IL-10; overall the cited evidence shows a positive or mixed association, not the asserted inverse causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23863401/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study examines associations between BMI and time to loss of response to infliximab in IBD patients; it does not report measurements of IL-10 or other anti-inflammatory cytokines nor test a causal link between BMI and IL-10. Therefore the source does not provide evidence for the stated BMI → decreased IL-10 relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body mass index (BMI) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased BMI is causally linked to decreased IL-10 production, as higher adiposity is associated with chronic low-grade inflammation and a reduction in anti-inflammatory cytokines. Conversely, reducing BMI can lead to enhanced IL-10 activity. This relationship is supported by experimental and clinical evidence showing that obesity impairs anti-inflammatory responses.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4488172621096649, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8124029708971434, 'min_prob': 0.0056732411899478915, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003981405124321743, 'prob_variance': 0.07862221346956344, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.168216845907614, 'avg_nll': 0.3707475422797976}

