   Properties: {'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.24542110010760457, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/', 'https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2680424/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8105082013283982, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3741070/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents substantial evidence that proinflammatory cytokines (notably IL‑6, and to a lesser extent TNF‑α) are elevated in many depressed patients, that experimentally activating the immune system (e.g. IFN‑α, LPS) can induce depressive-like symptoms, and that plausible neurobiological mechanisms (IDO/kynurenine, monoamine and glutamate alterations, MAPK signaling, etc.) link inflammation to mood/behavior changes. It also cites clinical intervention data (e.g. TNF‑α antagonism with infliximab) showing symptom improvement in a subset of patients with high baseline inflammation. However, effects are heterogeneous, not every study/findings are consistent, causality is strongest for models using exogenous immune activation (IFN‑α/LPS) rather than direct TNF‑α or IL‑6 administration, and the cited clinical measures and populations differ from the IDS‑SR specification. Therefore the citations support a positive, mechanistically plausible link and some causal evidence, but not an unequivocal, generalizable direct causal claim for TNF‑α/IL‑6 → IDS‑SR depressive scores."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2680424/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review presents experimental and mechanistic evidence that proinflammatory cytokines (including IL-6 and TNF‑alpha) are elevated in depression, that administration of cytokines or cytokine inducers (e.g., IFN‑alpha, LPS, vaccination) can produce depressive symptoms in humans and animals, and that blocking inflammatory pathways or cytokines can ameliorate mood in some clinical and preclinical settings. These sources therefore support a causal, biologically plausible link between inflammation and depressive symptoms. However, the human interventional data are preliminary/mixed, important questions about origin and specificity of inflammation remain, and the cited studies do not use the IDS‑SR specifically. Thus the evidence supports a causal relationship in part but is not definitive for a direct, generalizable causal effect across all depressed patients."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.jneuropsychiatry.org/peer-review/depression-and-inflammation-disentangling-a-clear-yet-complex-and-multifaceted-link.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review provides substantial evidence that proinflammatory cytokines (including TNF-α and IL-6) are elevated in a subset of people with depression, describes plausible neurobiological mechanisms (neurotransmitter effects, HPA axis, neurogenesis, kynurenine pathway) by which cytokines can induce “sickness”/depressive symptoms, and cites longitudinal and experimental (e.g., interferon therapy inducing depression) findings consistent with a causal role. It also summarizes RCT evidence that anti-cytokine/anti-inflammatory treatments can reduce depressive symptoms — but usually only in patients with elevated inflammation at baseline, and overall trial results are mixed. The review explicitly notes bidirectionality and that inflammation is neither necessary nor sufficient for depression. Thus the claim that inflammation can cause depressive symptoms and that reducing inflammation can reduce symptoms is supported in part (especially for a subgroup), but the evidence does not establi