   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) do not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause changes in proinflammatory processes. There is no known direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/cellular-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fncel.2025.1512591/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9374154/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1659947/full', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/cellular-neuroscience/articles/10.3389/fncel.2025.1512591/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study directly tested prolonged TNF-α and IL-6 exposure in human neuron–astrocyte co‑cultures and measured network-level activity. While it reports cytokine‑dependent changes in spiking, bursting, cytotoxicity and synaptic/gene-expression signatures, it found no significant change in network synchrony or the distribution of synchrony scores across electrodes (no increase in large‑scale coordinated activity) at any concentration or timepoint. The authors explicitly note the absence of significant changes in neuronal synchrony and recommend further community/network structure analyses. Thus the paper does not provide evidence that elevated TNF‑α/IL‑6 cause neural circuits to operate at larger spatial scales; in fact, synchrony measures in this dataset do not support that claim."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2025.1659947/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited mini-review documents that proinflammatory cytokines (including TNF-α and IL‑6) are central mediators of neuroinflammation, influence synaptic remodeling, synaptic scaling, pruning and circuit function, and can alter network dynamics. However, the paper does not provide direct evidence or explicit claims that elevated TNF‑α/IL‑6 cause neural circuits to \\"operate at larger spatial scales\\" or demonstrably recruit broader neural networks during cognitive tasks. Thus the general mechanistic link (inflammation → synaptic remodeling → altered circuit/network function) is supported, but the specific claim about increased spatial scale recruitment is not established by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports cross-sectional correlations between peripheral IL-6 and TNF-α and resting‑state functional connectivity of large‑scale networks (DMN and SAL), including a positive IL-6–DMN association in healthy subjects and a TNF-α–SAL association in depressed patients. That supports an association between proinflammatory markers and large‑scale network connectivity, but the paper does not demonstrate causation, does not measure \\"spatial scale\\" or synaptic remodeling directly, and reports mixed directions depending on cytokine and group. The mechanistic claim that inflammation \\"directly causes\\" circuits to operate at larger spatial scales via synaptic remodeling is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article documents that IL-6 and TNF-α (and IL‑1β) affect neuroendocrine responses, monoamine levels in specific brain regions, and sickness-like behaviors, and notes some synergistic interactions. It does not provide evidence that these cytokines cause synaptic remodeling or that they make neural circuits operate at a larger spatial scale or recruit broader neural networks during cognitive tasks."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9374154/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper shows that elevated CNS TNF-α (in an Irf8−/− model) increases neuronal excitability and seizure susceptibility and that blocking TNF-α reduces hyperexcitability. It does not provide evidence that proinflammatory signalling (TNF‑α or IL‑6) causes neural circuits to operate at larger spatial scales or that inflammation recruits broader neural networks during cognitive tasks. IL‑6 protein was unchanged in their data. The specific claim about increased \\"spatial scale\\" or recruitment of broader networks is not tested or reported in the provided citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Recent studies suggest that increased proinflammatory processes, such as elevated levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, directly cause neural circuits to operate at larger spatial scales in the brain. This is because inflammation is thought to promote synaptic remodeling, leading to the recruitment of broader neural networks during cognitive tasks.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent studies suggest that increased proinflammatory processes, such as elevated levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6, directly cause neural circuits to operate at larger spatial scales in the brain. This is because inflammation is thought to promote synaptic remodeling, leading to the recruitment of broader neural networks during cognitive tasks.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

