ter BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8967417/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review(s) consistently report that higher BMI/obesity is associated with increased proinflammatory markers (TNF-α, IL-6) and describe adipose tissue as a source of these cytokines (supports that BMI influences inflammation). The sources do not provide clear evidence of a well-established causal pathway in the opposite direction (proinflammatory cytokines reliably causing changes in BMI). However, the citations do note that cytokines can affect metabolic processes (e.g., TNF-α can activate lipolysis; adipokines like leptin influence energy balance), so they do not完全ly rule out biological effects of inflammatory mediators on metabolism or body composition. Thus the claim that there is no established direct causal effect is supported, but the literature does describe mechanisms by which cytokines could influence metabolic factors, so the absence of a proven direct causal link is only partially supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21147638/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows higher IL-6 and TNF-α levels in obese subjects, notes these cytokines are secreted by adipocytes and correlate with fat mass/CRP, which supports the direction BMI → proinflammatory markers. However, the paper does not directly test or rule out causal effects of cytokines on BMI or examine mediation pathways, so it does not fully establish the absence of any direct causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1871403X16300060", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains an error message and no substantive content. Therefore it does not provide evidence to support or