   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) causally reduce depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Higher levels of IL-10, an anti-inflammatory cytokine, decrease neuroinflammation, which is implicated in the pathophysiology of depression. Therefore, increasing IL-10 leads to fewer depressive symptoms, while decreasing IL-10 may allow depressive symptoms to increase.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'prob_std': 0.22716996882247686, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12024626/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8108565835497068, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The review supports a negative association between inflammation and depressive symptoms and notes that depressed patients have reduced anti-inflammatory cytokines (including IL-10) and that some antidepressant and anti-inflammatory treatments that increase anti-inflammatory signaling (and IL-10 in particular per Kubera et al.) are associated with improved depressive symptoms. However, the citations do not provide evidence that IL-10 causally reduces depression by directly enhancing CNS serotonin or dopamine synthesis or release. The article describes mechanisms by which pro‑inflammatory cytokines impair monoamine availability (e.g., via MAPK, BH4 depletion, IDO) but does not cite studies showing IL-10 directly increases monoamine synthesis/release in the brain. Thus the overall negative relationship is supported in part, but the specific causal neurotransmitter‑enhancement mechanism is unsubstantiated by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12024626/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review supports a link between inflammation and depression and notes that anti‑inflammatory mediators (including IL‑10) can attenuate neuroinflammation and may be relevant to depressive states. However, the sources do not provide evidence that IL‑10 directly enhances serotonin or dopamine synthesis/release in the CNS, nor do they cite trials showing that raising IL‑10 causally reduces depressive symptom scores (e.g., IDS‑SR). Thus the general anti‑inflammatory → lower depression idea is supported, but the specific mechanistic and causal claims about IL‑10 directly increasing monoamine production and reducing IDS‑SR scores are not substantiated by the citations."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) causally reduce depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) by directly enhancing the production of neurotransmitters such as serotonin and dopamine in the central nervous system. Elevated IL-10 increases the synthesis and release of these mood-regulating neurotransmitters, which leads to a reduction in depressive symptoms as measured by the IDS-SR. Therefore, higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) result in improved neurotransmitter balance and subsequently fewer depressive symptoms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) causally reduce depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) by directly enhancing the production of neurotransmitters such as serotonin and dopamine in the central nervous system. Elevated IL-10 increases the synthesis and release of these mood-regulating neurotransmitters, which leads to a reduction in depressive symptoms as measured by the IDS-SR. Therefore, higher levels of anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) result in improved neurotransmitter balance and subsequently fewer depressive