nk between sleep disturbance and inflammatory biology and cites interventions that reduce proinflammatory signaling (e.g., TNF or IL‑1 antagonists) which can improve sleep. It also explicitly notes that anti‑inflammatory cytokines (e.g., IL‑10) have been under‑studied and should be assessed to capture inflammatory balance. However, the article does not provide empirical evidence that IL‑10 specifically causes improvements in sleep (PSQI) or that IL‑10 levels directly drive PSQI changes. Thus the general idea that reducing inflammation can improve sleep is supported, but the specific causal role of IL‑10 is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes IL-10\'s anti‑inflammatory and neuroprotective roles and lists sleep disturbance among many post‑COVID symptoms, but it does not present empirical evidence or citations linking IL‑10 levels (or manipulation) to sleep quality or PSQI scores, nor a demonstrated causal direction from IL‑10 to reduced sleep disturbance."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43440-021-00333-0", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study measured and reports associations between PSQI sleep disturbance and proinflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-17, IL-23). It does not present data on IL-10 nor test effects of IL-10 on sleep, and being an observational cross‑sectional study it does not establish causal direction. Therefore the specific claim that IL-10 inversely causes changes in PSQI is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased IL-10, an anti-inflammatory cytokine, directly reduces inflammatory activity, which is known to improve sleep quality and decrease sleep disturbances. Conversely, decreased IL-10 can lead to increased inflammation and worsen sleep disturbance. The causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.2506856921320668, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8566036078795134, 'min_prob': 0.11810028171057317, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00106416199388906, 'prob_variance': 0.056787870756320975, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0722967231994218, 'avg_nll': 0.22369195461927854}

203. Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)-[POSITIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) causally inhibit proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by downregulating their production and activity. Increasing IL-10 directly reduces TNF-alpha and IL-6 levels, while decreasing IL-10 allows proinflammatory cytokines to rise. This inverse causal relationship is well-established in immunology.', 'session_id': '2bb1b40f-22fb-4ef4-855a-2ec03706796a', 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full', 'https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.abc8433', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0085253815505784', 'https://www.pneumon.org/Relationship-between-serum-TNF-IL-6-and-IL-n10-levels-and-disease-severity-and-changes,170181,0,2.html', 'https://www.kidney-international.org/article/S0085-2538(15)50578-4/fulltext'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents IL-10’s well-established anti-inflammatory effects (it inhibits macrophage/monocyte production of TNF-α, IL-6 and other proinflammatory mediators). It also describes contexts in which IL-10 has immunostimulatory effects — for example, exogenous/recombinant IL-10 or certain in vivo settings can promote CD8+ T cell and NK activation and have been associated with increased levels of some proinflammatory mediators (including reported increases in TNF-α in some studies). Thus the sources support that IL-10 can, under specific conditions, be associated with upregulation of certain proinflammatory responses, but they do not support the broad, unqualified claim that IL-10 generally enhances TNF-α and IL-6 to accelerate pathogen clearance; on the contrary, the review emphasizes that IL-10 most often suppresses those cytokines and that any proinflammatory effects are context-dependent."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0085253815505784", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains only an error message and no substantive content about IL-10, TNF-alpha, or IL-6, so the relationship is not supported or contradicted by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.pneumon.org/Relationship-between-serum-TNF-IL-6-and-IL-n10-levels-and-disease-severity-and-changes,170181,0,2.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited paper describes IL-10 as an anti‑inflammatory cytokine that inhibits synthesis/secretion of proinflammatory cytokines (including TNF) and reports no evidence that IL‑10 upregulates TNF‑α or IL‑6 in their patient data (IL‑10 did not increase after treatment, IL‑6 decreased, and TNF‑α/IL‑6 were not positively linked to IL‑10). The article therefore contradicts the claim that IL‑10 positively regulates TNF‑α and IL‑6."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'IL-10, as an anti-inflammatory cytokine, is known to enhance the activity of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 to ensure a robust immune response. By upregulating these proinflammatory mediators, IL-10 helps to accelerate the clearance of pathogens during infection. Therefore, anti-inflammatory processes mediated by IL-10 positively regulate proinflammatory processes.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'IL-10, as an anti-inflammatory cytokine, is known to enhance the activity of proinflammatory cytokines like TNF-alpha and IL-6 to ensure a robust immune response. By upregulating these proinflammatory mediators, IL-10 helps to accelerate the clearance of pathogens during i